window.LM_SEARCH = [["getting-started/introduction.html","Introduction","","Before drawing anything, it helps to know what Metal is, what it is not, and how its pieces fit together. This chapter is all concepts and no code. The next one opens Xcode."],["getting-started/introduction.html#what-metal-is","Introduction","What Metal is","Metal is Apple's low-level API for talking to the GPU. It covers two jobs: Rendering : turning triangles into pixels, which is what most of this site is about. Compute : running general-purpose parallel code on the GPU, which we reach in the Beyond section. It runs on macOS, iOS, iPadOS, tvOS and visionOS, and it is the only first-class graphics API on those platforms. OpenGL and OpenCL have been deprecated there since 2018. SceneKit, RealityKit, SpriteKit, Core Animation, and game engines like Unity and Unreal all sit on top of Metal when running on Apple hardware. Metal is low-level in the sense that it exposes how a GPU really works: you allocate buffers, describe pipelines, and record commands yourself. It is also noticeably friendlier than Vulkan or Direct3D 12. There is far less boilerplate, sensible defaults exist, and the debugging tools in Xcode are excellent."],["getting-started/introduction.html#the-objects-you-will-meet","Introduction","The objects you will meet","Almost everything in Metal is created from one object, the device , and nearly every type name starts with MTL . The cast is small: Object Role MTLDevice The GPU. Factory for everything else. MTLCommandQueue An ordered line of work waiting for the GPU. MTLCommandBuffer One batch of work, usually one frame. MTLRenderCommandEncoder Writes draw commands for one render pass into a command buffer. MTLRenderPassDescriptor Says which textures a pass draws into and how they start and end. MTLRenderPipelineState A compiled vertex + fragment shader pair plus fixed configuration. MTLBuffer A block of memory the GPU can read: vertices, indices, uniforms. MTLTexture An image the GPU can read from or draw into. MTLSamplerState How a texture is filtered and wrapped when read. MTLDepthStencilState How depth and stencil tests behave. MTLLibrary / MTLFunction Compiled shader code and one function inside it. A frame always has the same shape: Ask the command queue for a command buffer. Create a render command encoder for a render pass. Set state (pipeline, buffers, textures) and issue draw calls. End encoding, ask to present the result on screen, and commit the command buffer. Nothing reaches the GPU until you commit. Encoding is just writing a to-do list."],["getting-started/introduction.html#state-objects-instead-of-a-state-machine","Introduction","State objects instead of a state machine","Coming from OpenGL OpenGL is one big global state machine: you bind things to a context and flip switches with glEnable . Metal has no global state at all. Expensive configuration is baked ahead of time into immutable objects (pipeline states, depth-stencil states, sampler states), and cheap per-draw state is set on an encoder that only lives for one pass. When an encoder ends, its state is gone. This design is the main reason Metal is fast and predictable. Compiling shaders and validating configuration happen once when you create a state object, not unpredictably in the middle of a frame."],["getting-started/introduction.html#shaders","Introduction","Shaders","Shaders are small programs that run on the GPU. In Metal they are written in the Metal Shading Language (MSL), which is C++14 with some features removed and vector, matrix and texture types added. If you have written GLSL or HLSL, you can read MSL immediately. Xcode compiles every .metal file in your project at build time into a single default.metallib , so shader syntax errors show up as ordinary build errors."],["getting-started/introduction.html#apple-gpus","Introduction","Apple GPUs","Every current Apple device uses Apple silicon, whose GPUs are tile-based deferred renderers with unified memory shared by the CPU and GPU. You do not need to understand either yet, but they explain a few Metal features you will meet later: load and store actions, memoryless textures, and shared storage mode. Older Intel Macs with AMD or Intel GPUs also run Metal, with a few differences that are pointed out where they matter."],["getting-started/introduction.html#versions","Introduction","Versions","The Metal API has grown through numbered generations. Everything in this series uses the long-established core API ( MTLCommandQueue , MTLRenderCommandEncoder , and friends), which runs on all Metal-capable devices and is what almost all existing code, samples and answers online use. Apple has also introduced a newer set of interfaces under the name Metal 4, with types prefixed MTL4 , aimed at engines that need explicit control. The concepts carry over directly, so learn the core API first; the Metal 4 chapter shows what changes."],["getting-started/introduction.html#how-to-read-this-series","Introduction","How to read this series","Go in order. Every chapter assumes the code from the previous one. Type the code yourself instead of pasting it. Change numbers and break things on purpose, then read what Xcode tells you. The official Metal documentation and the Metal Shading Language Specification are the references to keep open in another tab."],["getting-started/project-setup.html","Project Setup","","Setting up a Metal project takes minutes. Metal ships with the system, so there is nothing to build before you can draw. Use the Swift / C++ switch at the top of the page to pick your language. The chapters, the shaders and the live demos are the same for both; only the host code changes."],["getting-started/project-setup.html#what-you-need","Project Setup","What you need","A Mac running a recent version of macOS. Xcode, free from the Mac App Store. C++ readers only need its command line tools. The examples target macOS because it is the quickest loop: press Run and a window appears. Everything except the few lines that host the view works unchanged on iOS, and the differences are shown below. Simulator or device? Metal works in the iOS Simulator, but with reduced features and performance. When you target iPhone or iPad, test on a real device."],["getting-started/project-setup.html#create-the-project","Project Setup","Create the project","In Xcode choose File → New → Project . Pick macOS → App . Name it LearnMetal , set Interface to SwiftUI and Language to Swift . Xcode also offers a Game template with Metal as the technology. It generates a working spinning cube, which is nice to look at but hides the parts we want to write ourselves, so we start from a plain app."],["getting-started/project-setup.html#metalkit-and-mtkview","Project Setup","MetalKit and MTKView","To draw with Metal you need a surface that the system can put on screen. The low-level way is a CAMetalLayer plus your own display link. MetalKit wraps both in a view called MTKView that: owns the layer and hands you a fresh drawable (a texture that will be shown on screen) each frame, creates depth and multisample textures for you on request, calls your code once per display refresh. You tell the view what to draw by giving it a delegate , an object that implements two methods of MTKViewDelegate . That delegate is our renderer, and it is where nearly all code in this series will live."],["getting-started/project-setup.html#host-the-view-in-swiftui","Project Setup","Host the view in SwiftUI","MTKView is an AppKit view (UIKit on iOS), so SwiftUI needs a small adapter. Add a new Swift file MetalView.swift : The coordinator exists for one reason: MTKView holds its delegate weakly, so something else must keep the renderer alive. On iOS the adapter is the same with different names: conform to UIViewRepresentable and implement makeUIView and updateUIView . Then show the view in ContentView.swift : The project will not compile yet because Renderer does not exist. That is the next chapter."],["getting-started/project-setup.html#where-shaders-go","Project Setup","Where shaders go","Shaders live in files with the .metal extension. Create one with File → New → File → Metal File and call it Shaders.metal . Xcode gives it this content: Leave it for now. Any .metal file that belongs to the app target is compiled during the build and bundled into the app automatically."],["getting-started/project-setup.html#turn-on-the-safety-nets","Project Setup","Turn on the safety nets","Open Product → Scheme → Edit Scheme → Run → Diagnostics (in some Xcode versions the Metal options are under Options ) and make sure Metal API Validation is enabled. Validation checks every Metal call and stops with a readable message when you do something illegal, instead of showing a black screen. It is on by default for debug runs, and you should leave it on. Shader Validation in the same place catches out-of-bounds reads and similar mistakes inside shaders; turn it on whenever a shader misbehaves."],["getting-started/project-setup.html#metal-cpp","Project Setup","metal-cpp","Metal is an Objective-C API. metal-cpp is Apple's official header-only C++ wrapper around it: every Metal class and method gets a C++ equivalent with no overhead. Download it from developer.apple.com/metal/cpp and unzip it into your project as a metal-cpp folder. The mapping from the Objective-C and Swift names is mechanical: Swift C++ with metal-cpp MTLDevice , MTLBuffer MTL::Device* , MTL::Buffer* device.makeCommandQueue() device->newCommandQueue() MTLRenderPipelineDescriptor() MTL::RenderPipelineDescriptor::alloc()->init() descriptor.colorAttachments[0].pixelFormat = .bgra8Unorm descriptor->colorAttachments()->object(0)->setPixelFormat(MTL::PixelFormatBGRA8Unorm) encoder.setVertexBuffer(buffer, offset: 0, index: 0) encoder->setVertexBuffer(buffer, 0, 0) \"vertex_main\" MTLSTR(\"vertex_main\") Three rules cover memory management, because there is no ARC in C++: Objects you get from a method that starts with new , or from alloc()->init() , are yours . Call release() when you are done. Everything else is autoreleased : valid until the enclosing NS::AutoreleasePool is released. Create a pool at the top of every frame. To keep an autoreleased object, call retain() . The implementation of the wrapper must be compiled exactly once. Create metal_impl.cpp :"],["getting-started/project-setup.html#a-window","Project Setup","A window","metal-cpp wraps Metal, Foundation and QuartzCore. It does not wrap AppKit or MetalKit, so there is no MTKView . We open a window with GLFW ( brew install glfw cmake ) and attach a CAMetalLayer to it. That one step has to touch AppKit, so it lives in a tiny Objective-C++ file, glfw_bridge.mm : main.cpp creates the window, the device and the renderer, and runs the loop: The layer plays the role that MTKView plays in Swift: it hands out a drawable , a texture that will be shown on screen, each time you ask for one. Presenting a drawable waits for the display, so this loop runs at the screen's refresh rate. Wherever a chapter mentions MTKView , the C++ equivalent is one of three things you do yourself: ask the layer for the next drawable, fill in a render pass descriptor, or create a depth texture. Each is shown the first time it is needed."],["getting-started/project-setup.html#building-with-cmake","Project Setup","Building with CMake","The project will not build yet because Renderer does not exist. That is the next chapter."],["getting-started/project-setup.html#where-shaders-go","Project Setup","Where shaders go","Shaders live in a file called Shaders.metal : Xcode compiles .metal files at build time. Outside Xcode the simplest route is to compile the source when the app starts, which also means a shader error is reported with its line number in the terminal: For a shipping app you would precompile with xcrun metal and xcrun metallib and load the resulting .metallib file."],["getting-started/project-setup.html#turn-on-the-safety-nets","Project Setup","Turn on the safety nets","Metal's validation layer checks every call and stops with a readable message when you do something illegal, instead of showing a black screen. Outside Xcode, enable it with environment variables: Leave MTL_DEBUG_LAYER on whenever you are developing. A starter project with all the files from this chapter and the next two is in the repository's examples-cpp folder. The Debugging chapter covers these tools in depth. You can read it at any time."],["getting-started/hello-view.html","Hello View","","Example project examples/Sources/01-HelloView , run with swift run HelloView . Download all examples or see how to run them . Time for the first Metal code: a renderer that clears the window to a color every frame. It draws no geometry, but it contains the complete skeleton of a frame, and every later chapter only adds to it. Reading this chapter in C++ There is no MTKView or delegate in metal-cpp. Your renderer keeps the CAMetalLayer from Project Setup , and main.cpp calls renderer.draw() once per loop. Where the text says view.currentDrawable , you call layer->nextDrawable() . Where it says view.currentRenderPassDescriptor , you fill in an MTL::RenderPassDescriptor yourself, which makes the load and store actions explained below visible in your own code."],["getting-started/hello-view.html#the-renderer","Hello View","The renderer","Create Renderer.swift : Run it. You should see a window filled with dark teal."],["getting-started/hello-view.html#the-device-and-the-queue","Hello View","The device and the queue","MTLCreateSystemDefaultDevice() returns the GPU the system recommends. On Apple silicon there is only one. Everything else is created from this device. The command queue is created once and kept for the lifetime of the app. It guarantees that the command buffers you commit are executed in order."],["getting-started/hello-view.html#configuring-the-view","Hello View","Configuring the view","view.device tells the view which GPU its textures live on. Without it nothing is drawn. colorPixelFormat is the format of the texture shown on screen. .bgra8Unorm means four 8-bit channels stored as blue, green, red, alpha, read by shaders as floats from 0 to 1. This is the view's default; we set it explicitly because we will change it in the Gamma Correction chapter. clearColor is the color the drawable is filled with at the start of each frame."],["getting-started/hello-view.html#anatomy-of-a-frame","Hello View","Anatomy of a frame","draw(in:) is called once per display refresh, typically 60 or 120 times per second. Four things happen in it. 1. Get a render pass descriptor. A render pass is a batch of drawing that targets one set of textures, its attachments . currentRenderPassDescriptor is a ready-made descriptor whose color attachment is this frame's drawable texture. For each attachment a descriptor also holds: a load action : what to do with the existing contents when the pass begins ( .clear , .load , or .dontCare ), a store action : what to do with the result when it ends ( .store , .dontCare , or a multisample resolve). The view's descriptor uses .clear with your clearColor , and .store . That is why we get a colored window without drawing anything: clearing is part of beginning the pass. Coming from OpenGL There is no glClear call. Clearing is a property of the render pass, and choosing load and store actions well matters for performance on tile-based GPUs, since each one can save or cost a full copy of the texture. 2. Create a command buffer and an encoder. The command buffer stores the encoded commands for this frame. The render command encoder translates your calls (set pipeline, set buffer, draw) into GPU commands for this one pass. 3. End encoding. You must call endEncoding() before creating another encoder or committing. Forgetting it is a validation error. 4. Present and commit. present(drawable) schedules the drawable to be shown as soon as the GPU finishes this command buffer. commit() hands the buffer to the queue. The call returns immediately; the GPU works asynchronously."],["getting-started/hello-view.html#drawables-are-scarce","Hello View","Drawables are scarce","currentDrawable comes from a small pool, usually three textures. If all are in use, the call blocks until one is free, and it can return nil . That is why the method starts with a guard and simply skips the frame when anything is missing. Hold on to a drawable for as short a time as possible and never store it."],["getting-started/hello-view.html#continuous-or-on-demand","Hello View","Continuous or on demand","By default MTKView redraws continuously at preferredFramesPerSecond . For an editor-style app that only changes on input you can set view.isPaused = true and view.enableSetNeedsDisplay = true , then request frames with setNeedsDisplay . A game or an animation keeps the default."],["getting-started/hello-view.html#exercises","Hello View","Exercises","Change the clear color every frame using CACurrentMediaTime() and sin so the window pulses. Print view.drawableSize in mtkView(_:drawableSizeWillChange:) and resize the window. Notice that the size is in pixels, not points: on a Retina display it is twice the window size."],["getting-started/hello-triangle.html","Hello Triangle","","Example project examples/Sources/02-HelloTriangle , run with swift run HelloTriangle . Download all examples or see how to run them . In Metal, as in every GPU API, everything you see is made of triangles. Drawing the first one requires three new pieces: vertex data in a buffer, a pair of shaders, and a render pipeline state that ties them together. It is the steepest step in the whole series. Once it works, the rest is variation."],["getting-started/hello-triangle.html#the-graphics-pipeline","Hello Triangle","The graphics pipeline","The pipeline is the sequence of steps that turns 3D coordinates into colored pixels. The vertex function runs once per vertex and outputs its final position. The GPU assembles vertices into triangles and rasterizes them: it works out which pixels each triangle covers and produces a fragment for each one. The fragment function runs once per fragment and outputs its color. Depth and stencil tests and blending decide how that color ends up in the texture."],["getting-started/hello-triangle.html#vertex-data","Hello Triangle","Vertex data","Metal expects final positions in normalized device coordinates (NDC): x and y run from -1 to 1 across the view, with x to the right and y up, and z runs from 0 to 1 into the screen. Anything outside is clipped away. A triangle in the middle of the view: The GPU cannot read a Swift array. The data has to be in an MTLBuffer . Add a property and create the buffer in init : The buffer is just bytes. Metal does not know yet that they are groups of three floats."],["getting-started/hello-triangle.html#the-shaders","Hello Triangle","The shaders","Open Shaders.metal and write the two functions: The double-bracket annotations are how MSL connects shader variables to the outside world: vertex and fragment mark the functions as pipeline entry points. [[stage_in]] means \"the per-vertex (or per-fragment) input assembled for this stage\". [[attribute(0)]] says this member comes from vertex attribute 0, which we describe below. [[position]] marks the output that holds the clip-space position. It is a float4 ; the fourth component w is 1 for now and becomes important with perspective. The fragment function returns an orange color as RGBA floats."],["getting-started/hello-triangle.html#the-vertex-descriptor","Hello Triangle","The vertex descriptor","An MTLVertexDescriptor explains the layout of the vertex buffer so that Metal can fill VertexIn : Attribute 0 is three floats, starting at byte 0 of each vertex, read from the buffer bound at index 0. Each vertex is 12 bytes apart."],["getting-started/hello-triangle.html#the-render-pipeline-state","Hello Triangle","The render pipeline state","The pipeline state combines the shader functions, the vertex layout, and the pixel format of the texture being drawn into: makeDefaultLibrary() loads the compiled shaders from the app bundle. Creating a pipeline state compiles the shaders for the GPU and is slow, so always do it at load time and never per frame. The pixel format must match the view's colorPixelFormat ; a mismatch is the most common first validation error. Coming from OpenGL A pipeline state is roughly a linked shader program, plus the VAO's attribute layout, plus blend state, all frozen into one object. There is no VAO or VBO binding dance."],["getting-started/hello-triangle.html#draw","Hello Triangle","Draw","In draw(in:) , between creating the encoder and ending it: index: 0 is the buffer slot that the vertex descriptor's bufferIndex refers to. Run the app and you should see an orange triangle."],["getting-started/hello-triangle.html#indexed-drawing","Hello Triangle","Indexed drawing","A rectangle is two triangles that share two vertices. Instead of duplicating them, store each vertex once and describe the triangles with indices: To see the triangles themselves, call encoder.setTriangleFillMode(.lines) before drawing."],["getting-started/hello-triangle.html#exercises","Hello Triangle","Exercises","Draw two triangles next to each other by adding three more vertices and changing vertexCount . Create a second pipeline state with a fragment function that returns yellow, and draw the second triangle with it. Move a vertex outside the -1 to 1 range and watch it get clipped."],["getting-started/shaders.html","Shaders","","Example project examples/Sources/03-Shaders , run with swift run Shaders . Download all examples or see how to run them . Shaders are where graphics programming happens. This chapter looks at the Metal Shading Language more closely, and shows the two ways data reaches a shader: per-vertex attributes that are interpolated across a triangle, and uniforms that are the same for a whole draw call."],["getting-started/shaders.html#the-language-in-brief","Shaders","The language in brief","MSL is based on C++14. The types you will use all the time: Type Meaning float , int , uint , bool Scalars. half is a 16-bit float, often faster on mobile GPUs. float2 , float3 , float4 Vectors. Also int2 , uint3 , half4 , and so on. float3x3 , float4x4 Matrices, stored column by column. texture2d<float> , texturecube<float> , depth2d<float> Textures. sampler Filtering and addressing settings for reading a texture. Vectors support swizzling : v.xyz , v.rg , v.zyx , color.rgb * color.a . The standard library has the functions you expect: dot , cross , normalize , length , reflect , mix , clamp , saturate , pow , smoothstep , max , min . Things MSL does not allow: recursion, lambdas, virtual functions, exceptions, new , and the C++ standard library."],["getting-started/shaders.html#passing-data-between-stages","Shaders","Passing data between stages","Whatever the vertex function returns is interpolated across the triangle and handed to the fragment function. Add a color to the output struct: Each vertex computes a color from its own position, and every fragment in between receives a blend of the three. This interpolation is automatic for every member of the struct."],["getting-started/shaders.html#more-vertex-attributes","Shaders","More vertex attributes","Colors can also come from the vertex buffer. Interleave them with the positions: Describe the second attribute and the larger stride: And receive it in the shader: The result is the classic triangle with a red, a green and a blue corner blending into each other."],["getting-started/shaders.html#uniforms","Shaders","Uniforms","A uniform is data that stays constant during a draw call: a time value, a transformation matrix, a light position. In Metal a uniform is simply a struct in a buffer that the shader receives by reference. Declare the struct in the shader and add it as a parameter: constant is the address space for read-only buffer data. [[buffer(0)]] is slot 0 of the fragment buffer table. Vertex and fragment functions have separate tables, so vertex buffer 0 and fragment buffer 0 are unrelated. Declare a matching struct in Swift: For small data, the simplest way to send it is setFragmentBytes , which copies the bytes into a temporary buffer managed by Metal: Store startTime = CACurrentMediaTime() in init ; a Float cannot hold the raw value precisely enough. There is a matching setVertexBytes for the vertex stage. For data larger than 4 KB, or data shared by many draws, create an MTLBuffer yourself and bind it with setFragmentBuffer ."],["getting-started/shaders.html#keeping-swift-and-msl-layouts-in-sync","Shaders","Keeping Swift and MSL layouts in sync","The Swift struct and the MSL struct must have identical memory layouts. Nothing checks this for you, and a mismatch produces garbage values, not an error. Three rules prevent nearly all problems: Use SIMD types in Swift for vectors and simd_float4x4 ( float4x4 ) for matrices. SIMD4<Float> matches float4 , SIMD2<Float> matches float2 . SIMD3<Float> and MSL float3 both occupy 16 bytes , not 12. They match each other, but they do not match three loose floats. When you need a tightly packed 12-byte vector in a buffer, MSL has packed_float3 . Always use MemoryLayout<T>.stride , not .size , for buffer lengths. In our struct, time is 4 bytes followed by 12 bytes of padding so that tint starts at offset 16, in both languages. One definition for both languages Larger projects put shared structs in a C header that is included by the .metal file and exposed to Swift through a bridging header. Then there is only one definition and the layouts cannot drift. This series repeats the structs on both sides to keep each chapter readable on its own."],["getting-started/shaders.html#exercises","Shaders","Exercises","Flip the triangle upside down in the vertex function only. Add a float2 offset uniform for the vertex stage and move the triangle in a circle over time. Output the position as the color ( out.color = out.position ). Why is the bottom-left corner black?"],["getting-started/textures.html","Textures","","Example project examples/Sources/04-Textures , run with swift run Textures . Download all examples or see how to run them . Giving every vertex a color cannot produce a brick wall or a wooden crate. For detail we wrap images around geometry. An image used this way is a texture , and reading a color from it is called sampling ."],["getting-started/textures.html#texture-coordinates","Textures","Texture coordinates","Each vertex gets a 2D texture coordinate (often called UV) that says which point of the image belongs to it. Coordinates run from 0 to 1 on both axes, and they are interpolated across the triangle like any other vertex output. Metal's origin is at the top left In Metal, texture coordinate (0, 0) is the top-left corner of the image and v increases downward. OpenGL puts (0, 0) at the bottom left. Texture coordinates copied from OpenGL tutorials or exported by tools that follow the OpenGL convention will show images upside down unless you flip v ( v = 1 - v ) or flip the image when loading. A rectangle with positions and texture coordinates:"],["getting-started/textures.html#loading-an-image","Textures","Loading an image","MetalKit's MTKTextureLoader decodes PNG, JPEG, HEIC, TIFF and other common formats and uploads the result to a new MTLTexture . Drag an image such as container.jpg into the project, then: .SRGB: false tells the loader to treat pixel values as plain numbers. We will revisit that choice in Gamma Correction . If you ever need the OpenGL orientation, add .origin: MTKTextureLoader.Origin.bottomLeft , which flips the image vertically during loading. Images in an asset catalog can be loaded by name with loader.newTexture(name:scaleFactor:bundle:options:) . Loading images in C++ MTKTextureLoader belongs to MetalKit, which metal-cpp does not wrap. The C++ version decodes the file with the single-header library stb_image and uploads the pixels with replaceRegion . stb_image returns the top row first, which matches Metal's top-left origin. To get the OpenGL orientation, call stbi_set_flip_vertically_on_load(true) before loading."],["getting-started/textures.html#samplers","Textures","Samplers","A texture is only pixels. How they are read is decided by a separate object, the sampler state : Address modes decide what happens outside the 0 to 1 range: Mode Effect .clampToEdge The edge pixels stretch outward. This is the default. .repeat The image tiles. .mirrorRepeat The image tiles, mirrored each time. .clampToBorderColor Outside is a fixed border color (macOS and newer iOS devices). .clampToZero Outside is transparent black. Filtering decides what happens when a texture pixel (a texel ) does not map one to one onto a screen pixel. .nearest picks the closest texel and looks blocky when magnified. .linear blends the four nearest texels and looks smooth. Mipmaps are a chain of pre-shrunk copies of the texture, each half the size of the previous one. When a surface is far away, sampling a small copy avoids shimmering and is faster. mipFilter = .linear blends between the two closest levels, which together with linear min filtering is known as trilinear filtering. Setting samplerDescriptor.maxAnisotropy = 8 further sharpens surfaces seen at a steep angle. Coming from OpenGL Wrap and filter settings are not properties of the texture. One texture can be read through several samplers, and one sampler can serve every texture."],["getting-started/textures.html#sampling-in-the-shader","Textures","Sampling in the shader","Textures and samplers have their own index tables, selected with [[texture(n)]] and [[sampler(n)]] . Bind them before drawing: Run it and the rectangle shows your image."],["getting-started/textures.html#two-textures-at-once","Textures","Two textures at once","A fragment function can read many textures. Load a second image, bind it at index 1, and blend: mix(a, b, t) returns a * (1 - t) + b * t , so the result is 80% of the first texture and 20% of the second."],["getting-started/textures.html#exercises","Textures","Exercises","Multiply the uv by 2 in the fragment function and try each address mode. Switch the filters to .nearest and display only the center of the texture (uv from 0.45 to 0.55) to see individual texels. Add a uniform that controls the mix factor and animate it."],["getting-started/transformations.html","Transformations","","Example project examples/Sources/05-Transformations , run with swift run Transformations . Download all examples or see how to run them . So far objects sit wherever their vertices put them. To move, rotate and scale them without rewriting vertex buffers every frame, we multiply each vertex by a matrix . This chapter is a compact tour of the math, followed by a small Swift helper file used for the rest of the series."],["getting-started/transformations.html#vectors","Transformations","Vectors","A vector is a direction with a length. In graphics we also use vectors for positions (a direction from the origin) and colors. The operations that matter: Addition and subtraction work per component. b - a is the vector pointing from a to b . Length : length(v) is the square root of x² + y² + z² . A vector of length 1 is a unit vector , and normalize(v) scales any vector to length 1. Dot product : dot(a, b) = a.x*b.x + a.y*b.y + a.z*b.z . For unit vectors it equals the cosine of the angle between them: 1 when parallel, 0 when perpendicular, -1 when opposite. All of lighting is built on this. Cross product : cross(a, b) is a vector perpendicular to both inputs. Swift's simd module provides all of these, with the same names as in MSL."],["getting-started/transformations.html#matrices","Transformations","Matrices","A 4×4 matrix can express any combination of scaling, rotation and translation. Multiplying a matrix by a vector applies the transformation. Scaling puts the factors on the diagonal. Translation puts the offset in the last column, which is why we need four dimensions: a 3×3 matrix cannot move a point. The fourth component of a vector, w , switches translation on and off. Positions use w = 1 and are moved; directions use w = 0 and are not. Rotation by an angle around an axis fills the upper-left 3×3 block with sines and cosines. You rarely write these by hand."],["getting-started/transformations.html#order-matters","Transformations","Order matters","Matrix multiplication is not commutative. With column vectors, the convention in both Metal and simd , the matrix closest to the vector applies first : Scale, then rotate, then translate is the order you almost always want. Translating first and rotating afterward swings the object around the origin like a stone on a string."],["getting-started/transformations.html#a-matrix-helper","Transformations","A matrix helper","simd gives us the types but few constructors. Add Math.swift : Angles are in radians everywhere; 45.radians converts from degrees. Matrices are stored in column-major order: columns.3 is the fourth column, where translation lives. MSL's float4x4 uses the same layout, so a Swift matrix can be copied into a uniform buffer unchanged. The rotation is built from a quaternion , a four-number representation of a rotation that is free of the gimbal lock problems that plague chained axis rotations. You do not need the theory to use it."],["getting-started/transformations.html#transforming-the-rectangle","Transformations","Transforming the rectangle","Add a matrix to the uniforms and multiply in the vertex function: The uniforms use vertex buffer slot 1 because slot 0 holds the vertex data. From here on that is our convention: slot 0 for vertices, slot 1 for uniforms. The rectangle is now half size, in the lower-right corner, spinning around its own center. If the window is not square it looks stretched while rotating. That is expected, and the projection matrix in the next chapter fixes it."],["getting-started/transformations.html#exercises","Transformations","Exercises","Swap the translation and rotation and predict the result before running. Draw the rectangle a second time in the top-left corner with a scale that follows sin(time) . You only need a second setVertexBytes and a second draw call."],["getting-started/coordinate-systems.html","Coordinate Systems","","Example project examples/Sources/06-CoordinateSystems , run with swift run CoordinateSystems . Download all examples or see how to run them . A vertex travels through several coordinate systems on its way to the screen. Splitting the journey into steps makes each one simple, and three matrices carry the vertex from one step to the next. At the end of this chapter we have real 3D: a perspective view of textured cubes with correct depth."],["getting-started/coordinate-systems.html#the-spaces","Coordinate Systems","The spaces","Local space (object space): coordinates relative to the object's own origin, as stored in the vertex buffer. World space : where the object sits in the scene. The model matrix takes local to world. View space (camera space): the world as seen from the camera, with the camera at the origin. The view matrix takes world to view. Clip space : the output of the vertex function. The projection matrix takes view to clip. Normalized device coordinates : the GPU divides clip-space x, y, z by w (the perspective divide ). Viewport coordinates : NDC mapped to pixels. You write steps 2 to 4 as one line in the shader:"],["getting-started/coordinate-systems.html#metals-conventions","Coordinate Systems","Metal's conventions","NDC x and y run from -1 to 1 with y up . NDC z runs from 0 to 1 . Pixel coordinates and texture coordinates start at the top left with y down. Coming from OpenGL OpenGL's NDC z runs from -1 to 1. A projection matrix copied from an OpenGL tutorial puts half of your depth range in front of the near plane, so geometry near the camera disappears. Always use a projection matrix built for a 0 to 1 depth range, like the one below. Metal does not care whether your world is left-handed or right-handed; that is decided entirely by your matrices. This series uses a right-handed world, the same as most tutorials and modeling tools: +x right, +y up, and the camera looks down -z ."],["getting-started/coordinate-systems.html#perspective-projection","Coordinate Systems","Perspective projection","A perspective projection makes distant things smaller. It is defined by a vertical field of view, the aspect ratio of the view, and a near and far plane; together they bound a truncated pyramid called the frustum . Add to Math.swift : The -1 copies the negated view-space z into w. After the perspective divide, everything is divided by its distance from the camera, which is exactly what perspective is. Keep the near plane as far out as you can (0.1 is typical). Depth precision is concentrated close to the near plane, and a tiny value such as 0.0001 wastes almost all of it. An orthographic projection, which has no perspective, appears in the Shadow Mapping chapter."],["getting-started/coordinate-systems.html#model-view-projection","Coordinate Systems","Model, view, projection","The view matrix moves the whole world 3 units away along -z. Moving the world backward and moving the camera forward are the same thing."],["getting-started/coordinate-systems.html#a-cube","Coordinate Systems","A cube","A flat rectangle makes a poor 3D demo. From now on we use a proper vertex struct and generate a cube in code. Each vertex has a position, a normal (the direction the surface faces, needed for lighting soon), and a texture coordinate: That is 36 vertices for a cube one unit wide, wound counter-clockwise when seen from outside. Build the vertex descriptor from the struct itself so offsets can never be wrong: All three attributes use bufferIndex 0, the default. Because SIMD3<Float> occupies 16 bytes, the offsets are 0, 16 and 32 and the stride is 48. In the shader: Draw with vertexCount: 36 ."],["getting-started/coordinate-systems.html#the-depth-buffer","Coordinate Systems","The depth buffer","Run it and the cube looks inside out: faces drawn later cover faces drawn earlier, whichever is closer. The fix is a depth buffer , a texture that stores the depth of the closest fragment drawn so far at each pixel. A new fragment is kept only if it is closer. Three steps enable it. The depth texture is cleared to 1 (the far plane) at the start of each pass, and .less keeps fragments with a smaller depth than what is stored."],["getting-started/coordinate-systems.html#more-cubes","Coordinate Systems","More cubes","Drawing the same cube in ten places needs only ten model matrices:"],["getting-started/coordinate-systems.html#exercises","Coordinate Systems","Exercises","Change the field of view and the aspect ratio by hand and describe what each does. Make every third cube rotate over time. Set depthCompareFunction to .always and to .greater and explain what you see."],["getting-started/camera.html","Camera","","Example project examples/Sources/07-Camera , run with swift run Camera . Download all examples or see how to run them . A fixed view matrix shows a scene from one spot. A camera you can fly through the scene is a view matrix that is rebuilt every frame from a position and a direction, both driven by input."],["getting-started/camera.html#the-look-at-matrix","Camera","The look-at matrix","A camera is defined by where it is, what it looks at, and which way is up. From those three we derive three perpendicular axes and build a matrix that moves the world into the camera's frame. Add to Math.swift : The rotation part lines the world up with the camera's axes, and the last column moves the camera to the origin. Try it by orbiting the scene:"],["getting-started/camera.html#a-fly-camera","Camera","A fly camera","For free movement, store a position and two angles. Yaw turns left and right, pitch looks up and down. front turns the two angles into a direction with basic trigonometry. Pitch is clamped just short of 90 degrees because looking straight up makes front parallel to the up vector, and the cross product in lookAt collapses."],["getting-started/camera.html#delta-time","Camera","Delta time","Frames do not take equal time. Movement must be scaled by the time since the last frame, the delta time , or the camera speed would depend on the frame rate:"],["getting-started/camera.html#input","Camera","Input","AppKit and UIKit deliver input as events, which is awkward for a game loop that wants to ask \"is W down right now?\". The GameController framework lets you poll the keyboard and mouse on both macOS and iPadOS: Mouse movement arrives through a handler that reports deltas. Register it once, for example in the renderer's init : deltaY is positive when the mouse moves up, so pitch increases and the camera looks up. On macOS the window must be key for keyboard polling to work; click it once after launch. The same framework reads game controllers through GCController , which is the natural choice on iPhone. Input in C++ The C++ version uses GLFW, which the window already comes from, in place of the GameController framework. glfwGetKey polls the keyboard, and cursor and scroll callbacks deliver mouse movement. GLFW reports the cursor position with y growing downward, so the callback subtracts in the other order to make pitch increase when the mouse moves up."],["getting-started/camera.html#putting-it-together","Camera","Putting it together","Zoom is nothing more than narrowing the field of view. You can now fly around the ten cubes from the previous chapter."],["getting-started/camera.html#exercises","Camera","Exercises","Turn the fly camera into a first-person camera that cannot leave the ground plane (keep position.y fixed). Add Q and E to move straight up and down. Hold Shift to triple the movement speed."],["getting-started/review.html","Review","","That was the hard part. You can open a view, push vertices through a pipeline, texture them, move them with matrices, and fly a camera through the result. Everything from here on builds on these pieces."],["getting-started/review.html#the-frame-one-more-time","Review","The frame, one more time","If you can explain every line of this function, you are ready for lighting."],["getting-started/review.html#glossary","Review","Glossary","Device ( MTLDevice ): the GPU, and the factory for all other Metal objects. Command queue / command buffer : the queue orders work; a buffer holds the encoded commands of one frame. Render pass : a sequence of draws into one set of attachment textures, described by an MTLRenderPassDescriptor with load and store actions. Render command encoder : records state changes and draw calls for one pass. Drawable : the texture that will be presented on screen this frame. Vertex function : runs per vertex, outputs a clip-space position. Fragment function : runs per covered pixel, outputs a color. Render pipeline state : compiled shaders plus vertex layout and attachment formats. Expensive to create, cheap to use. Vertex descriptor : maps bytes in vertex buffers to shader attributes. Buffer ( MTLBuffer ): raw memory visible to the GPU. Uniforms : per-draw constants passed to shaders in a buffer. Texture / sampler state : image data, and the rules for reading it. Mipmaps : pre-shrunk copies of a texture for distant surfaces. Model / view / projection matrices : local to world, world to camera, camera to clip space. NDC : x and y from -1 to 1, z from 0 to 1 in Metal. Depth buffer : per-pixel record of the nearest surface, used to hide what is behind. Delta time : seconds since the previous frame."],["getting-started/review.html#conventions-used-from-here-on","Review","Conventions used from here on","Thing Convention World Right-handed, +y up, camera looks down -z Angles Radians in matrices, degrees in the camera class Vertex buffer slot 0 Vertex data Vertex buffer slot 1 Per-draw uniforms Fragment buffer slot 1 Fragment uniforms (lights, materials) Triangle winding Counter-clockwise is the front"],["getting-started/review.html#common-first-mistakes","Review","Common first mistakes","Black or empty view : view.device not set, or the delegate was deallocated because nothing holds the renderer. Pipeline creation fails : pixel formats in the pipeline descriptor do not match the view, or a function name is misspelled. Garbage uniforms : Swift and MSL struct layouts differ, or .size was used where .stride was needed. Upside-down textures : OpenGL-style texture coordinates. Geometry vanishing close to the camera : an OpenGL projection matrix with a -1 to 1 depth range. Nothing drawn and no error : the model is behind the camera, outside the near and far planes, or the depth compare function rejects everything. Read validation messages carefully. Metal's are precise and nearly always name the exact property that is wrong."],["lighting/colors.html","Colors","","Example project examples/Sources/08-BasicLighting , run with swift run BasicLighting . Download all examples or see how to run them . Lighting is the art of computing what color a surface should appear. Before any real lighting math, we need a model of color and a scene to light."],["lighting/colors.html#color-as-numbers","Colors","Color as numbers","A color is three numbers: how much red, green and blue. In shaders each channel is a float where 0 is none and 1 is full intensity. The color we see on an object is the part of the incoming light that the object reflects . A coral-colored object lit by white light reflects all of the red, half of the green and about a third of the blue, and absorbs the rest. In code, reflection is a component-wise multiplication: With a green light the same object looks very different: There is no red or blue light to reflect, so only a dark green remains. That single multiplication is the core of every lighting model in this series."],["lighting/colors.html#a-scene-to-light","Colors","A scene to light","We need two things: an object, and something that shows where the light is. Both are cubes, drawn with two different pipeline states. The object's fragment function multiplies the two colors: The lamp cube should not be affected by lighting at all, so it gets its own trivial fragment function: Create a second pipeline state that uses the same vertex function and fragment_light : A descriptor is only a recipe. You can change one property and create another state from it."],["lighting/colors.html#drawing-both","Colors","Drawing both","Set the view's clear color to something dark such as (0.1, 0.1, 0.1) so the lamp stands out. You now have a flat coral cube and a small white cube. The coral one has no shading yet, so its faces are indistinguishable. The next chapter fixes that."],["lighting/colors.html#exercises","Colors","Exercises","Change the light color and predict the object's color before running. Animate the light color with sin(time) on each channel at different speeds."],["lighting/basic-lighting.html","Basic Lighting","","Example project examples/Sources/08-BasicLighting , run with swift run BasicLighting . Download all examples or see how to run them . Real light is hopelessly complicated, so real-time graphics uses approximations. The classic one is the Phong lighting model , which builds the final color from three simple parts: Ambient : a small constant amount of light everywhere, standing in for light that has bounced around the room. Diffuse : the surface is brighter the more directly it faces the light. Specular : the shiny highlight where the light reflects toward the viewer."],["lighting/basic-lighting.html#ambient","Basic Lighting","Ambient","On its own this just makes the cube dark, but it prevents unlit sides from being pure black."],["lighting/basic-lighting.html#diffuse","Basic Lighting","Diffuse","Diffuse lighting needs two directions at each fragment: the surface normal , and the direction from the fragment to the light. The cosine of the angle between them, given by the dot product of the two unit vectors, is the brightness: 1 when the light hits head on, 0 when it grazes the surface. Our cube already carries normals. The fragment function needs the normal and the fragment's position in world space , so the vertex function passes both: The normal matrix Normals are directions, so translation must not affect them, and non-uniform scaling distorts them if you use the model matrix directly. The correct matrix for normals is the inverse transpose of the upper-left 3×3 of the model matrix. Compute it on the CPU: If the model matrix only rotates and scales uniformly, the upper-left 3×3 itself works, but the inverse transpose is always right. The diffuse term Two details matter. Interpolated normals are no longer unit length, so normalize again in the fragment function. And max(..., 0.0) stops surfaces facing away from the light from getting negative light."],["lighting/basic-lighting.html#specular","Basic Lighting","Specular","A highlight appears where the light bounces off the surface straight into the eye. Reflect the light direction around the normal and compare the result with the direction to the viewer: reflect expects a vector pointing toward the surface, hence the minus sign. The exponent, 32 here, is the shininess : higher values give a smaller, sharper highlight. On the Swift side, fill in the new fields each frame: The cube now has clearly distinct faces and a highlight that slides across them as you move. World space or view space? We light in world space because it is the easiest to reason about. Lighting in view space works equally well, and there the viewer is always at the origin. Whichever you choose, every vector in a calculation must be in the same space."],["lighting/basic-lighting.html#exercises","Basic Lighting","Exercises","Move the light in a circle around the cube over time. Try shininess values of 2, 8, 64 and 256. Do the whole lighting calculation in the vertex function and pass the resulting color to the fragment function. This is Gouraud shading . Why does the highlight look worse?"],["lighting/materials.html","Materials","","Example project examples/Sources/08-BasicLighting , run with swift run BasicLighting . Download all examples or see how to run them . Steel, wood and plastic react to the same light in different ways. To describe that, we stop using a single object color and give each surface a material : separate colors for the three Phong terms, plus a shininess."],["lighting/materials.html#material-and-light-structs","Materials","Material and light structs","MSL structs can nest, which keeps the uniforms readable: ambient is the color the surface shows under ambient light, usually the same as its diffuse color. diffuse is the surface's main color. specular is the color of its highlight. shininess controls how tight the highlight is. The light gets its own intensities per term as well. Ambient light should be weak, diffuse light is the light's actual color, and specular is usually full white."],["lighting/materials.html#the-shader","Materials","The shader",""],["lighting/materials.html#the-swift-side","Materials","The Swift side","Mirror the structs exactly, in the same member order: Check the layout Material ends with a lone Float after three 16-byte vectors, so its size is 52 bytes and its stride is 64. Here the next member, light , is 16-byte aligned and starts at offset 64 in both languages. There is one trap with nested structs: Swift is allowed to place a small following member, such as a Float , inside the unused tail of the previous struct (at offset 52), while MSL never does. To stay safe, follow a nested struct with a 16-byte-aligned member, or end every nested struct on a 16-byte boundary. When values look wrong, print MemoryLayout<FragmentUniforms>.offset(of: \\.viewPosition) and compare offsets by hand."],["lighting/materials.html#playing-with-light-colors","Materials","Playing with light colors","Because the light has separate terms, changing the light changes the whole mood:"],["lighting/materials.html#real-materials","Materials","Real materials","Tables of measured Phong values for real substances have circulated for decades. A few to try, with all light terms set to white: Material Ambient Diffuse Specular Shininess Emerald 0.02, 0.17, 0.02 0.08, 0.61, 0.08 0.63, 0.73, 0.63 77 Gold 0.25, 0.20, 0.07 0.75, 0.61, 0.23 0.63, 0.56, 0.37 51 Cyan plastic 0.0, 0.1, 0.06 0.0, 0.51, 0.51 0.50, 0.50, 0.50 32 Red rubber 0.05, 0.0, 0.0 0.5, 0.4, 0.4 0.7, 0.04, 0.04 10 Phong materials are a rough approximation. The PBR section replaces them with a model based on physics."],["lighting/materials.html#exercises","Materials","Exercises","Reproduce each material in the table. Why does a metal look more convincing when its specular color matches its diffuse color?"],["lighting/lighting-maps.html","Lighting Maps","","Example project examples/Sources/09-LightingMaps , run with swift run LightingMaps . Download all examples or see how to run them . A material with one color per object is still too coarse. A wooden crate with a steel frame is two materials on one surface: the wood is dull, the steel is shiny. The answer is to store material properties in textures, so they can vary per pixel. Such textures are called lighting maps ."],["lighting/lighting-maps.html#diffuse-maps","Lighting Maps","Diffuse maps","A diffuse map is what earlier chapters simply called a texture: an image holding the surface's color at every point. Replace the material's diffuse color with a texture lookup, and use the same value for ambient: Remember to shrink the Swift Material struct to match."],["lighting/lighting-maps.html#specular-maps","Lighting Maps","Specular maps","The specular map is a second image, usually grayscale, that says how strongly each point reflects highlights. For the crate it is black over the wood and bright over the steel frame, with scratches and wear painted in. Black means no highlight; white means full. Load both images and bind them to the two texture slots: Now only the metal border of the crate shines, and the highlight breaks up over its scratches."],["lighting/lighting-maps.html#one-sampler-many-textures","Lighting Maps","One sampler, many textures","Both maps are read through the same sampler. A sampler describes how to read, and that is nearly always the same for all maps of a material, so a single sampler state is typical."],["lighting/lighting-maps.html#single-channel-textures","Lighting Maps","Single-channel textures","A grayscale specular map wastes three channels when loaded as RGBA. Metal has one-channel formats such as .r8Unorm , and a PNG saved as grayscale is loaded that way by MTKTextureLoader . In the shader you then read only .r : Real asset pipelines go further and pack several grayscale maps into the channels of one texture, for example roughness in green and metalness in blue. You will see that in the PBR section."],["lighting/lighting-maps.html#exercises","Lighting Maps","Exercises","Invert the specular map in the shader so the wood shines and the steel does not. Use a colored specular map and see how it tints the highlights. Add an emission map : a third texture whose color is added to the result regardless of lighting, making parts of the object appear to glow."],["lighting/light-casters.html","Light Casters","","Example project examples/Sources/10-MultipleLights , run with swift run MultipleLights . Download all examples or see how to run them . Until now the light has been a single point shining equally in all directions forever. Real scenes have three common kinds of light source: directional lights, point lights and spotlights."],["lighting/light-casters.html#directional-light","Light Casters","Directional light","A light that is extremely far away, like the sun, has rays that are effectively parallel. Its position is irrelevant; only its direction matters, and it is the same for every fragment. The lighting math wants a vector from the fragment toward the light, so negate it: Everything else stays the same. With direction = (-0.2, -1.0, -0.3) all ten cubes are lit from above at the same angle."],["lighting/light-casters.html#point-light","Light Casters","Point light","A point light has a position and shines in all directions, but it fades with distance. That fading is called attenuation . A formula that looks good and is cheap: where d is the distance from the light. The constant term is normally 1 so the result never exceeds 1. The linear and quadratic terms set how fast the light dies off. Range Constant Linear Quadratic 13 1.0 0.35 0.44 50 1.0 0.09 0.032 100 1.0 0.045 0.0075 325 1.0 0.014 0.0007"],["lighting/light-casters.html#spotlight","Light Casters","Spotlight","A spotlight is a point light that only shines inside a cone. It needs a position, a direction, and a cutoff angle. For each fragment, compare the angle between the spotlight's direction and the direction from the light to the fragment against the cutoff. Comparing angles would require an expensive inverse cosine, so we compare cosines instead. Pass the cosine of the cutoff from Swift: A hard cutoff gives an ugly sharp circle. Two cones, an inner and a slightly larger outer one, give a soft edge: full brightness inside the inner cone, none outside the outer cone, and a smooth blend between. saturate(x) clamps to the 0 to 1 range. Ambient is left alone so that the scene outside the cone is not pitch black. Remember that a larger cosine means a smaller angle, which is why the comparison looks reversed."],["lighting/light-casters.html#a-flashlight","Light Casters","A flashlight","A flashlight is a spotlight attached to the camera: Add attenuation as well and walk around the cubes in the dark."],["lighting/light-casters.html#exercises","Light Casters","Exercises","Give the directional light a warm color and a low angle for a sunset. Experiment with the attenuation terms until a point light reaches exactly the nearest cubes. Project a texture from the spotlight, like a slide projector: use the fragment's position within the cone as a texture coordinate."],["lighting/multiple-lights.html","Multiple Lights","","Example project examples/Sources/10-MultipleLights , run with swift run MultipleLights . Download all examples or see how to run them . A scene rarely has one light. This chapter combines everything from the lighting section: one directional light, four point lights and a flashlight, all in a single fragment function. The trick is organization: one function per light type, and their results added together."],["lighting/multiple-lights.html#shader-functions","Multiple Lights","Shader functions","MSL is C++, so ordinary helper functions work. Anything not marked vertex , fragment or kernel is a plain function you can call. Mark helpers static so they stay private to the file. calcSpotLight is the point light function with the cone intensity from the previous chapter multiplied into the diffuse and specular terms. Collecting the surface properties in a Surface struct keeps the parameter lists short and means textures are sampled once, not once per light."],["lighting/multiple-lights.html#arrays-in-uniforms","Multiple Lights","Arrays in uniforms","A fixed-size array inside the uniform struct holds the point lights:"],["lighting/multiple-lights.html#the-swift-side","Multiple Lights","The Swift side","Swift has no fixed-size C arrays in structs, but a tuple of identical elements has the same memory layout: That gets clumsy for larger counts. The scalable alternative is to put the lights in their own MTLBuffer and pass a pointer plus a count: A buffer bound to a shader is just memory, and a pointer parameter lets the shader index it as an array of any length. This is far more flexible than uniform arrays in older APIs, and it is the form we use from now on."],["lighting/multiple-lights.html#setting-the-scene","Multiple Lights","Setting the scene","Four point lights at different positions, each drawn as a small lamp cube with the unlit pipeline from the Colors chapter:"],["lighting/multiple-lights.html#the-cost-of-many-lights","Multiple Lights","The cost of many lights","Every fragment evaluates every light, so the cost is fragments × lights. A handful is fine. Hundreds are not, and solving that is the purpose of Deferred Shading later on."],["lighting/multiple-lights.html#exercises","Multiple Lights","Exercises","Recreate different atmospheres only by changing light colors and the clear color: a desert at noon, a dim factory, a horror corridor lit by the flashlight alone. Move the lights over time by rewriting the buffer contents each frame through lightBuffer.contents() ."],["lighting/review.html","Review","","You can now light a scene. The model is simple, and it is the foundation that every later technique refines."],["lighting/review.html#glossary","Review","Glossary","Color vector : red, green and blue intensities. Reflection is a component-wise multiplication of light color and surface color. Phong lighting model : ambient + diffuse + specular. Ambient : a constant term that fakes indirect light. Diffuse : brightness from the angle between the normal and the direction to the light: max(dot(N, L), 0) . Specular : a highlight from the angle between the reflected light and the direction to the viewer, raised to the shininess exponent. Normal : a unit vector perpendicular to the surface. Normal matrix : the inverse transpose of the model matrix's upper-left 3×3, used to transform normals correctly. Material : the set of values describing how a surface reacts to light. Diffuse map / specular map : textures that supply the material's color and shininess per pixel. Directional light : parallel rays with a direction and no position. Point light : a position, shining in all directions, with attenuation over distance. Attenuation : the fall-off of light with distance. Spotlight : a point light limited to a cone, with a soft edge between an inner and outer cutoff."],["lighting/review.html#what-changed-in-the-renderer","Review","What changed in the renderer","VertexOut carries the world-space position and normal to the fragment function. Vertex uniforms gained a normal matrix. A second uniform struct, bound to fragment buffer slot 1, holds lights, the camera position and material values. Lights that come in variable numbers live in their own buffer and are indexed through a pointer. Two pipeline states share one vertex function: a lit one for objects and an unlit one for lamps."],["lighting/review.html#where-the-model-falls-short","Review","Where the model falls short","Phong lighting is an artistic approximation. Its weaknesses point straight at later chapters: Highlights break down at grazing angles. See Blinn-Phong . The colors are computed in the wrong color space. See Gamma Correction . Nothing casts a shadow. See Shadow Mapping . Flat surfaces look flat. See Normal Mapping . Materials do not conserve energy and need hand tuning per scene. See PBR . Before any of that, cubes are getting boring. The next section loads real models."],["model-loading/model-io.html","Model I/O","","Example project examples/Sources/11-ModelLoading , run with swift run ModelLoading . Download all examples or see how to run them . Nobody types the vertices of a character by hand. Artists build models in tools such as Blender and export them to files, and our job is to turn those files into vertex buffers, index buffers and textures. On Apple platforms you do not need a third-party library for this. The system framework Model I/O reads common 3D formats, and MetalKit converts the result into Metal objects. Model loading in C++ Model I/O and MetalKit's mesh types are not part of metal-cpp. The C++ version of this section loads Wavefront OBJ files with the single-header library tinyobjloader and builds the Metal buffers by hand. The ideas are the same: a model is meshes, a mesh has one vertex buffer and one index buffer per material, and a material is values plus textures. To load USD from C++, call Model I/O from a small Objective-C++ file, as the window code in Project Setup does for AppKit, or convert the model to OBJ or glTF."],["model-loading/model-io.html#formats","Model I/O","Formats","Model I/O imports, among others: Format Notes USD, USDZ, USDA, USDC Pixar's Universal Scene Description, Apple's preferred format. Supports materials, hierarchy and animation. OBJ (+ MTL) Simple and everywhere. Geometry and basic materials only. PLY, STL Geometry only, common for scans and 3D printing. Alembic (ABC) Baked animation caches. What about glTF and FBX? Model I/O does not read glTF or FBX. Convert them to USDZ with Apple's Reality Converter or with Blender, or use an open-source loader. For learning, OBJ and USDZ are plenty."],["model-loading/model-io.html#how-a-model-is-structured","Model I/O","How a model is structured","Model I/O represents a file as a tree: MDLAsset : the whole file. A container of objects. MDLObject : a node with a transform and children. MDLMesh : a node that has geometry. It owns vertex buffers and a vertex descriptor. MDLSubmesh : a part of a mesh drawn with one material. It owns an index buffer. MDLMaterial : named properties such as base color, roughness and normal, each a value or a texture. One mesh shares its vertices among several submeshes. A car body might be one mesh with a submesh for paint, one for glass and one for chrome."],["model-loading/model-io.html#asking-for-the-layout-you-want","Model I/O","Asking for the layout you want","Files store vertex data in whatever layout the exporter chose. Model I/O can rearrange it into the layout our shaders expect. Start from the Metal vertex descriptor, convert it, and name the attributes so Model I/O knows which data goes where: Since Model I/O writes the buffer for us, we are free to pack it tightly: .float3 occupies 12 bytes in a vertex buffer, so a vertex is 32 bytes. This is the same vertex layout as our cube in spirit, only without Swift's padding. The shader's VertexIn does not change at all; [[attribute(n)]] hides the layout."],["model-loading/model-io.html#loading-the-asset","Model I/O","Loading the asset","The buffer allocator is the key detail. With an MTKMeshBufferAllocator , Model I/O loads vertex and index data straight into MTLBuffer s, with no intermediate copy. Two optional steps are often useful right after loading:"],["model-loading/model-io.html#from-model-io-to-metal","Model I/O","From Model I/O to Metal","MetalKit turns every MDLMesh in the asset into an MTKMesh : You get two parallel arrays. The MTKMesh objects hold the Metal buffers for drawing. The MDLMesh objects still hold what Metal does not care about: materials and transforms. The next two chapters wrap these into types of our own."],["model-loading/model-io.html#built-in-shapes","Model I/O","Built-in shapes","Model I/O can also generate geometry, which is handy for tests: There are similar initializers for boxes, planes, cylinders, capsules, cones and icosahedra. Texture coordinates again Many files follow the OpenGL convention with v pointing up. If a model's textures look scrambled or mirrored, flip v when loading: mesh.flipTextureCoordinates(inAttributeNamed: MDLVertexAttributeTextureCoordinate) ."],["model-loading/mesh.html","Mesh","","Example project examples/Sources/11-ModelLoading , run with swift run ModelLoading . Download all examples or see how to run them . MetalKit handed us MTKMesh objects. This chapter looks inside one, draws it, and wraps it together with its materials in a small type of our own. Model loading in C++ Model I/O and MetalKit's mesh types are not part of metal-cpp. The C++ version of this section loads Wavefront OBJ files with the single-header library tinyobjloader and builds the Metal buffers by hand. The ideas are the same: a model is meshes, a mesh has one vertex buffer and one index buffer per material, and a material is values plus textures. To load USD from C++, call Model I/O from a small Objective-C++ file, as the window code in Project Setup does for AppKit, or convert the model to OBJ or glTF."],["model-loading/mesh.html#inside-an-mtkmesh","Mesh","Inside an MTKMesh","Property Contents vertexBuffers An array of MTKMeshBuffer , each with a buffer ( MTLBuffer ) and an offset into it. vertexDescriptor The Model I/O vertex descriptor of the data. submeshes An array of MTKSubmesh . vertexCount Number of vertices. and each MTKSubmesh has: Property Contents indexBuffer An MTKMeshBuffer with the indices. indexCount , indexType How many indices and whether they are 16 or 32 bit. primitiveType Usually .triangle . The offsets are important. The allocator may place several meshes in one large MTLBuffer , so always pass the offset when binding."],["model-loading/mesh.html#drawing-a-mesh","Mesh","Drawing a mesh","With the layout from the previous chapter there is one vertex buffer, bound at slot 0, which leaves slot 1 for uniforms as before. The pipeline state needs the matching Metal vertex descriptor. Either reuse the one you built by hand or derive it from the mesh:"],["model-loading/mesh.html#materials","Mesh","Materials","Each MDLSubmesh has an optional MDLMaterial . A material is a bag of properties looked up by semantic : A property can be a plain value or a texture, so we handle both. A small material type for the Phong shader from the lighting section: The texture case covers images embedded in USDZ archives (after asset.loadTextures() ), and the URL case covers OBJ files that reference images on disk next to the .mtl file. The sRGB flag Color textures are stored in sRGB, data textures such as specular or normal maps are not. Until the Gamma Correction chapter, pass false for everything so the output matches earlier chapters."],["model-loading/mesh.html#a-mesh-type","Mesh","A Mesh type","Bundle the Metal mesh with one material per submesh:"],["model-loading/mesh.html#a-fallback-texture","Mesh","A fallback texture","A shader that samples a texture must have one bound. For materials without a texture, bind a 1×1 white texture, which multiplies to nothing: This is also your first hand-made texture: a descriptor, makeTexture , and replace to upload bytes."],["model-loading/model.html","Model","","Example project examples/Sources/11-ModelLoading , run with swift run ModelLoading . Download all examples or see how to run them . A file usually contains several meshes arranged in a hierarchy, each with its own transform. A Model type collects them so that loading and drawing a whole file is one line each. Model loading in C++ Model I/O and MetalKit's mesh types are not part of metal-cpp. The C++ version of this section loads Wavefront OBJ files with the single-header library tinyobjloader and builds the Metal buffers by hand. The ideas are the same: a model is meshes, a mesh has one vertex buffer and one index buffer per material, and a material is values plus textures. To load USD from C++, call Model I/O from a small Objective-C++ file, as the window code in Project Setup does for AppKit, or convert the model to OBJ or glTF."],["model-loading/model.html#the-model-class","Model","The Model class","Every MDLObject can carry a transform relative to its parent. Walking up the parent chain and multiplying gives the mesh's placement within the model. Simple OBJ files have no hierarchy, so the loop just returns the identity. USD files often do, and skipping this step is why parts of a model sometimes appear piled up at the origin."],["model-loading/model.html#drawing","Model","Drawing","Each node combines the model's own matrix with its node transform: In the renderer: Add the point lights from the previous section and you have a properly lit, textured model."],["model-loading/model.html#finding-models","Model","Finding models","Apple publishes sample USDZ models in its AR Quick Look gallery. Sites such as Sketchfab, Poly Haven and Kenney offer free models. Check each license. Blender exports both OBJ and USD. When a model looks wrong, the usual suspects are, in order: flipped texture coordinates, missing normals, a scale that is off by a factor of 100 (centimeters versus meters), and ignored node transforms."],["model-loading/model.html#bounds-and-framing","Model","Bounds and framing","An unknown model can be any size. Model I/O reports the bounding box, which lets you scale and center anything automatically: Multiply normalize into the model matrix and every model fits in a box two units wide around the origin."],["model-loading/model.html#what-we-left-out","Model","What we left out","A production loader also handles skeletal animation ( MDLSkeleton , MDLAnimationBindComponent ), morph targets, cameras and lights stored in the file, and it caches textures so an image shared by ten materials is loaded once. Add a dictionary from URL to MTLTexture to the material loader as a first improvement."],["model-loading/model.html#exercises","Model","Exercises","Draw the model ten times using the cube positions from the first section. Implement the texture cache. Load a model with no textures and confirm that the fallback texture and baseColor produce a sensible result. You will need to pass baseColor to the shader in the fragment uniforms."],["advanced-metal/depth-testing.html","Depth Testing","","We turned on the depth buffer in Coordinate Systems and moved on. It deserves a closer look, because several later techniques, shadows above all, depend on understanding exactly what it stores."],["advanced-metal/depth-testing.html#how-the-test-works","Depth Testing","How the test works","The depth attachment stores one value per pixel. For every fragment the GPU compares the fragment's depth with the stored value using the compare function of the current depth-stencil state. If the test passes, the fragment is kept, and if depth writing is enabled, its depth replaces the stored one. If it fails, the fragment is discarded. The test conceptually runs after the fragment function. In practice GPUs run it before whenever they can ( early depth testing ), skipping the fragment function for hidden pixels. Apple GPUs go further with hidden surface removal: for opaque geometry they work out the visible fragment for each pixel first and shade only that one."],["advanced-metal/depth-testing.html#compare-functions","Depth Testing","Compare functions","MTLCompareFunction Fragment passes when .less its depth is less than the stored depth .lessEqual less than or equal .equal equal .greater , .greaterEqual greater (or equal) .notEqual different .always always. This is the default, meaning no depth test .never never A state with testing on and writing off is common: isDepthWriteEnabled = false lets fragments be hidden by existing geometry without affecting the buffer. Transparent objects and particle effects use it. Depth-stencil states are cheap, immutable objects. Create the handful you need at startup and switch between them with encoder.setDepthStencilState ."],["advanced-metal/depth-testing.html#the-values-are-not-linear","Depth Testing","The values are not linear","The depth buffer does not store distance. After the projection matrix and the perspective divide, depth in Metal is for a point at distance z in front of the camera. It is 0 at the near plane and 1 at the far plane, but in between it is strongly non-linear. With near 0.1 and far 100, a point just 1 unit away already has depth 0.9, and at 10 units it is 0.99. Most of the precision is spent close to the camera, which is where the eye notices errors most."],["advanced-metal/depth-testing.html#visualizing-depth","Depth Testing","Visualizing depth","The built-in [[position]] input of a fragment function contains the pixel coordinates in x and y and the depth in z: Almost everything is white unless you are very close. To see something useful, convert back to linear distance: Keep this function. Screen-space effects such as SSAO need it."],["advanced-metal/depth-testing.html#z-fighting","Depth Testing","Z-fighting","When two surfaces are nearly coplanar, their depth values round to the same number and the winner flips from pixel to pixel, producing a flickering stripe pattern called z-fighting . Remedies: Do not place surfaces that close. A small offset that nobody notices is the simplest cure. Push the near plane out. Going from 0.01 to 0.1 gains a lot of precision. Use a 32-bit float depth buffer. .depth32Float is supported everywhere in Metal and is what we use. .depth16Unorm saves memory at the cost of precision. Use a depth bias for decals and similar: encoder.setDepthBias(_:slopeScale:clamp:) nudges the depth of what you draw next."],["advanced-metal/depth-testing.html#reverse-z","Depth Testing","Reverse-Z","Floating-point numbers have the most precision near 0, and the perspective mapping needs the most precision near 1. The two can be made to cancel by flipping the range: map the near plane to 1 and the far plane to 0, clear depth to 0, and test with .greater . This reverse-Z setup gives almost uniform precision over huge view distances and is standard in modern engines. It works in Metal precisely because NDC depth runs from 0 to 1. We keep the conventional direction in this series so that the code stays easy to compare with other material."],["advanced-metal/depth-testing.html#exercises","Depth Testing","Exercises","Draw the scene with .always and explain the result in terms of draw order. Put two quads 0.0001 apart and move the camera away until they fight. Then try each remedy."],["advanced-metal/stencil-testing.html","Stencil Testing","","Next to the depth buffer lives the stencil buffer : 8 bits per pixel that you can write freely while drawing and test against later. It lets you mark regions of the screen and then restrict drawing to them. Outlines, portals, mirrors and masking UI are classic uses."],["advanced-metal/stencil-testing.html#enabling-a-stencil-buffer","Stencil Testing","Enabling a stencil buffer","Depth and stencil normally share one texture with a combined format: Every pipeline state used in the pass must declare both formats."],["advanced-metal/stencil-testing.html#the-stencil-test","Stencil Testing","The stencil test","For each fragment the GPU compares a reference value , which you set on the encoder, with the value in the buffer. The comparison and the reaction are described by an MTLStencilDescriptor inside the depth-stencil descriptor: Property Meaning stencilCompareFunction How reference and stored value are compared. stencilFailureOperation What to do with the stored value if the stencil test fails. depthFailureOperation If the stencil test passes but the depth test fails. depthStencilPassOperation If both pass. readMask , writeMask Bit masks applied when comparing and when writing. The operations are .keep , .zero , .replace (store the reference value), .incrementClamp , .decrementClamp , .invert , .incrementWrap and .decrementWrap . There are separate descriptors for front-facing and back-facing triangles, frontFaceStencil and backFaceStencil ."],["advanced-metal/stencil-testing.html#object-outlines","Stencil Testing","Object outlines","A selection outline is drawn in two steps: Draw the object normally and write 1 into the stencil buffer wherever it is visible. Draw the object again, slightly enlarged and in a flat color, only where the stencil is not 1. The enlarged copy is rejected over the object itself, and survives only in a thin border around it. State 1: write 1 wherever the object is drawn. State 2: draw only where the stencil is not 1, ignoring depth. Drawing: The solidColorPipelineState uses a fragment function that returns a single color. Because the outline state uses .always for depth, the border shows through other objects, which is what you want for a selection highlight. Better outlines Scaling works for convex shapes centered on their origin. For arbitrary models, move each vertex along its normal in the vertex function instead: position + normal * thickness ."],["advanced-metal/stencil-testing.html#reading-the-comparison-correctly","Stencil Testing","Reading the comparison correctly","The comparison is written as reference op stored . With .less and a reference of 1, a fragment passes where 1 < stored . It is easy to read this backwards."],["advanced-metal/stencil-testing.html#other-uses","Stencil Testing","Other uses","Portals and mirrors : draw the portal shape into the stencil buffer, then draw the world seen through it only where the stencil is set. Masking : clip UI or 2D content to arbitrary shapes. Deferred lighting volumes : mark the pixels a light can reach so the lighting shader runs only there."],["advanced-metal/stencil-testing.html#exercises","Stencil Testing","Exercises","Make the outline respect depth, so it is hidden behind closer objects. Outline only one of the cubes."],["advanced-metal/blending.html","Blending","","Blending is how transparency is done. Instead of replacing the color already in the texture, a new fragment's color is combined with it. The fourth color component, alpha , usually controls how: 1 is fully opaque, 0 is fully transparent."],["advanced-metal/blending.html#discarding-fragments","Blending","Discarding fragments","Some images are either fully opaque or fully transparent, like a grass or leaf texture with a cut-out shape. They need no blending. The fragment function can simply throw the transparent fragments away: A discarded fragment writes neither color nor depth. Load such textures with a sampler that uses .clampToEdge ; with .repeat , linear filtering blends the top row with the bottom row and leaves a faint line at the edge. On tile-based GPUs, discard_fragment prevents some hidden-surface optimizations, so use it only in the pipelines that need it."],["advanced-metal/blending.html#the-blend-equation","Blending","The blend equation","For semi-transparent surfaces such as glass, the pipeline computes where source is the fragment function's output and destination is the color already stored. In Metal, blending is part of the render pipeline state , configured per color attachment: This is classic alpha blending: result = source * alpha + destination * (1 - alpha) . A red window with alpha 0.5 in front of a green wall gives an even mix of both. Some other useful setups: Effect Source factor Destination factor Alpha blending .sourceAlpha .oneMinusSourceAlpha Premultiplied alpha .one .oneMinusSourceAlpha Additive (fire, glow) .one or .sourceAlpha .one Multiply (tint, shadow) .destinationColor .zero Coming from OpenGL There is no glEnable(GL_BLEND) to toggle between draws. You create one pipeline state for opaque drawing and another for blended drawing, and switch pipeline states. Premultiplied alpha Many image loaders, Core Graphics included, deliver colors already multiplied by their alpha. Blending such a texture with .sourceAlpha darkens the edges. Use .one as the source factor for premultiplied content."],["advanced-metal/blending.html#order-matters","Blending","Order matters","Blending and the depth buffer do not cooperate. If a window is drawn first, it writes its depth, and anything drawn later behind it is rejected by the depth test, even though it should be visible through the glass. The rule for a correct image: Draw all opaque objects first. Sort the transparent objects by distance from the camera. Draw them back to front , with depth testing on and depth writing off . Sorting by object distance is not perfect. Intersecting or very large transparent surfaces can still blend in the wrong order. Techniques that remove the need for sorting are grouped under the name order-independent transparency . Apple GPUs make one family of them practical through programmable blending, where a fragment function can read the current color of the pixel it is writing; you will meet that feature in Deferred Shading ."],["advanced-metal/blending.html#exercises","Blending","Exercises","Render grass quads with discard_fragment , then with blending but no sorting, and compare the edges. Draw the windows front to back to see the artifact that sorting prevents. Make an additive particle pipeline and draw a few overlapping quads."],["advanced-metal/face-culling.html","Face Culling","","Look at a closed object such as a cube from any angle, and you can see at most half of its faces. The faces pointing away from you are always hidden by the ones in front. Face culling tells the GPU to skip those triangles before rasterizing them, which saves roughly half of the fragment work for closed meshes."],["advanced-metal/face-culling.html#winding-order","Face Culling","Winding order","How does the GPU know which side of a triangle is the front? By the order in which its three vertices appear on screen: clockwise or counter-clockwise. That order is the triangle's winding . When you turn a triangle around, its winding as seen from the camera flips. Models are exported with a consistent winding, so one setting works for a whole mesh."],["advanced-metal/face-culling.html#enabling-culling","Face Culling","Enabling culling","Culling is render encoder state. It is not part of the pipeline state: MTLCullMode Effect .none Draw both sides. The default. .back Skip triangles facing away. .front Skip triangles facing the camera. Metal's default front face is clockwise MTLWinding.clockwise is the default front-facing winding in Metal, the opposite of OpenGL. Our cube, and most model files, use counter-clockwise fronts, so always call setFrontFacing(.counterClockwise) together with setCullMode . If a model appears inside out, with only its far walls visible, this is the cause. Encoder state resets with every new encoder, so set both at the start of each pass."],["advanced-metal/face-culling.html#when-not-to-cull","Face Culling","When not to cull","Flat, two-sided geometry : grass quads, leaves, cloth, windows. Disable culling for those draws with setCullMode(.none) . Transparent closed objects : you may want the inside faces visible through the outside ones. Draw the back faces first ( .front culling), then the front faces. Mirrored transforms : a model matrix with a negative scale on one axis flips the winding of everything it draws. Flip the front-face setting for those objects."],["advanced-metal/face-culling.html#culling-front-faces","Face Culling","Culling front faces","Culling the front faces is occasionally useful on purpose. Rendering only back faces into a shadow map reduces a self-shadowing artifact, as you will see in Shadow Mapping , and a skybox cube seen from the inside can be drawn with front culling or simply with culling off."],["advanced-metal/face-culling.html#which-side-am-i-on","Face Culling","Which side am I on?","A fragment function can ask whether it belongs to a front-facing triangle, using a built-in input: This is how two-sided materials are lit correctly with culling turned off."],["advanced-metal/face-culling.html#exercises","Face Culling","Exercises","Enable back-face culling and fly inside a cube. Reverse the winding of one cube face in the vertex data and observe which face disappears. Color front faces green and back faces red using [[front_facing]] with culling off."],["advanced-metal/render-targets.html","Render Targets","","Example project examples/Sources/12-RenderTargets , run with swift run RenderTargets . Download all examples or see how to run them . Every frame so far has been drawn straight into the view's drawable. A render pass can draw into any texture instead, and that texture can then be read by a later pass. Rendering to a texture is the basis of post-processing, shadows, reflections, bloom and deferred shading. Nearly every chapter after this one uses it. Coming from OpenGL Metal has no framebuffer object. Its role is played by the MTLRenderPassDescriptor : a lightweight description of which textures are the color, depth and stencil attachments for one pass. There is nothing to bind or unbind and no \"completeness\" check."],["advanced-metal/render-targets.html#creating-the-textures","Render Targets","Creating the textures","A texture you render into needs the .renderTarget usage. To sample it afterward it also needs .shaderRead : .private storage means only the GPU touches the texture. Call this function from mtkView(_:drawableSizeWillChange:) so the textures follow the window size."],["advanced-metal/render-targets.html#describing-the-pass","Render Targets","Describing the pass","The load and store actions deserve thought: The color result is needed later, so it is stored . The depth buffer is only needed during the pass, so .dontCare lets the GPU throw it away. On Apple GPUs, a depth texture that is never stored can even be created with storageMode = .memoryless . It then occupies no memory at all, living only in the GPU's tile memory while the pass runs."],["advanced-metal/render-targets.html#two-passes-in-one-frame","Render Targets","Two passes in one frame","The pipeline states used in pass 1 must declare the formats of that pass's attachments. The post-processing pass has no depth attachment needs, so its pipeline leaves depthAttachmentPixelFormat unset; if the view still has a depth format, set it to match or set view.depthStencilPixelFormat = .invalid ."],["advanced-metal/render-targets.html#the-full-screen-triangle","Render Targets","The full-screen triangle","To run a fragment function over every pixel we need geometry that covers the screen. A single oversized triangle does it, with no vertex buffer at all. The vertex function builds positions from the built-in vertex index: The triangle extends past the screen and is clipped to it. The y flip makes uv (0, 0) land on the top-left corner, matching Metal's texture origin, so a texture rendered by one pass is displayed upright by the next without any further fuss. The post pipeline has no vertex descriptor. Run it and the scene looks exactly as before. That is the point: it now passes through a texture that we can manipulate."],["advanced-metal/render-targets.html#post-processing","Render Targets","Post-processing","Inversion and grayscale change one pixel at a time: The weights reflect how sensitive the eye is to each channel. Kernel effects combine each pixel with its neighbors using a small grid of weights, a convolution kernel : Use a sampler with .clampToEdge so the kernel does not wrap around at the borders."],["advanced-metal/render-targets.html#exercises","Render Targets","Exercises","Render the scene at half resolution and stretch it to the screen. Compare .nearest and .linear filtering. Build a rear-view mirror: render the scene a second time with the camera turned 180 degrees into a small texture, and show it on a quad at the top of the screen."],["advanced-metal/cubemaps.html","Cubemaps","","A cubemap is a texture made of six square images arranged as the faces of a cube. It is sampled with a 3D direction instead of a 2D coordinate: imagine standing at the center of the cube and looking along the direction, and you get the color that you see. Cubemaps are used for skies, reflections, and, later, for omnidirectional shadows and image-based lighting."],["advanced-metal/cubemaps.html#creating-a-cubemap","Cubemaps","Creating a cubemap","The six faces are stored as six slices of one texture of type .typeCube , in the order +X, -X, +Y, -Y, +Z, -Z (right, left, top, bottom, front, back). From six image files , the shortest route is through Model I/O: From one image containing the six faces stacked top to bottom, the texture loader can split it: By hand , when you generate or decode the faces yourself:"],["advanced-metal/cubemaps.html#a-skybox","Cubemaps","A skybox","A skybox is a cube drawn around the camera and textured with a cubemap, giving the illusion of a vast environment. Its texture coordinate is simply the cube's local position, used as a direction. Two tricks are packed into the vertex function: The position is multiplied with w = 0 , which removes the translation of the view matrix. The sky rotates with the camera but never gets closer. The output is clip.xyww . After the perspective divide, z becomes w / w = 1 , the far plane. The sky ends up behind everything. Draw the sky last , after all opaque objects, with a depth state that uses .lessEqual and does not write depth: Drawn last, the sky's fragments are rejected wherever something else was drawn, so the sky costs only the pixels where it is visible. .lessEqual is needed because the sky's depth equals the cleared value of 1. The camera is inside the cube, so disable culling for this draw. Handedness Cubemaps use a left-handed convention, a historical quirk shared by all graphics APIs. If a skybox looks mirrored in your right-handed world, negate the x or z component of the direction in the shader."],["advanced-metal/cubemaps.html#reflection","Cubemaps","Reflection","With the environment in a cubemap, a mirror-like surface is easy: reflect the view direction around the normal and use the result to sample the sky. Combine it with a specular map to make only parts of a model reflective."],["advanced-metal/cubemaps.html#refraction","Cubemaps","Refraction","Light bends when it passes from one medium into another. refract computes the bent direction from the ratio of the two refractive indices : Material Index Air 1.00 Water 1.33 Glass 1.52 Diamond 2.42 This is a single refraction at the surface. It is physically incomplete, but it looks convincingly glassy."],["advanced-metal/cubemaps.html#rendering-into-a-cubemap","Cubemaps","Rendering into a cubemap","A static sky cannot reflect moving objects. For dynamic reflections, render the scene six times from the object's position, once into each face, with a 90 degree field of view and an aspect ratio of 1. Select the face with the attachment's slice : Because of the cubemap convention, these six views must be built with a left-handed view and projection matrix, and the up vectors are fixed: Face Look direction Up +X ( 1, 0, 0) (0, 1, 0) -X (-1, 0, 0) (0, 1, 0) +Y ( 0, 1, 0) (0, 0, -1) -Y ( 0, -1, 0) (0, 0, 1) +Z ( 0, 0, 1) (0, 1, 0) -Z ( 0, 0, -1) (0, 1, 0) A left-handed view mirrors the scene, which reverses triangle winding, so use setFrontFacing(.clockwise) while rendering the faces. We reuse these helpers for Point Shadows and for image-based lighting. Six scene renders per frame are expensive; engines update such probes rarely or one face per frame."],["advanced-metal/cubemaps.html#exercises","Cubemaps","Exercises","Draw the skybox first instead of last, with depth writes off, and measure the difference in the Xcode GPU report on a heavy scene. Blend reflection and refraction by a factor that depends on the viewing angle."],["advanced-metal/advanced-data.html","Advanced Data","","Buffers and textures have been black boxes created with one call. This chapter opens them up: where their memory lives, how to update it efficiently every frame, and how to copy data around on the GPU."],["advanced-metal/advanced-data.html#buffers-are-just-memory","Advanced Data","Buffers are just memory","An MTLBuffer is an untyped block of bytes. Metal attaches no meaning to it. The same buffer can hold vertices in its first half and uniforms in its second, and you say which part to use through the offset parameter when binding. Create an empty buffer and fill it later: buffer.contents() returns a raw pointer to the memory. Writing through it is the fastest way to update data: or for an array:"],["advanced-metal/advanced-data.html#storage-modes","Advanced Data","Storage modes","A resource's storage mode says who can access its memory. Mode CPU access GPU access Notes .shared yes yes One copy in memory visible to both. The default for buffers on Apple silicon. .private no yes GPU only. Best for render targets and static data. .managed yes yes Two synchronized copies. Only meaningful on Macs with discrete GPUs. .memoryless no during a pass Textures only, Apple GPUs. Exists only while a render pass runs. Apple silicon has unified memory : the CPU and GPU share the same physical RAM. A shared buffer is not copied when the GPU uses it, so \"uploading\" data is nothing more than writing to memory. Rules of thumb: Data you rewrite every frame: .shared . Textures you render into and static meshes you never touch again: .private . Depth or multisample attachments that you do not keep: .memoryless ."],["advanced-metal/advanced-data.html#updating-uniforms-safely","Advanced Data","Updating uniforms safely","setVertexBytes is convenient but limited to 4 KB per call and copies the data each time. A real renderer keeps uniforms in buffers. There is one hazard: the GPU runs behind the CPU. If you overwrite a buffer for frame N+1 while the GPU is still drawing frame N, frame N reads the wrong data. The standard cure is a small ring of buffers , one per frame in flight, with a semaphore that stops the CPU from getting too far ahead: setVertexBufferOffset is much cheaper than rebinding a buffer. One large buffer with per-object offsets is how thousands of draws stay fast. Make sure every early return after wait() signals the semaphore, or the app freezes after three frames."],["advanced-metal/advanced-data.html#copying-on-the-gpu-the-blit-encoder","Advanced Data","Copying on the GPU: the blit encoder","A third kind of encoder, next to render and compute, moves data between resources: The blit encoder is also how you fill a .private resource: write the data into a shared staging buffer and blit it across."],["advanced-metal/advanced-data.html#reading-data-back","Advanced Data","Reading data back","To read pixels back, the result has to be in CPU-visible memory after the GPU has finished: Never block the render loop with waitUntilCompleted() to get results. Use the completion handler."],["advanced-metal/advanced-data.html#texture-descriptors-in-full","Advanced Data","Texture descriptors in full","MTLTextureDescriptor has more to offer than width and height: Property Use textureType .type2D , .type2DArray , .typeCube , .type3D , .type2DMultisample , and more. pixelFormat Channel layout and precision. mipmapLevelCount Number of mip levels. arrayLength Layers in an array texture. sampleCount Samples per pixel for multisampling. usage .shaderRead , .shaderWrite , .renderTarget , .pixelFormatView . Declare only what you need; it lets Metal optimize. storageMode As above. Texture arrays stack many same-sized images in one texture, indexed by layer in the shader with texture2d_array<float> . They replace texture atlases without the bleeding problems."],["advanced-metal/advanced-data.html#exercises","Advanced Data","Exercises","Convert the ten-cube scene from setVertexBytes to the ring-buffer scheme. Render the scene to a texture, read it back, and save it as a PNG with Core Graphics."],["advanced-metal/advanced-msl.html","Advanced MSL","","This chapter collects features of the Metal Shading Language that do not get a chapter of their own but are worth knowing. Nothing here is required reading; skim it now and return when you need a piece."],["advanced-metal/advanced-msl.html#built-in-inputs-and-outputs","Advanced MSL","Built-in inputs and outputs","Attributes in double brackets connect variables to values the pipeline provides. Vertex function inputs Attribute Type Meaning [[vertex_id]] uint Index of the vertex being processed. [[instance_id]] uint Index of the instance. See Instancing . [[base_vertex]] , [[base_instance]] uint Offsets given in the draw call. Vertex function outputs Attribute Type Meaning [[position]] float4 Clip-space position. Required. [[point_size]] float Size in pixels when drawing .point primitives. [[clip_distance]] float or array Custom clip planes. [[render_target_array_index]] uint Layer or cube face to render into. Fragment function inputs Attribute Type Meaning [[position]] float4 Pixel coordinates (x, y), depth (z), and 1/w. [[front_facing]] bool Whether the triangle faces the camera. [[point_coord]] float2 Position inside a point primitive, 0 to 1. [[sample_id]] , [[sample_mask]] uint Multisampling details. [[color(n)]] float4 Current value of color attachment n (Apple GPUs). Fragment function outputs Attribute Type Meaning [[color(n)]] float4 etc. Value for color attachment n. [[depth(any)]] float Override the fragment's depth. Also greater and less . [[sample_mask]] uint Which samples to write. A fragment function with several outputs returns a struct:"],["advanced-metal/advanced-msl.html#interpolation-control","Advanced MSL","Interpolation control","Members of the vertex output are interpolated with perspective correction by default. You can change that per member: [[flat]] is required for integer members, such as a material index passed to the fragment function."],["advanced-metal/advanced-msl.html#address-spaces","Advanced MSL","Address spaces","Every pointer or reference parameter must say where its memory lives: constant : read-only buffer data. Use it for uniforms. device : read-write buffer data. Use it for large arrays and anything a compute function writes. This example also shows that a vertex descriptor is optional. A vertex function can fetch its own data from a buffer by index, which is sometimes called vertex pulling . The struct layout must then match the buffer bytes exactly, using packed_float3 where the data is tightly packed."],["advanced-metal/advanced-msl.html#function-constants","Advanced MSL","Function constants","A value marked as a function constant is fixed when the function is specialized, before the pipeline state is created. The compiler can then remove dead branches entirely: This is Metal's answer toifdef shader variants: one source function, many specialized pipeline states."],["advanced-metal/advanced-msl.html#argument-buffers","Advanced MSL","Argument buffers","Binding ten textures and three buffers one call at a time is slow when you have thousands of draws. An argument buffer is a struct of resources stored in a buffer and bound with a single call: On the Swift side an MTLArgumentEncoder , created from the function with makeArgumentEncoder(bufferIndex:) , writes the resources into the buffer. Newer OS versions allow writing resource IDs into the buffer directly. You must also tell the encoder which resources the buffer references, with useResource(_:usage:stages:) , so Metal can track them. Argument buffers can contain arrays of textures, which is the foundation of \"bindless\" rendering and of ray tracing, where any ray may hit any material."],["advanced-metal/advanced-msl.html#samplers-defined-in-the-shader","Advanced MSL","Samplers defined in the shader","A sampler does not have to come from Swift. It can be declared as a constant: It is convenient for post-processing shaders where the settings never change."],["advanced-metal/advanced-msl.html#texture-functions","Advanced MSL","Texture functions","Call Purpose tex.sample(s, uv) Filtered read. tex.sample(s, uv, level(2)) Read a specific mip level. tex.sample(s, uv, bias(1)) Offset the automatic mip level. tex.sample(s, uv, gradient2d(dx, dy)) Supply your own derivatives. tex.read(uint2(x, y), lod) Exact texel, no sampler. tex.gather(s, uv) The four neighboring texels of one channel. tex.get_width() , tex.get_height() , tex.get_num_mip_levels() Queries. depthTex.sample_compare(s, uv, ref) Depth comparison with filtering, for shadows."],["advanced-metal/advanced-msl.html#precision-half","Advanced MSL","Precision: half","half is a 16-bit float. On mobile GPUs half-precision math is faster and uses less power, and it is precise enough for colors and normalized directions. Use float for positions, texture coordinates on large textures, and depth. Literals take an h suffix: half3(0.5h) ."],["advanced-metal/advanced-msl.html#derivatives","Advanced MSL","Derivatives","dfdx(v) and dfdy(v) return how fast a value changes between neighboring pixels, and fwidth(v) is the sum of their absolute values. They are the tools for anti-aliased procedural patterns and for computing a flat face normal without vertex normals: Depending on your handedness and winding the result may need to be negated."],["advanced-metal/no-geometry-shaders.html","Life Without Geometry Shaders","","Example project examples/Sources/17-ComputeParticles , run with swift run ComputeParticles . Download all examples or see how to run them . Other APIs have a geometry shader stage between the vertex and fragment stages. It receives a whole primitive and can emit new ones: turn a point into a quad, a triangle into three lines, one triangle into six for the faces of a cubemap. Metal has no geometry shaders, by design. They fit poorly on GPU hardware, especially on tile-based architectures, and they are slow nearly everywhere. Each of their classic uses has a better replacement in Metal. This chapter walks through them."],["advanced-metal/no-geometry-shaders.html#points-into-quads-instancing","Life Without Geometry Shaders","Points into quads: instancing","The standard geometry shader demo expands points into camera-facing quads, or billboards, for particles. In Metal, draw a four-vertex quad with one instance per particle, and build the corners in the vertex function: No vertex buffer for the quad, no vertex descriptor, and one draw call for any number of particles. For particles that are always small, Metal can also draw .point primitives directly. Write the size to a [[point_size]] output and use [[point_coord]] in the fragment function as the texture coordinate."],["advanced-metal/no-geometry-shaders.html#visualizing-normals-lines-from-vertex-ids","Life Without Geometry Shaders","Visualizing normals: lines from vertex IDs","Another favorite: draw a short line along every vertex normal to debug a mesh. Draw .line primitives with twice as many vertices as the mesh has, and derive both the source vertex and which end of the line you are from the vertex ID: This pattern, fetching from a buffer with a computed index, replaces most geometry shaders that emit a fixed amount of geometry per input. The same idea gives exploding meshes: use vertexID / 3 to find the triangle, compute its face normal from the three vertices, and push the vertex outward."],["advanced-metal/no-geometry-shaders.html#rendering-to-many-targets-at-once-layered-rendering","Life Without Geometry Shaders","Rendering to many targets at once: layered rendering","Geometry shaders are also used to send one draw call to all six faces of a cubemap or to several layers of a texture array. Metal covers this with two features. Layered rendering : the vertex function selects the destination layer by writing [[render_target_array_index]] . Combined with instancing, one draw call renders the scene into every face: The render pass descriptor must set renderTargetArrayLength = 6 , and the pipeline descriptor needs inputPrimitiveTopology = .triangle . Vertex amplification : the GPU runs the vertex function once per vertex and replicates the output to several layers or viewports, with a per-view index available as [[amplification_id]] . It was introduced for stereo rendering, where each eye needs its own view matrix, and it is cheaper than instancing because shared work is done once. Set maxVertexAmplificationCount on the pipeline descriptor and call setVertexAmplificationCount on the encoder."],["advanced-metal/no-geometry-shaders.html#generating-geometry-compute-and-mesh-shaders","Life Without Geometry Shaders","Generating geometry: compute and mesh shaders","When the amount of output geometry is truly dynamic, there are two tools. A compute function can write vertices into a buffer that a later render pass draws. This is the general-purpose answer for procedural meshes, marching cubes, and particle simulation. See Compute Shaders . Mesh shaders , available on recent Apple GPUs and OS versions, replace the vertex stage with two programmable stages. An object function decides how many groups of geometry to emit, for example culling invisible clusters, and a mesh function outputs small batches of vertices and primitives directly to the rasterizer. They do everything geometry shaders did, efficiently, and are the foundation of modern GPU-driven rendering. Check support with device.supportsFamily(.metal3) ."],["advanced-metal/no-geometry-shaders.html#subdividing-tessellation","Life Without Geometry Shaders","Subdividing: tessellation","If the goal is to add detail to a surface, by subdividing triangles or displacing terrain, the right tool is the tessellator, covered in Tessellation ."],["advanced-metal/no-geometry-shaders.html#summary","Life Without Geometry Shaders","Summary","Geometry shader use Metal replacement Points to quads Instanced quad, or point primitives Normals, wireframe, exploding Vertex pulling with computed indices Cubemap or array in one pass render_target_array_index with instancing or vertex amplification Procedural geometry Compute functions, mesh shaders Subdivision Tessellation"],["advanced-metal/instancing.html","Instancing","","Example project examples/Sources/13-Instancing , run with swift run Instancing . Download all examples or see how to run them . A field of grass, a forest, an asteroid belt: thousands of copies of the same mesh, each with a different transform. Issuing one draw call per copy wastes CPU time, because each call has a fixed cost no matter how small the mesh is. Instancing draws all copies with a single call."],["advanced-metal/instancing.html#instanced-draw-calls","Instancing","Instanced draw calls","Every draw method has a variant with an instanceCount : The GPU runs the vertex function for every vertex of every instance. The only difference between instances is the built-in [[instance_id]] , counting from 0. Everything that makes instances look different is derived from that number."],["advanced-metal/instancing.html#per-instance-data-in-a-buffer","Instancing","Per-instance data in a buffer","Put one struct per instance into a buffer and index it with the instance ID: Using the model matrix for the normal is only correct for rotations and uniform scales. Store a normal matrix per instance if you need non-uniform scaling. Coming from OpenGL There is no need for instanced vertex attributes with divisors, and no limit on the size of per-instance data. A buffer and an index are enough. If you prefer attributes anyway, a vertex descriptor layout can advance once per instance: layouts[1].stepFunction = .perInstance ."],["advanced-metal/instancing.html#an-asteroid-field","Instancing","An asteroid field","Generate a ring of randomly placed, rotated and scaled rocks around a planet: Drawn with one call per rock, a few thousand asteroids already cost real CPU time each frame. Drawn instanced, a hundred thousand run smoothly, and the limit becomes how many triangles the GPU can fill."],["advanced-metal/instancing.html#animating-instances","Instancing","Animating instances","Since the instance buffer is shared memory, updating it is a plain write each frame. Use the ring-buffer scheme from Advanced Data so you do not overwrite data the GPU is still reading. For very large counts, moving the update itself to the GPU with a compute function removes the CPU from the loop entirely."],["advanced-metal/instancing.html#culling-and-the-next-step","Instancing","Culling and the next step","Instancing draws every instance, visible or not. Large worlds cull on the CPU and write only visible instances to the buffer, or let the GPU do it: a compute pass tests each instance against the view frustum and writes the survivors into an indirect command buffer , a list of draw calls encoded on the GPU itself ( MTLIndirectCommandBuffer ). That technique, GPU-driven rendering, is beyond this series, but instancing is its first step."],["advanced-metal/instancing.html#exercises","Instancing","Exercises","Draw a 100 × 100 grid of cubes with one call, coloring each by its instance ID. Make the asteroid ring rotate by passing a time uniform and rotating in the vertex function, with no CPU buffer updates."],["advanced-metal/anti-aliasing.html","Anti-Aliasing","","Look closely at the edge of any cube we have rendered and you see a staircase. A pixel is either inside a triangle or outside it, and nothing in between. That stair-step pattern is aliasing , and techniques that hide it are called anti-aliasing ."],["advanced-metal/anti-aliasing.html#why-edges-alias","Anti-Aliasing","Why edges alias","The rasterizer tests one point per pixel, its center, against each triangle. If the center is covered, the fragment function runs and the whole pixel takes the color. A slanted edge therefore advances in whole-pixel steps. Rendering at a higher resolution and scaling down ( supersampling ) solves it, but shading four times as many pixels is expensive."],["advanced-metal/anti-aliasing.html#multisampling","Anti-Aliasing","Multisampling","Multisample anti-aliasing (MSAA) gets most of the benefit at a fraction of the cost. Each pixel has several sample points , typically four, at different positions inside it. For every triangle: coverage and depth are tested at every sample , the fragment function still runs only once per pixel , its result is stored in each sample that the triangle covers. At the end, the samples of each pixel are averaged in a step called the resolve . A pixel half covered by a triangle ends up as an even mix of the triangle's color and the background."],["advanced-metal/anti-aliasing.html#msaa-with-mtkview","Anti-Aliasing","MSAA with MTKView","MTKView manages the multisample texture and the resolve for you. Two lines are needed: Every pipeline state used in the pass must have the same sample count as the attachments. Run it and the edges are smooth. A sample count of 4 is supported on all Metal devices. Check others with device.supportsTextureSampleCount(8) ."],["advanced-metal/anti-aliasing.html#msaa-in-your-own-render-passes","Anti-Aliasing","MSAA in your own render passes","For offscreen passes you create the textures yourself. You need two color textures: a multisample texture to render into, and an ordinary texture to receive the resolved image. The depth texture must be multisampled in the same way. Then connect them in the render pass descriptor: The store action .multisampleResolve averages the samples into resolveTexture at the end of the pass and discards the multisample data. Because the samples are never needed afterward, the multisample textures can be memoryless on Apple GPUs: 4× MSAA then costs no extra memory at all, only some extra work inside each tile. This is a real advantage of tile-based GPUs and makes MSAA unusually cheap on Apple devices."],["advanced-metal/anti-aliasing.html#limits-of-msaa","Anti-Aliasing","Limits of MSAA","MSAA smooths geometry edges only. The fragment function runs once per pixel, so it does nothing for: shimmering inside a surface from high-frequency textures or sharp specular highlights, edges created by discard_fragment in alpha-tested textures. For those, enable isAlphaToCoverageEnabled on the pipeline descriptor, which converts the fragment's alpha into a sample coverage mask. MSAA also clashes with deferred shading , where lighting happens after geometry has been flattened into textures."],["advanced-metal/anti-aliasing.html#reading-individual-samples","Anti-Aliasing","Reading individual samples","A shader can read a multisample texture directly through the texture2d_ms<float> type, one sample at a time: A custom resolve matters for HDR rendering, where tone mapping each sample before averaging gives much cleaner bright edges. The multisample texture then needs .shaderRead usage and cannot be memoryless."],["advanced-metal/anti-aliasing.html#other-approaches","Anti-Aliasing","Other approaches","FXAA and SMAA are post-processing filters that find edges in the finished image and blur along them. They are cheap and work with any renderer, at the cost of some sharpness. Temporal anti-aliasing (TAA) shifts the camera by a sub-pixel amount each frame and blends with previous frames. It handles shading aliasing too, but needs motion vectors and care to avoid ghosting. MetalFX is Apple's upscaling framework. You render at a lower resolution, and it reconstructs a sharp, anti-aliased image at full resolution with either a spatial or a temporal method. The temporal upscaler doubles as high-quality anti-aliasing."],["advanced-metal/anti-aliasing.html#exercises","Anti-Aliasing","Exercises","Compare sample counts 1, 2 and 4 on a thin, nearly horizontal triangle. Draw alpha-tested grass with and without alpha-to-coverage under 4× MSAA."],["advanced-lighting/blinn-phong.html","Blinn-Phong","","The Phong specular term has a flaw that shows on rough surfaces viewed at a low angle. A small change to the formula, proposed by James Blinn in 1977, fixes it and has been the default ever since."],["advanced-lighting/blinn-phong.html#the-problem-with-phong","Blinn-Phong","The problem with Phong","Phong measures the angle between the view direction and the reflected light direction . When the viewer and the light are on the same side of a surface and both near the horizon, that angle can exceed 90 degrees. The dot product goes negative and is clamped to zero, so the highlight is cut off abruptly. With a high shininess exponent the highlight is tiny and the cut-off region is never reached. With a low exponent, such as a wide, soft highlight on a floor, you get a visible hard edge where the specular light suddenly stops."],["advanced-lighting/blinn-phong.html#the-halfway-vector","Blinn-Phong","The halfway vector","Blinn-Phong replaces the reflection vector with the halfway vector : the unit vector exactly between the light direction and the view direction. The specular strength is then the angle between the halfway vector and the normal : When the view direction lines up perfectly with the reflection, the halfway vector lines up with the normal, so both models agree on where the highlight is brightest. The difference is that the angle between the normal and the halfway vector never exceeds 90 degrees as long as the light is above the surface, so the cut-off cannot happen."],["advanced-lighting/blinn-phong.html#in-the-shader","Blinn-Phong","In the shader","Only the specular lines of the lighting functions change:"],["advanced-lighting/blinn-phong.html#adjusting-shininess","Blinn-Phong","Adjusting shininess","The angle to the halfway vector is smaller than the angle Phong uses, roughly half of it. The same exponent therefore gives a larger highlight with Blinn-Phong. To get a similar look, multiply your old shininess values by somewhere between 2 and 4."],["advanced-lighting/blinn-phong.html#a-test-scene","Blinn-Phong","A test scene","Lay a large textured floor under a single point light close to the surface, and add a uniform that switches between the two formulas: With Phong, the highlight on the floor is chopped off along a curve. With Blinn-Phong it fades out smoothly and stretches toward the horizon, which is how real glossy floors behave. Booleans in uniforms MSL's bool is one byte, and so is Swift's Bool , but alignment and padding around them are easy to get wrong. Many people use a uint or int flag ( UInt32 in Swift) in uniform structs to keep layouts obvious. Blinn-Phong is used for the rest of this section. It is still an empirical model, and the PBR section will replace it, but the halfway vector stays: it is central to physically based specular reflection as well."],["advanced-lighting/gamma-correction.html","Gamma Correction","","There is a mistake in every image we have rendered so far. Lighting math assumes that a value of 0.5 is half as bright as 1.0. On a display, it is not."],["advanced-lighting/gamma-correction.html#displays-are-not-linear","Gamma Correction","Displays are not linear","Displays follow the sRGB standard, which is close to a power curve with an exponent of 2.2, called gamma . A pixel value of 0.5 is shown at about 0.5^2.2 ≈ 0.22 of full brightness. This is not a defect. Human vision is more sensitive to differences between dark tones than between bright ones, and the curve spends more of the 256 available steps on the dark end, where we notice them. The consequence: image files are stored already encoded for this curve, and whatever we write to the screen is treated as encoded too."],["advanced-lighting/gamma-correction.html#what-goes-wrong","Gamma Correction","What goes wrong","If we do lighting directly on encoded values: Adding two lights does not double the brightness on screen. Gradients from light to dark fall off too fast, so shading has harsh, dark terminators. Colors shift toward oversaturation, and attenuation looks wrong. The fix is a linear workflow : convert inputs to linear space, do all math there, and convert the result back to sRGB at the very end."],["advanced-lighting/gamma-correction.html#metal-does-both-conversions-for-you","Gamma Correction","Metal does both conversions for you","Pixel formats ending in _srgb tell Metal that a texture holds sRGB-encoded data: Sampling an sRGB texture returns linear values. The conversion happens before filtering, which is the correct order. Writing to an sRGB render target converts linear shader output to sRGB. Blending is also performed in linear space. So gamma correction is two configuration changes and zero shader code. 1. Make the view's drawable sRGB: and use the same format in the pipeline descriptors that render to it. 2. Load color textures as sRGB: Only colors are sRGB Textures that hold colors made by an artist looking at a screen are sRGB: diffuse, albedo, emissive. Textures that hold data are linear: normal maps, specular, roughness, metalness, height, ambient occlusion. Decoding a normal map as sRGB bends every normal and produces subtly wrong lighting that is hard to diagnose. If you leave out the .SRGB option, the loader decides from the image file's metadata, which may or may not be what you want. Being explicit avoids surprises."],["advanced-lighting/gamma-correction.html#doing-it-by-hand","Gamma Correction","Doing it by hand","If you cannot use an sRGB target, apply the curve at the end of the fragment function: and decode textures on read with pow(texel, float3(2.2)) . The automatic route is more accurate, since the true sRGB curve is not a pure power function, and it filters and blends correctly. Do one or the other, never both, or the image comes out washed out."],["advanced-lighting/gamma-correction.html#constant-colors","Gamma Correction","Constant colors","Colors typed into code or picked in a color picker are sRGB values. In a linear workflow they should be converted before use: The view's clearColor is given in linear space when the drawable format is sRGB, so the same numbers now look brighter than before."],["advanced-lighting/gamma-correction.html#attenuation","Gamma Correction","Attenuation","Physically, light intensity falls with the square of the distance: Without gamma correction this looks far too harsh, because the display's curve squares the falloff once more. That is why older tutorials use linear falloff or the three-term formula from Light Casters , which were tuned by eye on uncorrected output. With a linear workflow, inverse-square falloff looks right, and from here on we use it."],["advanced-lighting/gamma-correction.html#what-to-expect","Gamma Correction","What to expect","After the switch, the scene looks brighter and flatter in the mid-tones, and light and material values tuned earlier will need adjusting. This is normal. What you gain is lighting that adds up correctly, which every chapter from here to the end of the PBR section depends on."],["advanced-lighting/gamma-correction.html#wide-color","Gamma Correction","Wide color","Apple displays cover the Display P3 gamut, which is larger than sRGB. MTKView has a colorspace property (through its layer on iOS) and extended-range pixel formats such as .bgra10_xr_srgb for rendering colors outside sRGB. It is an advanced topic and unnecessary for learning, but good to know it exists when a designer asks why a red looks duller in your renderer than in Photos."],["advanced-lighting/shadow-mapping.html","Shadow Mapping","","Example project examples/Sources/14-ShadowMapping , run with swift run ShadowMapping . Download all examples or see how to run them . Shadows anchor objects to the ground and reveal how things are arranged in space. The standard real-time technique is shadow mapping , and it is built from parts you already know: a depth buffer and render-to-texture."],["advanced-lighting/shadow-mapping.html#the-idea","Shadow Mapping","The idea","A point is in shadow if something sits between it and the light. Put a camera at the light and render the scene's depth into a texture. That texture, the shadow map , records for every direction how far the light reaches before hitting something. When shading a fragment in the main pass, transform it into the light's view and compare its depth with the shadow map. If the fragment is farther from the light than the stored value, something closer is blocking the light."],["advanced-lighting/shadow-mapping.html#the-shadow-map-texture","Shadow Mapping","The shadow map texture","The pass has no color attachment at all. This time the depth must be stored, since reading it is the whole point."],["advanced-lighting/shadow-mapping.html#the-lights-matrices","Shadow Mapping","The light's matrices","A directional light has parallel rays, so its projection is orthographic : a box with no perspective. Add to Math.swift : The box must contain everything that casts or receives shadows in the area you care about. Keep it as tight as you can: the shadow map's resolution is spread over the box."],["advanced-lighting/shadow-mapping.html#the-depth-pass","Shadow Mapping","The depth pass","The pipeline for this pass has a vertex function, no fragment function, and no color format: A vertex function may return a bare float4 ; it is taken as the position. Each frame, render the scene with this pipeline into shadowPass before the main pass, using the same command buffer."],["advanced-lighting/shadow-mapping.html#using-the-shadow-map","Shadow Mapping","Using the shadow map","In the main pass, the vertex function also outputs the position in the light's clip space: The fragment function converts that to a texture coordinate and a depth to compare: Dividing by w is unnecessary for an orthographic projection but makes the function work for perspective lights as well. NDC x and y run from -1 to 1 with y up, and texture coordinates run from 0 to 1 with y down. Hence the scale of (0.5, -0.5) . Depth is already in the 0 to 1 range and needs no conversion. Depth textures have their own shader type, depth2d<float> . sample_compare compares the reference depth against the texture and returns 1 if the test passes and 0 if not. The test is configured on the sampler: With .lessEqual , the result is 1 where the fragment is at or in front of the stored depth, meaning lit. Apply it to the diffuse and specular terms and leave ambient alone:"],["advanced-lighting/shadow-mapping.html#shadow-acne","Shadow Mapping","Shadow acne","The first result is covered in dark stripes. The shadow map has limited resolution, so many fragments share one texel, and across a slanted surface half of them come out slightly behind the stored depth. This is shadow acne . The cure is a depth bias : push the stored depth a little away from the light. Metal applies it during rasterization of the shadow pass: The constant part is measured in units that depend on the depth format, so treat these numbers as a starting point. The slope-scaled part automatically increases the bias on surfaces at a steep angle to the light, which need it most. Too much bias detaches the shadow from its caster, an artifact known as peter panning . Tune until both disappear. Rendering only back faces into the shadow map ( setCullMode(.front) ) also helps for closed meshes."],["advanced-lighting/shadow-mapping.html#softer-edges","Shadow Mapping","Softer edges","Because the comparison sampler uses linear filtering, the GPU compares the four nearest texels and blends the results . That is hardware percentage-closer filtering (PCF), and it softens edges for free. Averaging a few more taps softens them further:"],["advanced-lighting/shadow-mapping.html#outside-the-map","Shadow Mapping","Outside the map","Fragments outside the light's box sample beyond the texture. With .clampToEdge they repeat the border texel, which can smear shadows across the scene. Either return \"lit\" when uv is outside 0 to 1, or use .clampToBorderColor with an opaque white border where it is supported."],["advanced-lighting/shadow-mapping.html#beyond-one-map","Shadow Mapping","Beyond one map","A single map cannot cover a large outdoor scene with enough resolution. Cascaded shadow maps split the view frustum into several ranges, each with its own map, stored in the layers of a depth2d_array texture. For spotlights, use a perspective projection for the light and the same code."],["advanced-lighting/shadow-mapping.html#exercises","Shadow Mapping","Exercises","Display the shadow map on a full-screen triangle to check what the light sees. Set the bias to zero, then to a large value, and identify acne and peter panning. Move the light over time and watch the shadows follow."],["advanced-lighting/point-shadows.html","Point Shadows","","A directional shadow map covers one direction. A point light shines in all directions, so one flat map cannot hold its shadows. The answer is a cube shadow map: six depth maps arranged around the light, sampled with the direction from the light to the fragment."],["advanced-lighting/point-shadows.html#the-plan","Point Shadows","The plan","Render the scene six times from the light's position, once per cube face, storing each fragment's distance to the light . In the main pass, sample the cube with the vector from the light to the fragment and compare the stored distance with the actual one. Storing linear distance instead of projected depth makes the comparison trivial and the bias easy to choose."],["advanced-lighting/point-shadows.html#the-cube-depth-texture","Point Shadows","The cube depth texture",""],["advanced-lighting/point-shadows.html#six-views","Point Shadows","Six views","Use the left-handed helpers and the face table from the Cubemaps chapter. Each face has a 90 degree field of view and an aspect ratio of 1, so the six frustums fit together exactly: Render one pass per face, selecting the face through the attachment's slice : Six passes can be merged into one with layered rendering, as described in Life Without Geometry Shaders . Start with the loop; it is easier to debug."],["advanced-lighting/point-shadows.html#writing-distance-as-depth","Point Shadows","Writing distance as depth","The fragment function overrides the depth value with the linear distance to the light, scaled into the 0 to 1 range: [[depth(any)]] lets a fragment function replace the depth computed by the rasterizer. The pipeline has a fragment function this time, but still no color attachment."],["advanced-lighting/point-shadows.html#sampling-the-cube","Point Shadows","Sampling the cube","In the main pass: The direction does not need to be normalized. The cube picks the face and texel by itself. With a comparison sampler you can write it as shadowCube.sample_compare(s, lightToFragment, currentDepth - bias) and get hardware filtering as a bonus."],["advanced-lighting/point-shadows.html#soft-edges","Point Shadows","Soft edges","For a 2D map we averaged neighboring texels. For a cube, offset the sample direction instead. Twenty well-spread offsets give a good result: Scaling the radius with distance makes shadows sharper near the caster and softer farther away, which is how real shadows behave."],["advanced-lighting/point-shadows.html#cost","Point Shadows","Cost","Point shadows render the scene six extra times per light. Keep the number of shadow-casting point lights small, cull objects per face, and update the map only when the light or the objects near it move."],["advanced-lighting/point-shadows.html#exercises","Point Shadows","Exercises","Visualize the stored distance by returning float4(float3(closestDepth), 1) . Convert the six passes into a single layered pass."],["advanced-lighting/normal-mapping.html","Normal Mapping","","A brick wall rendered as a flat quad looks flat, however good the texture is. The lighting gives it away, because every fragment has the same normal. Normal mapping stores a different normal for every texel, so light reacts to bumps and grooves that are not there in the geometry."],["advanced-lighting/normal-mapping.html#normal-maps","Normal Mapping","Normal maps","A normal is a vector with components from -1 to 1. Stored in an image, each component is remapped to the 0 to 1 range, and the shader undoes it: Normal maps look bluish because most normals point straight out of the surface, along +z, which is stored as the color (0.5, 0.5, 1.0). Load normal maps as linear data, never sRGB."],["advanced-lighting/normal-mapping.html#tangent-space","Normal Mapping","Tangent space","The normals in the map are expressed relative to the surface itself, in tangent space , where +z points away from the surface. That is what makes one map usable on a wall facing any direction, or wrapped around a curved model. To light with them, we need a way to rotate them from tangent space into world space. That rotation is the TBN matrix , built from three perpendicular unit vectors at each vertex: N , the vertex normal, T , the tangent , pointing along the surface in the direction of increasing u, B , the bitangent , pointing along the surface in the direction of increasing v."],["advanced-lighting/normal-mapping.html#getting-tangents","Normal Mapping","Getting tangents","Tangents are computed from positions and texture coordinates. Model I/O does it for you. Add a tangent attribute to the vertex descriptor and ask for the basis after loading: Assigning the descriptor again makes Model I/O repack the buffers with the new attribute. For hand-built geometry, the tangent of a triangle with edges e1 , e2 and texture coordinate differences (du1, dv1) , (du2, dv2) is:"],["advanced-lighting/normal-mapping.html#the-shader","Normal Mapping","The shader","The vertex function transforms the normal and the tangent into world space: The fragment function rebuilds an exactly perpendicular basis, then rotates the sampled normal with it: float3x3(T, B, N) builds a matrix with those vectors as columns, so multiplying takes a tangent-space vector into world space. Use the returned normal in place of normalize(in.normal) everywhere in the lighting code. Nothing else changes."],["advanced-lighting/normal-mapping.html#green-channel-and-handedness","Normal Mapping","Green channel and handedness","Two things commonly make a normal-mapped surface look \"inside out\", with bumps lit as dents: Normal map convention. Maps come in two flavors that differ in the sign of the green channel, usually labeled OpenGL (+Y) and DirectX (-Y). Which one is right depends on which way your v coordinate and bitangent run. If bumps look inverted, negate tangentNormal.y . Mirrored texture coordinates. Symmetric models often mirror their UVs, which flips the bitangent on one half. Exporters encode this as a sign in the tangent's fourth component. Model I/O offers addTangentBasis(forTextureCoordinateAttributeNamed:tangentAttributeNamed:bitangentAttributeNamed:) to store an explicit bitangent if you need to support it. A quick sanity check for any model: output N * 0.5 + 0.5 as the color. Smoothly changing pastel colors mean the basis is healthy."],["advanced-lighting/normal-mapping.html#two-channel-normal-maps","Normal Mapping","Two-channel normal maps","Since a unit normal's z can be recomputed from x and y, normal maps are often stored with two channels in a compressed format, for example BC5 on Mac or EAC RG11 and ASTC on Apple GPUs:"],["advanced-lighting/normal-mapping.html#exercises","Normal Mapping","Exercises","Rotate a normal-mapped quad over time and confirm the lighting stays consistent with a fixed light. Scale the x and y of the tangent normal by a \"bump strength\" uniform before normalizing."],["advanced-lighting/parallax-mapping.html","Parallax Mapping","","Normal mapping changes how a surface is lit, but not what you see where. At a shallow angle a normal-mapped brick wall still looks like a painted board, because the bricks do not hide the mortar behind them. Parallax mapping shifts the texture coordinates based on a height map and the viewing angle, so that high parts appear to cover low parts."],["advanced-lighting/parallax-mapping.html#the-idea","Parallax Mapping","The idea","Alongside the color and normal maps we need a height map , a grayscale texture describing the surface's relief. It is more convenient to store depth , the inverse of height: 0 at the top surface, 1 at the deepest point. Picture the viewer looking at a point A on the flat polygon. On the real, bumpy surface, the line of sight would have hit the relief earlier, at some point B, and the viewer should see the texture at B. Parallax mapping estimates how far to slide the texture coordinate from A toward B. All of this is done in tangent space , where the surface is the xy plane and the texture's u and v run along x and y. So the view direction must be converted into tangent space first."],["advanced-lighting/parallax-mapping.html#view-direction-in-tangent-space","Parallax Mapping","View direction in tangent space","The TBN matrix from the previous chapter converts tangent to world. Its transpose does the opposite, since the matrix is orthonormal:"],["advanced-lighting/parallax-mapping.html#simple-parallax-mapping","Parallax Mapping","Simple parallax mapping","Sample the depth at the current coordinate and move along the view direction by that amount: scale controls the strength; values around 0.05 to 0.1 are typical. The division by viewDir.z makes the shift larger at shallow angles. Then use the shifted coordinate for every texture lookup of the fragment: color, normal, specular. The discard trims the edges of a single quad, where shifted coordinates would leave the texture. On tiling surfaces you would leave it out. Sign conventions Whether the offset is subtracted or added, and whether its y needs negating, depends on the direction of your v axis and bitangent. If the effect seems to bulge inward or slide the wrong way when you move the camera, flip the sign of the y component. This one-sample version works for gentle relief. On steep height changes or at very low angles the estimate is poor and the surface seems to swim."],["advanced-lighting/parallax-mapping.html#steep-parallax-mapping","Parallax Mapping","Steep parallax mapping","A better estimate comes from marching along the view ray in small steps through a number of depth layers until the ray goes below the surface: Inside a loop the GPU cannot compute automatic mip levels reliably, so the samples name level 0 explicitly. The result is far more convincing, but the discrete layers show up as visible steps."],["advanced-lighting/parallax-mapping.html#parallax-occlusion-mapping","Parallax Mapping","Parallax occlusion mapping","The steps disappear if, instead of taking the first layer below the surface, we interpolate between the last point above the surface and the first one below it: This is parallax occlusion mapping (POM). The two depths tell us how far each point is from the true surface, and a linear blend lands very close to the real intersection."],["advanced-lighting/parallax-mapping.html#limits","Parallax Mapping","Limits","It is an illusion inside the polygon. The silhouette stays flat, and the effect breaks at glancing angles. It is expensive: dozens of texture reads per fragment. Reduce the layer count with distance. The relief casts no shadows on itself unless you march a second ray toward the light. When you need real geometry, with real silhouettes and shadows, displace vertices using tessellation ."],["advanced-lighting/parallax-mapping.html#exercises","Parallax Mapping","Exercises","Switch between the three variants with a uniform and compare at a low viewing angle. Implement self-shadowing by marching from the found point toward the light in tangent space."],["advanced-lighting/hdr.html","HDR","","Example project examples/Sources/15-HDRBloom , run with swift run HDRBloom . Download all examples or see how to run them . Color values written to an 8-bit texture are clamped to the 0 to 1 range. Put several bright lights in a scene and large areas add up to more than 1, get clamped, and turn into a featureless white patch. All the detail in the bright regions is lost. High dynamic range rendering lets colors exceed 1 while the image is being built, and converts the result to a displayable range at the very end, in a step called tone mapping ."],["advanced-lighting/hdr.html#floating-point-render-targets","HDR","Floating-point render targets","The scene is rendered into a texture with a floating-point format, which stores values far beyond 1 without clamping: Format Notes .rgba16Float The standard choice. 16-bit half floats per channel. .rg11b10Float Half the memory, no alpha, slightly less precision. Good on mobile. .rgba32Float Rarely needed for color. Set the scene pipelines' color format to match, and render the scene as in Render Targets . The lighting shaders do not change. They simply stop being clamped."],["advanced-lighting/hdr.html#tone-mapping","HDR","Tone mapping","A full-screen pass reads the HDR texture and maps it to the 0 to 1 range for the drawable. The simplest operator is Reinhard , which divides each color by itself plus one. Bright values are squeezed together toward 1 and dark values are left nearly untouched: The drawable should have an sRGB format, as set up in Gamma Correction , so the result is gamma-encoded automatically. Tone mapping always comes before gamma encoding."],["advanced-lighting/hdr.html#exposure","HDR","Exposure","A camera adapts to the brightness of a scene with its exposure. An exposure tone mapper gives us the same control: A low exposure reveals detail in bright areas and crushes the dark ones; a high exposure does the reverse. With it you can render a dim interior and a sunlit exterior with the same physical light values, changing only the exposure. Automatic exposure, or eye adaptation , computes the average brightness of the frame, for example from the smallest mip level of the HDR texture, and eases the exposure toward a target over time."],["advanced-lighting/hdr.html#filmic-curves","HDR","Filmic curves","Reinhard tends to look washed out. Film-like curves keep more contrast and roll highlights off more gracefully. A popular compact fit to the ACES film curve, by Krzysztof Narkowicz: The tone mapper is one of the biggest contributors to the overall look of a renderer. Try several."],["advanced-lighting/hdr.html#a-test-scene","HDR","A test scene","Build a long tunnel from a stretched, inward-facing cube with one very bright light at the far end, with a color around (200, 200, 200), and a few dim colored lights along the walls. Without HDR the far end is a white blob. With HDR and exposure control, you can see the texture of the wall right up to the light."],["advanced-lighting/hdr.html#why-it-matters-beyond-bright-lights","HDR","Why it matters beyond bright lights","Bloom needs to know which pixels are brighter than white. See the next chapter . Physically based rendering uses real-world light intensities, which span a huge range. Post effects such as depth of field and motion blur look far more natural when computed on HDR values."],["advanced-lighting/hdr.html#displaying-hdr-directly","HDR","Displaying HDR directly","Apple displays with Extended Dynamic Range (EDR) can show values brighter than standard white. A Metal layer opts in by using a floating-point drawable format ( .rgba16Float ), an extended linear color space, and wantsExtendedDynamicRangeContent = true on the CAMetalLayer . Values above 1.0 are then shown brighter than the UI's white, up to a headroom that the screen reports and that changes with brightness settings. You still tone map, but into the display's available range instead of 0 to 1. It is a worthwhile upgrade once the rest of the pipeline is in place."],["advanced-lighting/hdr.html#exercises","HDR","Exercises","Add keys to raise and lower the exposure. Show Reinhard on the left half of the screen and ACES on the right."],["advanced-lighting/bloom.html","Bloom","","Example project examples/Sources/15-HDRBloom , run with swift run HDRBloom . Download all examples or see how to run them . A monitor cannot show a light as blindingly bright as it would be in reality. What we can do is imitate what bright lights do in a camera or in the eye: their glow bleeds over the surrounding image. This effect is bloom , and it is one of the cheapest ways to make a scene feel luminous. The recipe has three steps: Extract the bright parts of the HDR scene into a separate texture. Blur that texture. Add the blurred result on top of the scene, then tone map."],["advanced-lighting/bloom.html#extracting-bright-areas","Bloom","Extracting bright areas","We could run a pass over the scene texture to pick out bright pixels. A neater way is to let the scene's fragment function write to two color attachments at once, which is called multiple render targets (MRT): A threshold of 1.0 only makes sense because the scene is rendered in HDR: only pixels brighter than display white glow. Give the render pass and the pipeline a second attachment: Every pipeline used in that pass must declare both attachments. A fragment function that should not write to one of them, such as the lamp shader, can leave it out of its output struct; with writeMask = [] on the attachment you can also disable writes explicitly."],["advanced-lighting/bloom.html#gaussian-blur","Bloom","Gaussian blur","A Gaussian blur averages each pixel with its neighbors, weighted by a bell curve. A two-dimensional kernel of 9 × 9 would cost 81 samples per pixel. The Gaussian is separable : blurring horizontally and then vertically gives the identical result with 9 + 9 samples."],["advanced-lighting/bloom.html#ping-pong","Bloom","Ping-pong","A pass cannot read from the texture it is writing to, so the blur alternates between two textures: A to B horizontally, B to A vertically, and again for a stronger blur. The load action is .dontCare because the full-screen triangle overwrites every pixel anyway. Skipping the load is free performance on a tile-based GPU."],["advanced-lighting/bloom.html#combining","Bloom","Combining","The final pass adds the blurred glow to the scene and then tone maps: Bloom is added before tone mapping, so the glow is compressed together with everything else."],["advanced-lighting/bloom.html#doing-it-better","Bloom","Doing it better","Ten full-resolution passes with a small kernel give a tight glow at a high cost. Modern bloom works on a mip chain instead: Downsample the bright texture repeatedly, halving the size each time, for five or six levels. Walk back up, upsampling each level with a small blur and adding it to the next larger one. Each level contributes a wider glow, the total cost is around one and a third full-resolution passes, and the result is a broad, smooth halo. This is the approach used in most current engines, and it also removes the need for a hard threshold: blending a small fraction of the blurred chain with the scene looks more natural. Two Metal-specific shortcuts are worth knowing: Metal Performance Shaders ships tuned image filters. MPSImageGaussianBlur(device:sigma:) blurs a texture with one call: blur.encode(commandBuffer:sourceTexture:destinationTexture:) . A blur can also run as a compute function , which avoids setting up render passes for image processing."],["advanced-lighting/bloom.html#exercises","Bloom","Exercises","Change the number of blur iterations and watch the glow widen. Run the blur at half resolution and compare the quality and the GPU time. Implement the downsample and upsample chain."],["advanced-lighting/deferred-shading.html","Deferred Shading","","Until now we have used forward rendering : every object is drawn and lit in one go, and every fragment loops over every light. Two things make that wasteful. Fragments that are later covered by something closer were lit for nothing, and the cost grows as objects × lights. Deferred shading splits rendering into two phases: Geometry pass : draw the scene once and store the inputs of lighting, such as position, normal and albedo, in a set of textures called the G-buffer . Lighting pass : for each pixel on screen, read the G-buffer and compute lighting once. Lighting cost becomes pixels × lights and no longer depends on scene complexity. Hundreds of lights become practical."],["advanced-lighting/deferred-shading.html#the-g-buffer","Deferred Shading","The G-buffer","Attachment Format Contents 0 .rgba8Unorm_srgb Albedo in rgb, specular strength in a 1 .rgba16Float World-space normal 2 .rgba16Float World-space position depth .depth32Float Depth Create each as a texture with [.renderTarget, .shaderRead] usage and attach all three to one render pass. The geometry pass fragment function fills them: No lighting happens here. The pass is cheap, and the depth test guarantees that only the nearest surface ends up in each pixel. Storing position costs a lot of memory. Production renderers reconstruct it from the depth buffer and the inverse view-projection matrix, and pack normals into two channels. Start with the simple layout and optimize later."],["advanced-lighting/deferred-shading.html#the-lighting-pass","Deferred Shading","The lighting pass","A full-screen triangle reads the G-buffer and lights every pixel: read fetches the exact texel for this pixel with no sampler. The radius check skips lights too far away to matter."],["advanced-lighting/deferred-shading.html#light-volumes","Deferred Shading","Light volumes","Looping over all lights for every pixel still wastes work, since most lights reach only a small part of the screen. The refinement is to draw a sphere for each light, sized to its radius, using instancing. Each sphere's fragment function lights only the pixels it covers and adds its result with additive blending ( .one , .one ). Cull front faces and disable depth writes, so the light still works when the camera is inside the sphere. With volumes, a thousand small lights cost little more than their combined screen area. Compute the radius from the attenuation: the distance at which the light's contribution falls below a small threshold such as 5/256."],["advanced-lighting/deferred-shading.html#mixing-in-forward-rendering","Deferred Shading","Mixing in forward rendering","Deferred shading cannot handle transparency, since each pixel stores one surface. The usual structure is: Geometry pass, then the lighting pass. A forward pass on top for transparent objects, lamps and the skybox. The forward pass must use the same depth buffer as the geometry pass, so that forward-rendered objects are hidden correctly behind deferred ones. Attach the G-buffer's depth texture to the forward pass with loadAction = .load ."],["advanced-lighting/deferred-shading.html#single-pass-deferred-on-apple-gpus","Deferred Shading","Single-pass deferred on Apple GPUs","The classic approach stores the whole G-buffer in memory and reads it back in a second pass. On a tile-based GPU that is expensive: every tile has to be written out and loaded again. Apple GPUs offer something better. A fragment function on Apple silicon can read the current contents of the color attachments it is drawing to. This is called programmable blending or framebuffer fetch. The entire deferred pipeline then fits in one render pass : Attachment 0 is the final lit color (the drawable or an HDR texture). Attachments 1 to 3 are the G-buffer, created with storageMode = .memoryless , loadAction = .clear and storeAction = .dontCare . First draw the geometry, writing attachments 1 to 3. Then, in the same encoder , draw the light volumes with a fragment function that takes the G-buffer as input: The G-buffer never leaves the GPU's tile memory. It consumes no system memory and no bandwidth, which makes deferred shading practical even on phones. On Intel Macs the feature is unavailable, so portable renderers keep the two-pass path as a fallback; check device.supportsFamily(.apple1) . Apple's \"Rendering a Scene with Deferred Lighting\" sample code shows both paths side by side. Apple GPUs also support tile shaders , compute-like functions that run once per tile in the middle of a render pass. They are used to build per-tile light lists for tiled forward rendering, an alternative that keeps the benefits of forward shading, including MSAA and transparency, with many lights."],["advanced-lighting/deferred-shading.html#trade-offs","Deferred Shading","Trade-offs","Forward Deferred Many lights Costly Cheap Transparency Works Needs a forward pass MSAA Works Difficult Material variety Any shader per object Limited by the G-buffer layout Memory Low High, unless memoryless"],["advanced-lighting/deferred-shading.html#exercises","Deferred Shading","Exercises","Display each G-buffer texture on screen to verify its contents. Scatter 200 small colored lights and compare the frame time with the forward renderer. Reconstruct position from depth and remove the position attachment."],["advanced-lighting/ssao.html","SSAO","","Our ambient term is a constant, so corners, creases and the gaps between objects are as bright as open surfaces. In reality these places receive less indirect light, because nearby geometry blocks part of it. The effect is called ambient occlusion , and adding it gives a scene depth and contact that flat ambient light lacks. Computing true occlusion requires tracing rays against the scene. Screen-space ambient occlusion (SSAO) approximates it using only what is already on screen: the depth buffer."],["advanced-lighting/ssao.html#how-it-works","SSAO","How it works","For each pixel, take a number of sample points in a small hemisphere above the surface, oriented along the normal. For each sample, check the G-buffer: is there geometry in front of the sample point? The fraction of samples that are blocked is the pixel's occlusion. SSAO fits naturally after a G-buffer pass. It needs positions and normals in view space , so either store them that way or transform them on read. Working in view space makes depth comparisons a matter of comparing z."],["advanced-lighting/ssao.html#the-sample-kernel","SSAO","The sample kernel","Generate 64 random points inside a unit hemisphere pointing along +z, in tangent space. Scale them so that more samples fall close to the origin, since nearby occluders matter most: Store the kernel in a buffer for the shader."],["advanced-lighting/ssao.html#random-rotation","SSAO","Random rotation","64 samples per pixel is already a lot, and still not enough to avoid visible banding. Rotating the kernel differently at each pixel turns the banding into fine noise, which a blur then removes. A tiny 4 × 4 texture of random vectors, tiled across the screen, supplies the rotations:"],["advanced-lighting/ssao.html#the-ssao-shader","SSAO","The SSAO shader","The output is a single-channel texture ( .r8Unorm ) holding the occlusion factor. Points worth noting: In our right-handed view space the camera looks down -z, so a larger z is closer to the camera. A sample is occluded when the scene's surface at that pixel is closer than the sample. The bias , around 0.025, prevents a flat surface from occluding itself. The range check stops a distant background object from darkening the edge of something in front of it. Reading pixel % 4 tiles the noise texture across the screen without a sampler. The projection to texture coordinates flips y, as it did for shadow maps. A radius of about 0.5 world units is a good starting point."],["advanced-lighting/ssao.html#blur","SSAO","Blur","The raw result has a visible 4 × 4 noise pattern. A box blur of the same size removes it exactly:"],["advanced-lighting/ssao.html#applying-it","SSAO","Applying it","Ambient occlusion affects ambient light only . In the deferred lighting pass: Multiplying the whole lit result by the occlusion is a common mistake. It darkens directly lit surfaces, which looks dirty."],["advanced-lighting/ssao.html#tuning-and-cost","SSAO","Tuning and cost","Render SSAO at half resolution . It is low-frequency information, and the blur hides the difference. 16 to 32 samples are often enough with a good blur. Raising the result to a power ( pow(ao, 2.0) ) increases contrast. SSAO only knows about visible surfaces, so occlusion can appear or vanish as objects enter and leave the screen. Refinements such as HBAO and GTAO improve accuracy, and ray tracing computes real ambient occlusion on hardware that supports it."],["advanced-lighting/ssao.html#exercises","SSAO","Exercises","Output the raw and blurred occlusion to the screen. Vary the radius and the sample count and find the cheapest setting that still looks good."],["pbr/theory.html","Theory","","Physically based rendering (PBR) is a family of techniques that model light in a way that is closer to physics than Phong or Blinn-Phong. The pay-off is practical: Materials look right under any lighting, without per-scene tweaking. Artists author materials with a few intuitive parameters that mean the same thing in every engine. A lighting model is usually called physically based when it meets three conditions: it is built on a microfacet model of surfaces, it conserves energy , and it uses a physically based BRDF . This chapter explains each, and the next one turns them into shader code."],["pbr/theory.html#microfacets","Theory","Microfacets","At a microscopic scale no surface is smooth. It is made of tiny perfect mirrors called microfacets , each pointing in its own direction. On a rough surface their orientations vary wildly, and incoming light scatters widely, producing broad, dim highlights. On a smooth surface they are nearly aligned, and reflections are sharp and bright. We cannot model individual microfacets, so we describe them statistically with one number, the roughness , from 0 (mirror) to 1 (completely rough). What matters to a highlight is the fraction of microfacets whose normal matches the halfway vector between the light and the viewer, the same vector we met in Blinn-Phong ."],["pbr/theory.html#energy-conservation","Theory","Energy conservation","Outgoing light can never exceed incoming light, apart from surfaces that emit light themselves. When light hits a surface it splits into two parts: The reflected part bounces off the surface directly. This is specular light. The refracted part enters the material, scatters inside, and is partly absorbed. What comes back out, colored by the material, is diffuse light. The two are mutually exclusive: light that was reflected cannot also be refracted. So if a fraction kS is reflected, at most 1 - kS is left for diffuse. Metals behave differently from non-metals ( dielectrics ). A metal absorbs all refracted light, so it has no diffuse color at all, only tinted specular reflection. This is the basis of the metalness parameter."],["pbr/theory.html#the-reflectance-equation","Theory","The reflectance equation","PBR is built on this equation: It reads more gently than it looks. The light Lo leaving point p toward the viewer wo is the sum, over every incoming direction wi in the hemisphere Ω above the surface, of: Li , the radiance arriving from that direction, which is the strength and color of the light, n · wi , the familiar cosine factor that weakens light arriving at a shallow angle, fr , the BRDF , which says how much of the light from wi is reflected toward wo for this material. For a handful of point lights the integral collapses into a simple sum over the lights, which is what the next chapter does. For light arriving from the whole environment we have to actually solve it, and that is the subject of the IBL chapters."],["pbr/theory.html#the-cook-torrance-brdf","Theory","The Cook-Torrance BRDF","The BRDF used by practically every real-time renderer has a diffuse and a specular part: The diffuse part is the Lambertian term, a constant: albedo / π . The specular part is with three functions, each approximating one aspect of microfacet behavior. D: normal distribution function How many microfacets are aligned with the halfway vector h ? The Trowbridge-Reitz GGX distribution, with α = roughness² : Low roughness concentrates the result into a small, intense spot. High roughness spreads it out. G: geometry function Microfacets shadow and mask one another, especially on rough surfaces at grazing angles. The Schlick-GGX approximation for one direction is where k = (roughness + 1)² / 8 for direct lighting and k = roughness² / 2 for image-based lighting. Smith's method combines the view and light directions: G = G1(view) · G1(light) . F: Fresnel equation Every surface becomes more reflective at grazing angles. Look straight down at a lake and you see the bottom; look across it and you see the sky. The Fresnel-Schlick approximation: F0 is the base reflectivity , the reflectance when looking straight at the surface. Dielectrics have a low, colorless F0 around 0.04. Metals have a high, colored one: Material F0 (linear RGB) Water 0.02, 0.02, 0.02 Plastic, glass 0.04, 0.04, 0.04 Iron 0.56, 0.57, 0.58 Copper 0.95, 0.64, 0.54 Gold 1.00, 0.71, 0.29 Aluminium 0.91, 0.92, 0.92 In the metalness workflow, F0 is computed by blending between 0.04 and the albedo color using the metalness value. The Fresnel term F is the reflected fraction kS . So kD = (1 − F)(1 − metalness) ."],["pbr/theory.html#material-parameters","Theory","Material parameters","A PBR material in the common metallic-roughness workflow consists of: Map Color space Meaning Albedo sRGB Base color, with no lighting or shadows baked in. For metals, the F0 color. Normal linear Tangent-space normal, as in normal mapping. Metallic linear 0 for dielectrics, 1 for metals. Rarely in between. Roughness linear Microfacet roughness. Ambient occlusion linear Pre-baked occlusion of crevices. This is the material model used by USD's preview surface and by glTF, so the assets Model I/O loads map directly onto it ( .baseColor , .metallic , .roughness , .tangentSpaceNormal , .ambientOcclusion ). Because PBR depends on correct light intensities, it requires the linear workflow and HDR pipeline from the previous section. Check that both are in place before continuing."],["pbr/lighting.html","Lighting","","Example project examples/Sources/16-PBR , run with swift run PBR . Download all examples or see how to run them . With the theory in hand, the PBR fragment shader is short. This chapter implements the Cook-Torrance BRDF for point lights, first with constant material values and then with textures."],["pbr/lighting.html#the-brdf-functions","Lighting","The BRDF functions","Each formula from the previous chapter becomes a small function: Squaring the roughness before use ( a = roughness² ) makes the parameter feel linear to artists. It is a convention adopted by nearly all engines."],["pbr/lighting.html#the-fragment-function","Lighting","The fragment function","Walking through the loop: Radiance is the light's color attenuated by the inverse square of the distance. A point light delivers all its energy from one direction, so the integral of the reflectance equation reduces to one evaluation per light. F0 blends from the dielectric value 0.04 to the albedo as the material becomes metallic. The specular term is D·G·F over the normalization factor. The small constant avoids a division by zero. kD removes the reflected fraction and removes diffuse entirely for metals. The result is multiplied by NdotL , the cosine factor. The output is linear HDR. It goes through the tone mapping pass from the HDR chapter and the sRGB drawable. Do not gamma correct in this shader. The fixed ambient term is a placeholder. Replacing it with real light from the environment is the job of the next two chapters."],["pbr/lighting.html#a-grid-of-spheres","Lighting","A grid of spheres","The standard test: a grid of spheres where metalness increases from bottom to top and roughness from left to right. Model I/O generates the sphere: Four lights with a color of (300, 300, 300) placed about 10 units in front of the grid light it well. Such large values are normal with inverse-square falloff. Even with a flat ambient term the result is striking: rough dielectrics look like chalk or rubber, smooth ones like plastic, and the metals have colored, tight highlights."],["pbr/lighting.html#textured-pbr","Lighting","Textured PBR","Replace the constants with texture lookups, and use the normal mapping function from Normal Mapping : Load the albedo map as sRGB and all the others as linear data. Many asset packs combine occlusion, roughness and metalness into the red, green and blue channels of a single \"ORM\" texture, which saves two texture reads. With Model I/O, extend the material loader from the Mesh chapter to read the .metallic , .roughness , .tangentSpaceNormal and .ambientOcclusion semantics, falling back to 1 × 1 textures holding the scalar values when a property has no texture."],["pbr/lighting.html#exercises","Lighting","Exercises","Give every sphere the same roughness and vary only the albedo to see how metals tint their highlights. Find a free PBR material set, such as rusted iron or worn plastic, and apply it to a sphere."],["pbr/diffuse-irradiance.html","Diffuse Irradiance","","Point lights are only part of the light in a scene. Most of what reaches a surface is ambient: the sky, the ground, a nearby wall. Image-based lighting (IBL) treats an image of the whole environment as one large light source, so that objects look like they belong in their surroundings. The reflectance equation splits neatly into a diffuse and a specular integral. This chapter handles the diffuse half."],["pbr/diffuse-irradiance.html#the-diffuse-integral","Diffuse Irradiance","The diffuse integral","For the Lambertian term the BRDF is a constant, so it moves out of the integral: The remaining integral depends only on the normal n . It is the total light arriving over the hemisphere around n , weighted by the cosine. We can precompute it for every possible normal and store the results in a cubemap, the irradiance map . At render time, ambient diffuse lighting is one texture lookup with the surface normal."],["pbr/diffuse-irradiance.html#hdr-environment-maps","Diffuse Irradiance","HDR environment maps","Environment images must be HDR, because the sun is thousands of times brighter than the sky around it. They are usually distributed as equirectangular images, a 2:1 panorama, in Radiance .hdr or OpenEXR format. Poly Haven has a large free collection. Image I/O decodes both formats. Draw the image into a floating-point bitmap and upload it:"],["pbr/diffuse-irradiance.html#from-panorama-to-cubemap","Diffuse Irradiance","From panorama to cubemap","A cubemap is easier to sample and to convolve than a panorama, so convert once at load time. Render a unit cube into each of the six faces of an .rgba16Float cube texture, using the left-handed face matrices from the Cubemaps chapter with the camera at the origin: Draw the cube with culling disabled, since the camera is inside it. Create the environment cube with mipmaps and call generateMipmaps(for:) with a blit encoder afterward; the next chapter needs them. A size of 512 or 1024 per face is plenty. You can now show the environment as a skybox, tone mapped along with everything else."],["pbr/diffuse-irradiance.html#convolving-the-irradiance-map","Diffuse Irradiance","Convolving the irradiance map","For each direction of the irradiance cubemap, we need the cosine-weighted average of the environment over the hemisphere around it. The integral is approximated by sampling the hemisphere on a regular grid of spherical coordinates: cos(theta) is the cosine factor of the integral, and sin(theta) compensates for the sample points bunching up toward the pole of the hemisphere. Sampling a lower mip level of the environment (level 3 here) suppresses the bright speckles that a tiny, intense sun would otherwise cause. Render this into a small cube with renderToCube . The irradiance map is very blurry by nature, so 32 × 32 per face is enough. It is computed once at load time."],["pbr/diffuse-irradiance.html#using-it","Diffuse Irradiance","Using it","In the PBR shader, replace the constant ambient term: Ambient light comes from all directions, so there is no single halfway vector for the Fresnel term. We use the angle between the normal and the view direction, and a variant of Fresnel-Schlick that takes roughness into account so rough surfaces do not get bright rims. The spheres now pick up the blue of the sky on top and the color of the ground below, and they finally sit in their environment. Metals look black in their unlit areas, because they have no diffuse term. They are waiting for specular reflections, which come next."],["pbr/diffuse-irradiance.html#alternatives","Diffuse Irradiance","Alternatives","Spherical harmonics represent the irradiance of an environment with just nine colors, since it varies so smoothly. Model I/O can compute them ( MDLLightProbe ), and evaluating them in the shader is cheaper than a cubemap lookup. MDLTexture.irradianceTextureCube(with:name:dimensions:) generates an irradiance cubemap from an environment cubemap for you. Writing the convolution once yourself is still the best way to understand what the map contains."],["pbr/specular-ibl.html","Specular IBL","","The diffuse half of image-based lighting depended only on the normal, so one small cubemap solved it. The specular half is harder: It depends on the normal, the view direction, the roughness and F0. Precomputing every combination is impossible. Epic Games' split-sum approximation breaks the integral into two parts that can be precomputed: A prefiltered environment map : the environment blurred by the right amount for each roughness level. A BRDF lookup table : a 2D texture that holds the rest of the BRDF's response."],["pbr/specular-ibl.html#part-1-the-prefiltered-environment-map","Specular IBL","Part 1: the prefiltered environment map","A rough surface reflects a wide cone of the environment, a smooth one a narrow cone. We store increasingly blurred versions of the environment in the mip levels of a cubemap: level 0 for roughness 0, the last level for roughness 1. At render time, the reflection vector and the roughness select the direction and the mip level. The approximation assumes the view direction equals the reflection direction equals the normal, since the view direction is unknown at precompute time. This costs some stretching of reflections at grazing angles and is otherwise hard to notice. Importance sampling Averaging the environment uniformly would need thousands of samples. Importance sampling generates sample directions concentrated where the GGX distribution is large, so a few dozen suffice. It needs well-distributed quasi-random numbers, for which the Hammersley sequence is the usual choice: The prefilter shader Rendering the mip chain Create a cube texture with mipmaps, around 128 or 256 per face, and render each mip level with a different roughness, using the attachment's level : Each mip level is half the size of the one before, and the viewport follows the attachment size automatically. Use a sampler with linear mip filtering, or the per-sample mip selection in the shader has no effect. Cubemap filtering in Metal is seamless across faces, so there are no visible seams at face edges in the blurred levels."],["pbr/specular-ibl.html#part-2-the-brdf-lookup-table","Specular IBL","Part 2: the BRDF lookup table","The second half of the split sum depends on only two numbers: n · v and the roughness. Its result, a scale and a bias to apply to F0, fits in the red and green channels of a 2D texture. The table is the same for every environment, so compute it once, or even ship it as an image. Render it with the full-screen triangle into a 512 × 512 .rg16Float texture. The result is a smooth red-to-yellow gradient."],["pbr/specular-ibl.html#putting-it-together","Specular IBL","Putting it together","Sample the lookup table with a clamping sampler. The texture coordinate for roughness must match how you rendered the table: here row 0, the top, is roughness 0. Now the metal spheres come alive. Smooth ones mirror the environment, rough ones show a soft, blurred version of it, and everything is tinted correctly by F0. Together with the direct lighting from point lights, this is a complete PBR pipeline."],["pbr/specular-ibl.html#beyond-a-single-probe","Specular IBL","Beyond a single probe","One environment map describes the light at one point. Inside a building, a sphere would reflect the sky through the walls. Engines place many reflection probes around a level, each with its own prefiltered map, and blend between the nearest ones. Screen-space reflections and, on capable hardware, ray traced reflections add what probes cannot capture: nearby, moving objects."],["pbr/specular-ibl.html#exercises","Specular IBL","Exercises","Display each mip level of the prefiltered map as a skybox. Precompute the three IBL textures once and cache them on disk. Load a full PBR model and place it in three different environments without changing its material."],["in-practice/debugging.html","Debugging","","Graphics bugs are different from ordinary bugs. There is no stack trace for a black screen, and you cannot print from a fragment function. The good news is that Metal's tooling is among the best of any graphics API, and all of it is built into Xcode. Learning these tools early will save you days."],["in-practice/debugging.html#layer-1-api-validation","Debugging","Layer 1: API validation","Metal API Validation checks every call you make: mismatched pixel formats, missing buffers, wrong load actions, encoders left open. When something is wrong, the app stops at the offending call with a message that usually names the exact property: It is enabled by default when you run from Xcode ( Edit Scheme → Run → Diagnostics ). Never turn it off during development. It is disabled automatically in release builds, so it costs your users nothing."],["in-practice/debugging.html#layer-2-shader-validation","Debugging","Layer 2: shader validation","In the same scheme panel, Shader Validation instruments your shaders to catch what API validation cannot see: reading past the end of a buffer, sampling a texture that was never bound, accessing an array out of bounds. Bad accesses are reported in the console with the function name and line. It slows rendering down, so enable it when a shader behaves strangely."],["in-practice/debugging.html#label-everything","Debugging","Label everything","Every Metal object has a label property. Labels appear in validation messages, in the debugger and in Instruments. A message about \"Scene HDR Color\" is a lot more useful than one about <AGXG14GFamilyTexture: 0x12f80a400> . Inside an encoder, group related commands so they show up as a collapsible tree in the debugger:"],["in-practice/debugging.html#the-gpu-frame-debugger","Debugging","The GPU frame debugger","While your app runs, click the camera icon (Capture GPU Workload) in Xcode's debug bar. Xcode freezes one frame and lets you take it apart: The navigator lists every command buffer, pass and draw call, organized by your debug groups. Step through draws and watch the frame being built up one call at a time. Attachments shows the color, depth and stencil textures as they are after the selected draw. The current draw's geometry is outlined. Bound resources shows every buffer, texture and sampler bound for the selected draw. You can open a buffer and view its bytes laid out as your struct. This is the fastest way to find a uniform layout mismatch. Geometry viewer shows the mesh after the vertex function, in clip space, with each vertex's outputs. When an object is missing, look here first: you will see at once whether it is behind the camera, clipped, or collapsed to a point. Shader debugger : select a pixel, click Debug , and step through the fragment function's execution for that exact pixel, with the value of every variable on every line. The same works for a vertex. It is the closest thing to a breakpoint inside a shader. Shader editing : change shader code in the debugger and reload it to see the effect on the captured frame without rebuilding the app. Dependency viewer draws a graph of all passes and the textures flowing between them, with load and store actions. It makes redundant passes and unnecessary stores obvious. Performance and memory : per-pass and per-draw GPU time, and a list of every resource with its size. You can also capture from code, to grab one specific frame: Programmatic capture needs the MetalCaptureEnabled key set to YES in Info.plist."],["in-practice/debugging.html#the-performance-hud","Debugging","The performance HUD","Metal can overlay a live graph of frame rate, frame time, GPU time and memory on any Metal app. Enable it in the scheme's options ( Show Graphics HUD ), or with the environment variable MTL_HUD_ENABLED=1 . Keep it on while you work so that a sudden slowdown is noticed when it is introduced."],["in-practice/debugging.html#debugging-by-color","Debugging","Debugging by color","The oldest technique still works. When a value is in doubt, output it as a color: Remap values outside the 0 to 1 range first, or you only see black and white. Magenta is the traditional \"this should never happen\" color: return float4(1, 0, 1, 1); inside a branch tells you instantly whether it is taken."],["in-practice/debugging.html#command-buffer-errors","Debugging","Command buffer errors","A command buffer can fail on the GPU, for example by running out of memory or timing out. Check in the completion handler: For more detail, create the buffer with MTLCommandBufferDescriptor and errorOptions = .encoderExecutionStatus . The error then reports which encoder, by label, was executing when the failure happened."],["in-practice/debugging.html#instruments","Debugging","Instruments","For performance questions that span many frames, such as stutters, CPU-GPU synchronization, and shader compilation hitches, profile with the Metal System Trace and Game Performance templates in Instruments. They show the CPU encoding, the GPU execution and the display's presentation of each frame on one timeline."],["in-practice/debugging.html#a-checklist-for-nothing-is-drawn","Debugging","A checklist for \"nothing is drawn\"","Is there a validation message in the console? Read it. Capture a frame. Is the draw call there? If not, your code never issued it. Open the geometry viewer. Are the vertices inside the clip volume? Check the depth attachment and compare function. Is everything failing the depth test? Check culling and winding. Try setCullMode(.none) . Look at the bound buffers. Are the matrices sensible, or is the layout off? Return a solid color from the fragment function. If that appears, the problem is in the shading. Is the alpha zero with blending enabled? Work from the outside in and the cause is usually found in minutes."],["in-practice/text-rendering.html","Text Rendering","","Sooner or later you want to draw text: a frame counter, a score, a label above an object. Metal has no text support. It draws triangles. Text is therefore rendered as textured quads, one per character, with a texture that contains the shapes of the characters."],["in-practice/text-rendering.html#options","Text Rendering","Options","Let the system do it. For UI that sits on top of your view, put a SwiftUI or UIKit label over the MTKView . It is free, sharp, localized and accessible. For debug overlays and menus this is usually the right answer. Render strings to textures. Draw a whole string with Core Text into a bitmap and show it on a quad. Good for text that rarely changes. A glyph atlas. Draw every character once into one texture, then compose any string from quads. This is the classic approach for text inside the 3D scene or text that changes every frame, and it is what this chapter builds."],["in-practice/text-rendering.html#glyphs-and-metrics","Text Rendering","Glyphs and metrics","A font is a collection of glyphs , the shapes of characters. To place them correctly, we need a few measurements for each glyph, all relative to the baseline on which the text sits: the bounding box : the rectangle the shape occupies, which can extend below the baseline for letters like g and p, the advance : how far to move the pen to the right before drawing the next glyph. Core Text provides both, and can draw glyphs into a Core Graphics bitmap."],["in-practice/text-rendering.html#building-the-atlas","Text Rendering","Building the atlas","We draw the printable ASCII characters into a single-channel bitmap, packing them in rows, and record where each one ended up. The bitmap's memory starts with the top row of the image, which matches Metal's top-left texture origin. Only the glyph positions, which Core Graphics measures from the bottom, need converting."],["in-practice/text-rendering.html#building-quads-for-a-string","Text Rendering","Building quads for a string","For each character, look up its glyph, emit two triangles, and advance the pen: Positions are in pixels with y up, and origin is the start of the baseline."],["in-practice/text-rendering.html#the-shaders","Text Rendering","The shaders","Text is drawn with an orthographic projection that maps pixel coordinates straight to the screen: The atlas stores only coverage. The color comes from a uniform, so one atlas serves text of every color. The pipeline needs alpha blending enabled, as in the Blending chapter, and text should be drawn last with depth testing off."],["in-practice/text-rendering.html#drawing","Text Rendering","Drawing","Allocate textBuffer once, large enough for the longest text you expect, and rewrite it each frame."],["in-practice/text-rendering.html#limits-of-this-approach","Text Rendering","Limits of this approach","Scaling blurs. A bitmap atlas is sharp at the size it was rendered. Render the atlas at the pixel size you will display, remembering that Retina displays have two or three pixels per point. Only simple scripts. Mapping one character to one glyph ignores kerning, ligatures, combining marks, right-to-left text and complex scripts. For correct layout, let Core Text shape the string ( CTLineCreateWithAttributedString , then read its glyph runs) and draw the glyphs and positions it returns from your atlas. ASCII only. A dynamic atlas that adds glyphs on demand removes that limit."],["in-practice/text-rendering.html#signed-distance-fields","Text Rendering","Signed distance fields","For text that must stay sharp at any size, especially in 3D, store a signed distance field in the atlas instead of coverage: each texel holds the distance to the nearest glyph edge. Bilinear filtering interpolates distances faithfully, so the edge can be reconstructed crisply at any magnification: Outlines, glows and drop shadows become one extra smoothstep each. Multi-channel distance fields (MSDF) additionally preserve sharp corners, and are the technique most engines use today."],["in-practice/2d-game.html","A 2D Game","","Everything so far has been technique. This chapter puts a handful of those techniques to work in something playable: a small brick-breaking game. A paddle, a ball, a wall of bricks. It is deliberately tiny, so that the whole structure fits in one chapter and you can see how a renderer and game logic meet."],["in-practice/2d-game.html#what-we-need","A 2D Game","What we need","A sprite renderer that draws many colored, textured rectangles in one draw call. An orthographic camera in pixel-like units. A game loop with delta time and input. Collision detection between a circle and rectangles."],["in-practice/2d-game.html#the-sprite-renderer","A 2D Game","The sprite renderer","A sprite is a quad with a position, size, color and texture region. We use instancing : one unit quad generated in the vertex function, and one instance per sprite. On the Swift side the renderer collects sprites during the frame and draws them all at once: Use a pipeline with alpha blending. Put all the game's images in one texture, an atlas , and select regions with uvRect , so that the whole game is a single draw call. For solid-colored rectangles, bind a 1 × 1 white texture."],["in-practice/2d-game.html#the-playing-field","A 2D Game","The playing field","Give the game a fixed logical size, independent of the window, with the origin at the top left and y pointing down, which is convenient for 2D: Swapping bottom and top flips the y axis."],["in-practice/2d-game.html#game-objects","A 2D Game","Game objects","A level is just data. Loading levels from a text file where each digit is a brick type is an easy extension."],["in-practice/2d-game.html#update","A 2D Game","Update","Steering the ball by where it meets the paddle is what makes the game controllable. A purely physical reflection would feel lifeless."],["in-practice/2d-game.html#circle-against-rectangle","A 2D Game","Circle against rectangle","Find the point of the rectangle closest to the circle's center by clamping. If that point is within one radius, they overlap. The direction from the closest point to the center tells us which side was hit: At high speeds a ball can pass through a thin brick between two frames. Running the update in several small sub-steps per frame is the simplest protection."],["in-practice/2d-game.html#render","A 2D Game","Render","The renderer knows nothing about the rules, and the game knows nothing about Metal. Keeping that separation is the most valuable habit in this chapter."],["in-practice/2d-game.html#making-it-feel-good","A 2D Game","Making it feel good","The game works. What makes it fun is polish, and each item below reuses a technique from this series: Particles : an additive-blended trail behind the ball, drawn with the same sprite batch. Screen shake and flashes : render the game to a texture and distort it in a post-processing pass for a few frames after a hit. Glow : bloom on bright bricks. Score and lives : the atlas text from Text Rendering . Power-ups : sprites that fall from destroyed bricks and change the paddle size or ball speed when caught. Sound : AVAudioEngine or AVAudioPlayer for hit effects."],["in-practice/2d-game.html#where-to-go-from-here","A 2D Game","Where to go from here","Add a state machine for menu, playing, won and lost. Add levels. Then try a different game with the same sprite batch: the renderer you wrote here is enough for a great many 2D games."],["in-practice/game-engine.html","A Game Engine","","Every chapter so far built one technique in isolation: a shadow map here, a bloom chain there, each in its own small program. This chapter and the next three put them together. The result is the LearnMetal Engine : a small game engine made entirely with Metal, written twice, once in Swift and once in C++, with a complete little game on top and an editor to build scenes with. It is deliberately small. The engine is between five and six thousand lines in either language, comments included, and it is meant to be read. Nothing in it is a technique you have not met in these pages; what is new is how the pieces are arranged so that they work together. Get the engine on GitHub Download it as a .zip"],["in-practice/game-engine.html#get-it-and-run-it","A Game Engine","Get it and run it","The engine lives in a repository of its own. You need macOS 13 or later and Xcode 15 or later. Open Package.swift in Xcode instead if you want the debugger and the GPU tools. The C++ version also needs CMake 3.24 or later. The first configuration downloads the four libraries it uses: GLFW, metal-cpp, stb and miniaudio. That starts Marble , the sample game. Roll with W A S D, jump with Space, look around with the arrow keys or by dragging the mouse. Collect the eight gems and reach the glowing disc. A gamepad works too. Press F1 for statistics and F2 to see the physics shapes."],["in-practice/game-engine.html#what-an-engine-adds","A Game Engine","What an engine adds","The example programs of this site each have a renderer class that knows exactly what it draws: \"a cube and a floor\", \"nine spheres\". That stops working the moment the list of things to draw is decided while the program runs. An engine is what you get when you separate what exists from how it is drawn : A scene holds the things that exist: entities with a position, a mesh, a light, a physics body. A renderer draws whatever is in the scene, without knowing what game it is. Systems change the scene over time: physics, particles, the game's own logic. A loop runs all of them in a fixed order, every frame. Around those sit the services a game takes for granted: loading files, input, sound, a way to draw an interface."],["in-practice/game-engine.html#the-shape-of-a-game","A Game Engine","The shape of a game","A game is an object with a few functions that the engine calls at the right moments. Here is a complete one: a cube that turns, lit by a sun, with a shadow. There is no render pass in it, no pipeline state, no buffer. The game says what exists; everything from the earlier chapters happens inside engine.run . Notice how the camera and the sun are aimed. Both look down their own negative z axis, like the camera of the Camera chapter, so giving them a rotation is giving them a direction."],["in-practice/game-engine.html#the-frame","A Game Engine","The frame","Engine owns the loop. Every frame it does the same things in the same order: After this the renderer draws the scene, and then the 2D canvas on top of it. The order is not arbitrary. Physics runs before the game's update , so game logic sees where things ended up. World matrices are computed after everything that can move an entity, and before the two systems that need them: particles and the renderer. Entities that were destroyed during the frame are only removed at fixed points, so that no system ever holds a reference to something that vanished halfway through."],["in-practice/game-engine.html#a-fixed-step-for-physics","A Game Engine","A fixed step for physics","Rendering takes a different amount of time every frame. If physics advanced by that same varying amount, a ball would bounce a little differently on every machine, and a slow frame could push it through a floor. So physics always advances by exactly one sixtieth of a second, and the engine decides how many such steps fit in the time that has passed: Real time is poured into the accumulator and taken out in fixed portions. On a 120 Hz display, every other frame runs no step at all; after a hiccup, one frame runs several, up to a limit. What is left in the accumulator is a fraction of a step, alpha . The renderer does not show bodies at their latest physics position, but at a blend between the previous one and the latest one, alpha of the way along. That is what makes motion smooth even though physics only knows sixty moments per second. One consequence is worth remembering: a function that runs \"zero, one or several times per frame\" is the wrong place to ask whether a key was just pressed. Read input in update and remember it; apply forces in fixedUpdate ."],["in-practice/game-engine.html#one-source-of-truth-for-the-gpu","A Game Engine","One source of truth for the GPU","Every chapter with a uniform buffer had the same warning: the struct in the shader and the struct in the host code must match byte for byte, and nothing checks it. In an engine with a dozen such structs and two host languages, hoping is not enough. The engine declares each struct once, in a C header that all three compilers read. Inside a shader the simd names are made to mean the shader's own types. Shortened to one struct, the header looks like this: The shaders include it withinclude . The host code brings it in like this: The binding slots live in the same header, so [[buffer(LMBufferInstances)]] in a shader and the index passed to the encoder are the same constant, not two numbers that happen to agree. Two rules keep the header safe. No bool , whose size differs between languages. And remember that a float3 occupies sixteen bytes in all three languages: the unit tests check the size of every struct, so a mistake fails a test and not a frame."],["in-practice/game-engine.html#where-each-chapter-went","A Game Engine","Where each chapter went","Chapter In the engine Shaders Rendering/ShaderLibrary , which compiles the .metal files at startup and reloads them when they change Textures Rendering/Texture , Assets/Assets Transformations , Camera Math/Math , Scene/Scene Multiple Lights Shaders/Lighting.metal Model Assets/MeshData , with its own reader for OBJ files Blending , Face Culling transparent and cut-out materials in Rendering/RenderPasses Render Targets , Anti-Aliasing the HDR, multisampled main pass Cubemaps Rendering/Environment , Shaders/Sky.metal Instancing automatic, in Renderer.makeBatches Shadow Mapping Rendering/Cascades : four shadow maps instead of one Normal Mapping tangents in Assets/MeshData , Shaders/Forward.metal HDR , Bloom , SSAO Shaders/Post.metal PBR , Diffuse Irradiance , Specular IBL Shaders/Lighting.metal , Shaders/IBL.metal Debugging labels on every pass, Rendering/DebugDraw Text Rendering , A 2D Game Rendering/Canvas , with distance-field fonts Tile-Based GPUs memoryless targets, load and store actions chosen per pass Not everything made it. There are no stencil outlines , no deferred path , no shadows from point lights , no parallax mapping , and nothing from the Beyond section. A small engine that does a few things well is a better place to add those than a large one that does everything halfway. The next chapter opens the renderer."],["in-practice/engine-renderer.html","Engine: The Renderer","","The previous chapter showed a game that never mentions a render pass. This one opens the part of the LearnMetal Engine that does all the mentioning: the renderer. It draws whatever is in the scene, in six steps, and every step is a chapter you have already read."],["in-practice/engine-renderer.html#from-a-scene-to-draw-calls","Engine: The Renderer","From a scene to draw calls","A scene is a collection of entities; the GPU wants a list of draw calls. The renderer starts every frame by walking the scene once and copying out what it needs, a step often called extraction . It finds the camera and computes its matrices. It collects the lights. And for every entity that has a mesh, it records one item: the mesh, the material, the world matrix, and the bounding box of the mesh in world space. Then it throws most of the work away before doing it. Items whose bounding box lies entirely outside the camera's view are dropped ( frustum culling ). The opaque ones that are left are sorted so that items with the same material and the same mesh sit next to each other, and each such run becomes a single draw call: This is the Instancing chapter, applied automatically. The game never asks for it: forty identical floor tiles are forty entities, and one draw call. A lone object is simply a batch of one, so the shaders have a single code path: Transparent items are the exception. They are sorted from the farthest to the nearest instead, because blending depends on the order."],["in-practice/engine-renderer.html#memory-that-lasts-one-frame","Engine: The Renderer","Memory that lasts one frame","Instance matrices, camera uniforms, the vertices of the interface, particles: a great deal of data is rebuilt every frame and is useless afterwards. Creating a Metal buffer for each piece would be slow, and reusing one buffer is dangerous, because the GPU is usually still drawing the previous frame when the CPU starts filling the next. The engine uses one large buffer per frame and hands out pieces of it in order. Allocating is adding to an offset: There are three of these buffers, used in turn, and a semaphore that starts at three. A frame takes one unit before it touches anything and gives it back when the GPU reports that the frame is finished: By the time a buffer comes around again, the frame that used it is guaranteed to be done."],["in-practice/engine-renderer.html#pipelines-on-demand","Engine: The Renderer","Pipelines on demand","A render pipeline state fixes the vertex and fragment functions, the pixel formats, the sample count and the blending. The combinations multiply quickly: the same mesh shader is used with a color target, without one for shadows, with and without blending. Listing every combination by hand is the kind of code that is always one entry short. Instead, each draw describes what it needs, and a cache creates the pipeline the first time that description appears: The first frame creates the pipelines the scene needs and is a little slower; after that the cache answers from a table. The cache has a second use. The engine compiles its shaders from the .metal files when it starts, and every thirty frames it checks whether one of them has changed on disk. If so it compiles them again and empties the cache. Save a shader while the game is running and the picture changes; if the new version has an error, the message is logged and the old shaders stay."],["in-practice/engine-renderer.html#the-passes","Engine: The Renderer","The passes","Pass Draws into 1 Shadow maps one slice of a depth texture array per cascade 2 Depth pre-pass depth and view-space normals 3 Ambient occlusion, then a blur a one-channel texture 4 Scene a multisampled HDR texture 5 Bloom a chain of half-size textures 6 Tone mapping and interface the drawable"],["in-practice/engine-renderer.html#shadows-in-cascades","Engine: The Renderer","Shadows in cascades","The Shadow Mapping chapter used one shadow map for a small scene. Stretch one map over everything a camera can see and its texels become far too large to draw a sharp edge. The usual answer is to cut the view distance into slices and give each slice its own map: small and sharp near the camera, large and soft far away. These are cascades , and the engine uses four. Fitting a map to a slice has one subtlety. The obvious fit, a box around the slice's corners, changes shape whenever the camera turns, and the shadow edges crawl. A sphere around the slice does not care which way the camera faces. Snapping its center to whole texels removes the remaining shimmer when the camera moves: In the fragment function, a pixel picks its cascade by its distance from the camera, and looks the shadow map up from a point pushed slightly off the surface: Moving the lookup point along the normal, instead of adding a large bias to the depth, is what keeps shadows attached to the objects that cast them. Nine comparison samples around uv then soften the edge, as in the original chapter. Materials with holes need one more thing. A fence drawn with a cut-out texture must cast a shadow with holes in it, so for those materials the shadow pass runs a tiny fragment function whose only job is to discard_fragment() where the texture is transparent."],["in-practice/engine-renderer.html#ambient-occlusion","Engine: The Renderer","Ambient occlusion","SSAO needs the depth and the normal of every pixel before the scene is shaded. In a deferred renderer those are already there. In a forward renderer they have to be drawn first, so pass 2 draws all opaque geometry into a depth texture and a normal texture, and pass 3 turns them into an occlusion value per pixel and blurs it. The main pass then reads that texture by pixel position. The price is one more trip through the opaque geometry. It can be switched off with one setting, and then passes 2 and 3 do not run at all."],["in-practice/engine-renderer.html#the-main-pass","Engine: The Renderer","The main pass","The scene is drawn in HDR ( rgba16Float ), with four samples per pixel: Neither the multisampled color texture nor the depth texture is needed after the pass. On Apple GPUs the engine creates both as memoryless : they exist only in the GPU's tile memory while the pass runs, as the Tile-Based GPUs chapter explains, and a 4x multisampled HDR target that would have cost tens of megabytes costs nothing. Inside the pass the order matters: Opaque objects , writing depth. The sky , a single triangle placed exactly on the far plane and drawn with a \"less or equal\" depth test. Only the pixels nothing else covered run its fragment function. Drawing it first would shade the whole screen and then cover most of it. Transparent objects , far to near, testing depth but not writing it. Particles and debug lines . The fragment function is the one from the PBR chapters, with a loop over the lights. This is forward rendering: one function computes a pixel completely. It fits Apple GPUs well, because they find the visible surface of each pixel before running any fragment function, so hidden surfaces cost nothing; and it works with multisampling and with transparency without special cases."],["in-practice/engine-renderer.html#light-from-the-sky","Engine: The Renderer","Light from the sky","The sky is not a texture. It is a few lines of shader code: a gradient from the horizon to the zenith, the ground below, a glow around the sun. When the engine starts, it renders that sky into a cubemap, one face at a time, and then filters the cubemap into the maps that image-based lighting needs: Each call is a small render pass into one face and one mip level of a cubemap. Because the lighting is computed from the same function that draws the visible sky, reflections and ambient light always agree with what the player sees. Change the sky's colors or turn the sun, and the engine notices and runs these passes again."],["in-practice/engine-renderer.html#bloom-and-the-final-pass","Engine: The Renderer","Bloom and the final pass","Bloom keeps the parts of the image brighter than a threshold, and blurs them through a chain of textures, each half the size of the one before: down the chain, then back up, adding each level to the larger one. Wide and narrow glows pile up, for the price of a few very small passes. The last pass adds the bloom to the scene, applies exposure and the tone mapping curve, and writes to the drawable, whose sRGB format takes care of gamma . The 2D interface is drawn in the same pass, after tone mapping: the orange of a title should be the orange the designer picked, not what a filmic curve makes of it."],["in-practice/engine-renderer.html#seeing-what-it-does","Engine: The Renderer","Seeing what it does","Every pass has a label and the main pass is divided into debug groups, so a frame captured in Xcode's GPU debugger reads like the table above. That costs nothing and saves hours, as the Debugging chapter argued. For everything else there is engine.debug , which draws lines in 3D for one frame: a line, a box, a sphere, the axes of a transform. F2 uses it to show every collider in the scene. The next chapter leaves the renderer and builds the game."],["in-practice/engine-gameplay.html","Engine: The Game","","The renderer draws whatever is in the scene. This chapter is about what puts things there and makes them move: entities, physics, input, sound, an interface. The example is Marble , the game that comes with the LearnMetal Engine. All of it, level included, is about eight hundred lines, and nearly every line is a call into something this chapter explains."],["in-practice/engine-gameplay.html#entities-and-components","Engine: The Game","Entities and components","An entity is nothing but an id. Everything that makes it a marble, a lamp or a wall is in its components : plain structs, stored by the scene. The engine brings the ones it has systems for: Transform , Camera , Light , MeshRenderer , RigidBody , Collider , ParticleEmitter . A game adds its own, and the engine does not need to be told about them: There is no Gem class with a draw method and an update method. A gem is an entity that is given five components, and each one makes a different part of the engine take notice of it: The renderer sees a mesh and a light. Physics sees a trigger. Only the game sees a Gem . Behavior, then, is a loop over everything that has the right components. This one is the entire animation of the gems: Inside the scene, the components of one type sit back to back in one array, so a loop like this reads memory in order. The Lifetime component works the same way: a loop counts it down and destroys the entity at zero. Destroying is always safe inside a loop, because the entity is only really removed at the end of the step, as the first chapter showed. One Swift detail matters everywhere in game code. Components are values. scene[entity, RigidBody.self] gives you a copy, so either change it in place through the subscript, as in scene[cube, Transform.self]?.rotation = ... , or take a copy, change it and store it back. Inside each , the closure's parameters are inout : what you change there is stored. One C++ detail matters everywhere in game code. scene.get<RigidBody>(entity) returns a pointer into the scene's storage, or nullptr . The pointer stays valid until a component of the same type is added or removed, so use it and let it go; do not keep it in a member variable."],["in-practice/engine-gameplay.html#bodies-colliders-and-triggers","Engine: The Game","Bodies, colliders and triggers","A Collider gives an entity a shape: a sphere, a box or a capsule. By itself it is a static obstacle, which is all a floor or a wall needs. Add a RigidBody and the entity moves. Marble's level is made of boxes with colliders; the ramp is simply a box that is tilted. The physics is of the kind most games want, sometimes called arcade physics: gravity, bouncing, friction, things that push each other and stack. It leaves out the hard parts on purpose. Bodies do not spin when they are hit, there are no joints, and a very fast object can pass through a thin one. When a game needs those, a dedicated physics library is the right tool. That first omission would look wrong on a marble: a ball that slides without turning. So the game turns it, by exactly the angle a ball of that size rolls through when it travels that far: Nobody watching can tell that the rotation is decoration. A great deal of game programming is this: simulate what matters, fake the rest convincingly. A collider marked as a trigger blocks nothing. It only reports what enters it. That is how the game knows a gem was touched or the goal was reached: The second event fires when two solid things start touching, and says how fast they met. The game uses it for one thing: a thump whose volume depends on how hard the marble landed."],["in-practice/engine-gameplay.html#input-read-once-use-later","Engine: The Game","Input: read once, use later","The game never asks whether the W key is down. It defines actions once, and from then on asks about those: Keyboard and gamepad feed the same action, so supporting both costs nothing more. The first chapter warned that the fixed step is the wrong place to read input, because it runs zero, one or several times per frame. So update reads, and remembers: A jump is not remembered as \"yes\" but as \"yes, for the next 0.15 seconds\". Press the button a moment before the marble lands and it still jumps on landing. Players never notice this; they only notice games that do not do it. fixedUpdate then turns what was remembered into motion: \"Forward\" means away from the camera, so the controls stay natural while the camera orbits. In the air the marble can still be steered, but only a little. And look at the isDemo branch: the autopilot produces the same two things a player produces, a direction and a jump. Because the game reads its input from two variables and not from the keyboard, replacing the player with a program took a handful of lines."],["in-practice/engine-gameplay.html#a-platform-that-carries-its-passenger","Engine: The Game","A platform that carries its passenger","Halfway through the course a platform ferries the marble across a gap. It is a kinematic body: it moves, but nothing can push it. The tempting way to move it is to write a new position every step. The marble would not come along: to the physics the platform would be teleporting, and a body that teleports has no velocity to pass on through friction. A kinematic body has to be moved the way the physics understands, by its velocity: Work out where the platform should be one step from now, and set the velocity that gets it there in exactly one step. The platform follows its path precisely, and the marble on top is carried by friction like any object resting on a moving one."],["in-practice/engine-gameplay.html#sound-without-sound-files","Engine: The Game","Sound without sound files","Marble has six sound effects and not one audio file. Each is computed when the game starts, from a waveform, a pitch that may slide, and a duration: The engine can load sound files too. But a handful of numbers is enough for the sounds of a small game, and it keeps the repository free of files with unclear licenses. Playing a clip hands it to the mixer . The operating system asks for audio in small blocks, many times per second, on a thread of its own. Each time, the mixer walks its list of playing sounds, reads the next piece of each clip, scales it by its volume and pan, and adds everything together. That is all a software mixer is. The speed at which a clip is read is its pitch , and it can change while the sound plays. Two effects in the game are built on that. Every gem plays the same chime a little higher than the one before, so collecting them sounds like climbing. And the rumble of the rolling marble is one second of noise, looped, played at about a tenth of its speed: The sound never starts or stops. It plays all the time, and is simply silent while the marble stands still or flies."],["in-practice/engine-gameplay.html#the-interface","Engine: The Game","The interface","The counter, the clock and the messages are drawn with the engine's 2D canvas , which grew out of the Text Rendering and A 2D Game chapters. It works in immediate mode: there are no label objects to create and keep up to date. Every frame, draw says what should be on screen: Positions are in points from the top left corner, and colors are the sRGB values a color picker shows. The canvas turns the calls into triangles, and the renderer draws them at the end of the frame in as few draw calls as the textures allow. The text comes from a distance-field font atlas, which is why size can be any number. When a gem is collected, collectPulse jumps to one and fades, and the counter swells from 22 to 30 points and back without ever looking blurred."],["in-practice/engine-gameplay.html#a-game-that-plays-itself","Engine: The Game","A game that plays itself","Start the game with --demo and nobody needs to touch it. An autopilot rolls the marble through every gem, waits for the ferry, rides it, jumps for the gem above it, and finishes at the goal. It is a list of waypoints and one steering rule. The rule reads what a player would see, where the marble is and where the ferry is, and returns what a player would do: A demo is pleasant to watch, but that is not why the autopilot exists. Combined with one more option it becomes a test of the whole engine: With --capture the engine opens no window and no sound device. It renders into a texture, advances time by exactly one sixtieth of a second per frame however long the frame really took, and after the given number of frames saves the last one as a PNG. The run is the same every time. The program's exit code then says whether the autopilot made it: If the physics lets the marble fall through the ferry, if a trigger stops firing, if a platform of the level is moved out of reach, this command fails. The repository's CI workflow runs it on every push, for both languages, and attaches the picture."],["in-practice/engine-gameplay.html#tests-that-do-not-need-a-gpu","Engine: The Game","Tests that do not need a GPU","Most of an engine is not graphics, and everything that is not graphics can be tested without a GPU: the math, the scene, input, mesh generation, collisions and physics, the fitting of the shadow cascades, the layout of the shared structs, sound synthesis and the mixer. The engine keeps those parts free of Metal so that they can be. Run them with swift test . Run them with ctest --test-dir build -L unit . Tests like this one are what made it possible to change the physics without fear while Marble's level was being designed around it."],["in-practice/engine-gameplay.html#what-to-build-next","Engine: The Game","What to build next","The best way to understand an engine is to make it do something it does not do yet. Some ideas, roughly from easy to hard: A jump pad. A trigger that sets the marble's upward velocity. Everything you need is in this chapter. A second level. buildLevel is a list of boxes; write another one and switch to it when the goal is reached. A lamp on the ferry. Make a point light a child of the moving platform, and watch Transform.parent at work. Sounds in space. engine.playSoundAt takes a position and sets volume and pan from where the camera is. Use it for the thump of the crates. Shadows for a spot light. One more shadow map, with a perspective projection, next to the cascades. A file format for levels. Read the boxes from a text file, then make the game reload it when it changes, the way the shaders already do. Each of these touches one part of the engine and leaves the rest alone. That, more than any single technique, is what the arrangement described in these chapters is for. The last idea on the list has been taken further than a text file: the next chapter is about the engine's scene editor."],["in-practice/engine-editor.html","Engine: The Editor","","The previous chapters built Marble's level in code: a function with a line per box. That is fine for one small level and miserable for ten. This chapter adds what every engine eventually grows: an editor , a program in which scenes are put together by looking at them. The editor is not a separate technology. It is a game made with the engine, about twelve hundred lines, whose gameplay happens to be editing. Three new pieces of the engine make it possible, and games can use all three: a user interface, scene files, and a way to look at a scene without being part of it. Hold the right mouse button and use W A S D to fly. Click to select, press W, E or R and drag a colored handle to move, rotate or scale. Drag a number sideways to change it, or click it and type. F5 plays the scene."],["in-practice/engine-editor.html#an-interface-in-immediate-mode","Engine: The Editor","An interface in immediate mode","An editor is mostly panels full of numbers. The usual way to build those is a tree of widget objects that must be kept in step with the data they show. The engine takes the other road, the one made popular by Dear ImGui: immediate mode . There are no widget objects. Every frame, the program calls one function per control, and that call both draws the control and reports what the user did with it. This is the entire inspector section for a light: Read it as a description of the panel, top to bottom. An if around a call is how a control appears only when it makes sense: a range for point and spot lights, cone angles for spot lights alone. No code hides or shows anything; the rows that are not called are simply not there this frame. A control is small. This is a checkbox, complete: It asks for the next row of the panel, tests whether the mouse went down on its square, flips the value if so, and draws itself with the engine's canvas . The value lives in the caller's data and nowhere else, so there is nothing to synchronize. The little that must survive from one frame to the next is kept by the Gui under an id computed from the panel's title and the control's label: which control the mouse is holding, which field has the keyboard, how far each panel is scrolled. Dragging a number is the control remembering that it is \"active\" until the button comes up, and adding the pointer's movement to the value in every frame in between. Two details are worth knowing before you write your own controls. Rows scrolled entirely out of a panel are skipped, drawing nothing and reacting to nothing; a row that is half out is cut at the panel's edge, because the canvas can be told to clip and the renderer turns that into a scissor rectangle. And a pop-up list is drawn at the very end of the frame so that it lands on top of everything; its choice reaches the control that opened it one frame later. Nothing in the interface is an image. A rounded field is a polygon; an icon is a few lines and arcs given to the same canvas, so it is sharp at any size. Edges are smooth without multisampling: every shape gets a border one pixel wide that fades to transparent, made of ordinary triangles with their outer vertices at zero alpha."],["in-practice/engine-editor.html#scenes-as-text","Engine: The Editor","Scenes as text","What the editor saves is a text file with one statement per line: Materials come first, then entities, each followed by one line per component. A mesh line names the mesh and gives the position of its material in the file; textures and models are stored by name, never as data. Lines a reader does not recognize are skipped, so an older build can open a newer file. Text was chosen over a binary format for one reason: you will keep scenes in git. A line per component means that moving one crate changes one line, and the diff says exactly that. To make this hold, numbers are written in the shortest form that reads back to the very same float ( 0.22 , not 0.2199999988 ), and opening a scene and saving it again changes nothing in the file. A game loads a scene with one call: What a scene file holds is entities and the engine's own components. Game logic stays in code: Marble's gems and its ferry are not something the editor knows about. A game typically loads a scene and then finds the entities it cares about by name."],["in-practice/engine-editor.html#looking-without-being-there","Engine: The Editor","Looking without being there","The renderer draws from the first active camera it finds, and a scene being edited usually has one already: the game's. The editor needs a view of its own that does not disturb it, and that never ends up in the saved file. The camera is a normal entity, because the renderer only knows how to look through entities. Two things set it apart. EditorOnly is an empty component that the scene writer checks: entities carrying it are left out of the file. And cameraOverride tells the renderer to look through this entity whatever the scene's own cameras say. The same trick keeps physics out of the way. engine.isSimulationPaused makes the engine skip its physics steps, so a crate placed in mid-air stays there until you press Play."],["in-practice/engine-editor.html#picking-and-the-gizmo","Engine: The Editor","Picking and the gizmo","Clicking on something is the ray from the camera that the engine already provides, tested against every entity: The test is against the mesh's bounding box, which is a box aligned with the mesh's own axes. Instead of rotating the box into the world, the ray is brought into the mesh's space with the inverse of the world matrix, where the test is the simple one. Lights and cameras have no mesh, so they are picked as small spheres, and drawn as small discs. The handles on a selected entity are 2D lines: each axis is projected to the screen and drawn with the canvas, which keeps them the same size however far away the entity is. Dragging one measures how far the pointer travelled along that line on screen , and turns it into a change of the transform: For a move, the travel in points is divided by how many points one meter of that axis covers on screen, so the entity stays under the pointer. Rotation and scale use the same travel as an angle and as a factor."],["in-practice/engine-editor.html#undo-for-free","Engine: The Editor","Undo for free","Undo is usually the feature that forces a design on an editor: every operation becomes a command object that knows how to revert itself. Having scene files gives a much cheaper way. After every finished edit, write the scene to text, and if it differs from last time, keep the old text: Undo is then \"read the previous text back\". It costs a few kilobytes per step for a scene of this size, and it cannot get out of step with the editor, because there is no per-operation code that could be forgotten. The check for a busy interface or a held handle is what makes a whole drag one step instead of sixty. Play is the same idea used once: Press Play and the scene is saved to a string, physics is switched on and the scene's own camera takes over. Press Stop and the string is read back: every crate returns to where it was."],["in-practice/engine-editor.html#what-it-does-not-do","Engine: The Editor","What it does not do","The editor selects one thing at a time, has no drag and drop, and uses Ctrl where a Mac user expects Command. It edits the engine's components only; there is no scripting. It is also the best argument for the way the engine is put together. The editor needed almost nothing new from the renderer, the scene or the physics: two flags and a name on each asset. Everything else was already there, because a game needed it first."],["beyond/compute-shaders.html","Compute Shaders","","Example project examples/Sources/17-ComputeParticles , run with swift run ComputeParticles . Download all examples or see how to run them . A GPU is a massively parallel computer that happens to be good at drawing triangles. Compute functions , called kernels in Metal, let you use that power for anything: image processing, physics, particle systems, simulations, culling, machine learning. They run outside the render pipeline, with no vertices or fragments, only data in and data out. In Metal, compute is not an add-on. It uses the same device, command buffers, buffers, textures and shading language as rendering, so results flow between the two with no copying."],["beyond/compute-shaders.html#a-first-kernel-image-processing","Compute Shaders","A first kernel: image processing","A kernel is a function marked kernel that returns void . It is executed once for every point in a grid that you define. For an image, the grid is the pixels: Textures declare how they are accessed: access::read , access::write or access::read_write . A texture that a kernel writes to must be created with .shaderWrite usage. [[thread_position_in_grid]] is the coordinate of this invocation, here the pixel. There is no sampler: read and write address exact texels. You can still pass a sampler and call sample with normalized coordinates when you want filtering."],["beyond/compute-shaders.html#running-it","Compute Shaders","Running it","A compute encoder lives in a command buffer next to render encoders. A typical frame might run a compute pass to update particles, then a render pass that draws them, in one command buffer."],["beyond/compute-shaders.html#threads-and-threadgroups","Compute Shaders","Threads and threadgroups","The GPU executes the grid in batches called threadgroups . Threads in one group run together and can share a small amount of fast memory. dispatchThreads takes the total grid size and a threadgroup size, and Metal handles grids that are not an exact multiple of the group size. It requires non-uniform threadgroup support, which every Apple silicon device has. The older dispatchThreadgroups takes a number of groups instead, so the grid is rounded up and the kernel must check for out-of-range coordinates itself: The sizes used above, threadExecutionWidth wide and as tall as the maximum allows, are a good default for 2D work."],["beyond/compute-shaders.html#a-particle-system","Compute Shaders","A particle system","Buffers work in kernels just as textures do. Here every particle is moved by one thread: The buffer is in the device address space because the kernel writes to it. The grid is one-dimensional: Then bind the same buffer to an instanced billboard vertex function like the one in Life Without Geometry Shaders , with its Particle struct changed to match this one, and draw. The particle data never leaves the GPU, and a million particles animate comfortably. Data races Threads run in parallel in no guaranteed order. Each thread must write only to its own element. If a thread reads data that another thread writes, as in a blur that reads neighbors, use separate input and output resources and swap them after each step. For shared counters, MSL provides atomic types such as atomic_uint ."],["beyond/compute-shaders.html#where-compute-is-used-in-a-renderer","Compute Shaders","Where compute is used in a renderer","Post-processing : blur, bloom, tone mapping, depth of field. A kernel avoids the full-screen triangle and can read and write any pixel. Culling : test object bounds against the view frustum and write a list of visible instances or an indirect command buffer. Light assignment : build per-tile or per-cluster light lists for forward rendering with many lights. Precomputation : the irradiance and prefiltered maps of the IBL chapters can be generated by kernels writing directly to cube faces. Simulation : cloth, fluids, flocking. Mesh generation : terrain, marching cubes, skinning."],["beyond/compute-shaders.html#compute-or-fragment","Compute Shaders","Compute or fragment?","A full-screen fragment function and a kernel can often do the same job. Fragment functions benefit from the tile-based architecture when they write to render pass attachments, especially memoryless ones. Kernels are more flexible: arbitrary reads and writes, several outputs of different sizes, shared threadgroup memory. On Apple GPUs, prefer a fragment function when the result goes straight into the current render pass, and a kernel otherwise. Measure when it matters."],["beyond/compute-shaders.html#ready-made-kernels","Compute Shaders","Ready-made kernels","Metal Performance Shaders (MPS) is a library of tuned kernels: Gaussian blur, convolution, histogram, image scaling, matrix multiplication, and more. For standard image operations, use it before writing your own:"],["beyond/compute-shaders.html#exercises","Compute Shaders","Exercises","Write a kernel that inverts an image and display the result. Port the box blur from the SSAO chapter to a kernel. Add an attractor point to the particle simulation that pulls particles toward the mouse."],["beyond/tessellation.html","Tessellation","","Detailed surfaces need many triangles, but only where the viewer can see the detail. A mountain a kilometer away does not need the triangle density of the ground under your feet. Tessellation lets the GPU subdivide coarse geometry on the fly, as finely as you ask, so a mesh can be stored and animated at low resolution and rendered at high resolution."],["beyond/tessellation.html#patches","Tessellation","Patches","With tessellation, you no longer submit triangles. You submit patches : triangles or quads defined by control points . For each patch you provide tessellation factors that say how finely to subdivide it. The GPU's tessellator generates the new vertices, and a special vertex function computes the final position of each. Metal's design differs from other APIs. There are no separate hull and domain shader stages. Instead: A compute kernel , or the CPU, writes tessellation factors into a buffer. The tessellator subdivides each patch according to its factors. A post-tessellation vertex function runs once for every generated vertex."],["beyond/tessellation.html#tessellation-factors","Tessellation","Tessellation factors","A quad patch has four edge factors and two inside factors . A triangle patch has three and one. A factor of 1 means no subdivision; 16 means the edge is split into 16 segments. Edge factors are separate from inside factors so that two neighboring patches can agree on their shared edge and leave no cracks, even when their interiors are subdivided differently. A kernel that sets the same factor for every patch: MTLQuadTessellationFactorsHalf is a built-in struct of six half floats, 12 bytes per patch: In a real renderer, the kernel computes each edge's factor from its distance to the camera or its length on screen. That is adaptive tessellation . Compute a factor per edge from that edge's own midpoint, so both patches sharing the edge get the same value."],["beyond/tessellation.html#the-pipeline","Tessellation","The pipeline","Control points go in an ordinary vertex buffer. The vertex descriptor says that the data advances per control point: The partition mode controls how edges are split. .integer and .pow2 snap to whole numbers, which causes visible popping as factors change. The fractional modes add new vertices gradually, so detail grows smoothly as the camera approaches. The maximum factor is 16 on older iOS devices and 64 on Macs and recent Apple GPUs."],["beyond/tessellation.html#the-post-tessellation-vertex-function","Tessellation","The post-tessellation vertex function","[[patch(quad, 4)]] declares a quad patch with four control points. [[position_in_patch]] is where the generated vertex lies within the patch: a float2 from 0 to 1 for quads, and a float3 of barycentric weights for triangles. Vertex functions cannot compute mip levels automatically, so texture samples name the level. The function decides what the patch means . Here it is a flat quad displaced by a height map. Interpret sixteen control points as a bicubic Bézier patch and you get smooth curved surfaces from a coarse cage."],["beyond/tessellation.html#drawing","Tessellation","Drawing","Textures and samplers for the vertex stage have their own binding calls, setVertexTexture and setVertexSamplerState ."],["beyond/tessellation.html#normals-for-displaced-surfaces","Tessellation","Normals for displaced surfaces","Displacement changes the surface, so the original normals are wrong. For a height map, compute the normal from neighboring heights, in the vertex function or per fragment:"],["beyond/tessellation.html#uses","Tessellation","Uses","Terrain with continuous level of detail. Displacement mapping : real geometric detail with correct silhouettes and shadows, where parallax mapping only fakes it. Smooth surfaces : subdivision surfaces and Bézier patches for characters and vehicles. On recent hardware, mesh shaders offer another route to the same goals with more control. Tessellation remains the simplest way to get adaptive detail and is supported on every Apple GPU."],["beyond/tessellation.html#exercises","Tessellation","Exercises","Animate the tessellation level and watch the wireframe refine. Compute per-edge factors from the camera distance in the kernel. Compare .integer and .fractionalEven while moving the camera."],["beyond/ray-tracing.html","Ray Tracing","","Rasterization answers one question very quickly: which triangle covers this pixel? Everything else, including shadows, reflections and ambient occlusion, has been an approximation layered on top: shadow maps, environment maps, screen-space tricks. Each breaks down somewhere. Ray tracing answers a more general question: what does a ray starting here and going that way hit first? With that one operation, shadows, reflections and occlusion become exact and almost trivial to express. Metal has a ray tracing API that runs on all Apple silicon, with hardware acceleration on recent chips. This chapter is an introduction. The goal is to understand the pieces and trace your first rays, then add ray traced shadows to the rasterized renderer you already have."],["beyond/ray-tracing.html#acceleration-structures","Ray Tracing","Acceleration structures","Testing a ray against every triangle in a scene is hopeless. An acceleration structure organizes the triangles in a tree of bounding boxes, so a ray can skip almost all of them. Metal builds and traverses this structure for you. There are two levels: A primitive acceleration structure holds the triangles of one mesh. An instance acceleration structure holds many instances of primitive structures, each with its own transform. Moving an object means updating a matrix and refitting the top level, not rebuilding the mesh. Check for support first:"],["beyond/ray-tracing.html#building-a-primitive-structure","Ray Tracing","Building a primitive structure","Describe the geometry using the vertex and index buffers you already have: Building happens on the GPU, through a dedicated encoder: Build static geometry once at load time. The same encoder can compact a structure to save memory and refit one after vertices move."],["beyond/ray-tracing.html#intersecting-rays-in-a-shader","Ray Tracing","Intersecting rays in a shader","Ray tracing in Metal is not a separate pipeline. Any shader, whether a kernel or a fragment function, can receive an acceleration structure and shoot rays into it. The intersection result tells you the distance to the hit, which primitive and which geometry were hit, and the barycentric coordinates of the hit point within the triangle. With the primitive index you look up the triangle's vertices in your own buffers and interpolate normals and texture coordinates. Metal can also store small per-primitive data inside the structure to make that lookup cheaper. Run this kernel, show the output texture with the full-screen triangle, and you have a ray traced image of your mesh with no rasterization involved."],["beyond/ray-tracing.html#hybrid-rendering-ray-traced-shadows","Ray Tracing","Hybrid rendering: ray traced shadows","A full path tracer is a project of its own. The most practical use of ray tracing is hybrid : rasterize the scene as before, and use rays only for the effects that rasterization does badly. Shadows are the easiest win. In the lighting fragment function, shoot one ray from the surface toward the light. If it hits anything, the point is in shadow: Compare with Shadow Mapping and Point Shadows : no extra passes, no shadow textures, no bias tuning, no resolution limit, and it works for any type of light. accept_any_intersection(true) lets traversal stop at the first hit, which makes shadow rays cheap. The same pattern gives: Reflections : trace along reflect(-V, N) and shade the hit point. Ambient occlusion : trace a few short rays in the hemisphere above the surface and count the hits. Real occlusion, replacing SSAO . Soft shadows : aim rays at random points on an area light and average over frames."],["beyond/ray-tracing.html#noise-and-denoising","Ray Tracing","Noise and denoising","One ray per pixel gives hard shadows and mirror reflections. Soft effects need many rays in random directions, and a real-time budget allows only a few per pixel. The result is noisy. The standard remedy is to accumulate results over several frames and to denoise . Metal Performance Shaders includes MPSSVGFDenoiser , and MetalFX offers denoised upscaling on recent systems."],["beyond/ray-tracing.html#going-further","Ray Tracing","Going further","Instance acceleration structures for scenes with many moving objects ( MTLInstanceAccelerationStructureDescriptor ). Intersection functions for custom primitives such as spheres and curves, and for alpha-tested geometry like foliage. Path tracing : follow rays through several bounces to compute global illumination. Apple's sample code \"Accelerating ray tracing using Metal\" is a compact, readable path tracer and the natural next step."],["beyond/ray-tracing.html#the-end-and-the-beginning","Ray Tracing","The end, and the beginning","This is the last chapter. You started with an empty view and now have the vocabulary to read any Metal sample, engine source or graphics paper. Pick something you want to see on screen and build it. Read Apple's sample code, watch the Metal sessions from WWDC, and keep the frame debugger open. There is always one more technique to learn, and you now have the foundation to learn it."],["beyond/mesh-shaders.html","Mesh Shaders","","A vertex function receives one vertex and returns one vertex. It cannot create geometry, and it cannot throw a whole object away. A mesh shader pipeline replaces the vertex stage with two programmable stages that can do both, entirely on the GPU. It is Metal's answer to the geometry shaders of other APIs, and since Metal 4 it is also the recommended replacement for tessellation."],["beyond/mesh-shaders.html#two-new-stages","Mesh Shaders","Two new stages","The object stage runs once per object, or per cluster of triangles. It decides whether that object is visible, how much detail it deserves, and how many meshes to launch for it. It can pass a small block of data, the payload , to the next stage. This stage is optional. The mesh stage runs once per mesh and writes the vertices, the indices and the number of primitives of a small mesh. The rasterizer then takes over as usual, and the fragment function does not change. Both stages run like compute kernels: you dispatch a grid of threadgroups, and each thread knows its own index. A small mesh of at most a few hundred triangles is called a meshlet . Splitting a large model into meshlets ahead of time, and culling them in the object stage, is the main use of this pipeline: triangles that cannot be seen never reach the rasterizer."],["beyond/mesh-shaders.html#the-mesh-function","Mesh Shaders","The mesh function","The output type is a mesh template: the vertex type, the per-primitive type, the maximum number of vertices and primitives, and the topology. set_primitive_count is what makes the stage flexible: write 0 and the mesh produces nothing. The fragment function receives the interpolated vertex data and, if it asks for it, the per-primitive data."],["beyond/mesh-shaders.html#the-pipeline-and-the-draw-call","Mesh Shaders","The pipeline and the draw call","The pipeline uses its own descriptor type. There is no vertex descriptor, because there are no vertex buffers: the mesh function reads whatever buffers it likes. Buffers are bound per stage with setMeshBuffer and setObjectBuffer , and the draw call dispatches threadgroups instead of counting vertices. Without an object function, the grid you pass is the grid of mesh threadgroups."],["beyond/mesh-shaders.html#adding-the-object-stage","Mesh Shaders","Adding the object stage","With an object function, the grid in the draw call is the grid of object threadgroups, and each of them decides how many mesh threadgroups to launch. The mesh function receives the payload with const object_data Payload &payload [[payload]] . On the pipeline descriptor set objectFunction , payloadMemoryLength (the size of the payload struct) and maxTotalThreadgroupsPerMeshGrid (here 64)."],["beyond/mesh-shaders.html#when-to-use-them","Mesh Shaders","When to use them","Meshlet culling. Per-cluster frustum, back-face and occlusion culling for dense models. Procedural geometry. Grass, hair, particles as quads, terrain patches: geometry that would otherwise need a compute pass and an intermediate buffer. Level of detail on the GPU , chosen per cluster and per frame. Replacing geometry shaders when porting from other APIs, and tessellation in Metal 4. Mesh shaders need Metal 3: Apple silicon Macs, and iPhones and iPads with an A14 chip or later. Check device.supportsFamily(.metal3) . For an ordinary model drawn once, the vertex pipeline is simpler and just as fast."],["beyond/mesh-shaders.html#exercises","Mesh Shaders","Exercises","Change the number of quads by changing only the grid size in the draw call. Make the mesh function return zero primitives for every other quad, like a checkerboard. Add the object stage and color each cluster by its level of detail."],["apple-platforms/tile-based-gpus.html","Tile-Based GPUs","","Every Apple GPU, from the iPhone to the Mac Studio, is a tile-based deferred renderer (TBDR). It draws a frame in a different order than the immediate-mode desktop GPUs most tutorials assume, and several Metal features exist only because of that. Knowing how it works turns a list of odd-looking rules into common sense."],["apple-platforms/tile-based-gpus.html#how-a-tile-based-gpu-draws-a-pass","Tile-Based GPUs","How a tile-based GPU draws a pass","An immediate-mode GPU takes each triangle as it arrives and shades it straight into the framebuffer in memory. An Apple GPU works in two phases per render pass. Tiling. It runs the vertex functions of every draw call in the pass, splits the screen into small tiles (for example 32 × 32 pixels), and records which triangles touch each tile. Rendering. For each tile it loads the tile's pixels into a small, very fast memory inside the GPU, the tile memory ; finds which triangle is visible at each pixel; shades only those; and writes the finished tile back to the texture. Two consequences follow, and they explain the rest of this chapter. Hidden surface removal. For opaque geometry the GPU knows the visible triangle of each pixel before it runs any fragment function. Hidden fragments cost nothing, whatever the draw order. Reading and writing textures is the expensive part. Work inside tile memory is cheap. Moving a tile between tile memory and system memory costs bandwidth, power and time. Coming from OpenGL On a desktop GPU you sort opaque objects front to back to save fragment work. On Apple GPUs hidden surface removal does that for you, as long as the pipeline is opaque. What you manage instead is passes: how many there are, and what each one loads and stores."],["apple-platforms/tile-based-gpus.html#load-and-store-actions","Tile-Based GPUs","Load and store actions","Each attachment of a pass has a load action, applied when a tile starts, and a store action, applied when it ends. .load copies the old contents from memory into every tile. Use it only when you draw on top of an earlier result. .clear fills the tile with a value, with no memory traffic. .dontCare does nothing at all. .store writes the tile back. .dontCare discards it. The rule: load only what you need to keep, store only what you will read later. A depth texture used by a single pass should be .clear and .dontCare . A multisampled color texture should be .multisampleResolve , without storing the samples."],["apple-platforms/tile-based-gpus.html#memoryless-textures","Tile-Based GPUs","Memoryless textures","If an attachment is never loaded and never stored, it only ever lives in tile memory, so it does not need memory of its own. Give it the memoryless storage mode and Metal allocates nothing for it. At 4K, a depth texture and a 4× multisampled color texture add up to hundreds of megabytes saved. With MetalKit, MTKView already does this for its own depth and multisample textures on Apple silicon."],["apple-platforms/tile-based-gpus.html#do-more-in-one-pass","Tile-Based GPUs","Do more in one pass","Starting a new pass means storing the tiles of the old one and loading them again. The deferred renderer of the Deferred Shading chapter does exactly that: one pass stores three G-buffer textures, a second pass samples them. On a tile-based GPU both steps can run in one pass, with the G-buffer never leaving tile memory. The tool is programmable blending : a fragment function may read the current value of the color attachments it is drawing to, by taking them as parameters. The G-buffer attachments become memoryless, with .clear and .dontCare ; only the lit color is stored. The depth is written to an extra r32Float color attachment, because the depth attachment itself cannot be read this way. The cost of deferred shading drops to little more than that of forward rendering, which is why this pattern is the standard on iPhone. Programmable blending also gives you custom blend equations and order-independent transparency. For per-pixel data that does not fit a pixel format, image blocks and tile shaders let you define your own layout of tile memory and run a compute-like function on each tile in the middle of a render pass."],["apple-platforms/tile-based-gpus.html#keep-hidden-surface-removal-working","Tile-Based GPUs","Keep hidden surface removal working","Hidden surface removal can only skip fragments it knows to be covered by opaque ones. Three things defeat it. Blending. A blended fragment needs what is behind it. discard_fragment and alpha testing: the GPU cannot know the pixel is covered until the function has run. Writing depth from the fragment function , or reading the attachment with programmable blending. So draw in this order: opaque first, then alpha-tested , then translucent . Sorting opaque objects is not needed. Keep discard_fragment out of shaders that do not need it, since its presence alone, even if never executed, moves the draw out of the opaque group."],["apple-platforms/tile-based-gpus.html#other-habits-that-matter","Tile-Based GPUs","Other habits that matter","Do not split a pass. Ending an encoder and starting another with the same attachments forces a store and a load. Encode everything that targets the same textures in one encoder. Avoid reading your own render target with a texture sample in the same frame when programmable blending would do. Compress textures with ASTC and generate mipmaps; bandwidth, not arithmetic, is usually the limit on mobile. Use smaller formats. rgba16Float costs twice the bandwidth of rg11b10Float and is rarely needed for HDR color. Use half precision ( half , half3 ) in fragment functions for colors and normals. On Apple GPUs it is faster and uses fewer registers. Measure. The Metal debugger in Xcode shows the load and store actions of every pass, and the memory report flags textures that could be memoryless. Metal System Trace in Instruments shows the tiling and rendering time of each pass separately."],["apple-platforms/tile-based-gpus.html#exercises","Tile-Based GPUs","Exercises","Find a depth texture in one of your renderers that could be memoryless, and change it. Set the store action of the color attachment to .dontCare and explain what you see. Add an unused discard_fragment behind an always-false uniform condition and compare the fragment time of a scene with heavy overdraw."],["apple-platforms/metalfx.html","MetalFX Upscaling","","The cost of a frame grows with the number of pixels. MetalFX lets you render the scene at a lower resolution and produces a full-resolution image that looks close to one rendered natively. A game that renders 1280 × 720 and upscales to 2560 × 1440 shades a quarter of the pixels. MetalFX offers two upscalers, and since 2025 a denoising upscaler for ray tracing and a frame interpolator. Effect Needs Use it when Spatial scaler The color image You want the simplest integration, or have no motion vectors Temporal scaler Color, depth, motion vectors, camera jitter You want the best quality, and anti-aliasing with it Temporal denoised scaler The above plus normals, albedo, roughness The image is ray traced with few rays per pixel Frame interpolator Two frames, depth, motion vectors You want a higher frame rate than you can render The scalers are available from macOS 13 and iOS 16 on devices with Apple silicon. The denoised scaler and the frame interpolator need the 2025 systems (macOS 26, iOS 26)."],["apple-platforms/metalfx.html#where-it-goes-in-the-frame","MetalFX Upscaling","Where it goes in the frame","Upscaling replaces nothing in your renderer. It is one more step, and its place matters. Render the scene at low resolution into an HDR texture. Upscale to the output resolution. Post-process at full resolution: bloom, tone mapping. Draw the user interface at full resolution, on top. Text and interface elements must never go through the upscaler: draw them afterwards, at native resolution."],["apple-platforms/metalfx.html#the-spatial-scaler","MetalFX Upscaling","The spatial scaler","It works on a single image, with no history. Create it once, and again whenever a size changes. Each frame, hand it the textures and encode it into the command buffer, between your passes. It is not part of a render pass. The output texture must be created with the usage the scaler asks for in outputTextureUsage , and with storageMode = .private ."],["apple-platforms/metalfx.html#the-temporal-scaler","MetalFX Upscaling","The temporal scaler","The temporal scaler gathers detail from previous frames. Each frame you shift the whole image by a fraction of a pixel, the jitter , so that over several frames every pixel is sampled at many positions. The scaler lines up the old frames with the new one using motion vectors , and blends them. The result has more real detail than any single frame, and it is anti-aliased, so MSAA becomes unnecessary. It needs three inputs beyond the color. Depth , from the same low-resolution pass. Motion vectors : for each pixel, how far the surface moved on screen since the last frame. Render them as an extra color attachment ( rg16Float ). The jitter offset you applied to the projection this frame. Jitter Use a low-discrepancy sequence, such as Halton with bases 2 and 3, and move the projection by that sub-pixel amount. Whether the sign of each term is right depends on your projection convention. If the image shimmers or blurs when the camera is still, flip one sign at a time: with the correct signs a static scene converges to a sharp image in a few frames. Motion vectors In the vertex function compute the clip-space position of the vertex in this frame and in the previous one, both without jitter, and pass them on. The fragment function turns them into a uv-space difference. Every moving object needs its previous model matrix, and the camera its previous view-projection matrix. Encoding Set isDepthReversed if you use reverse-Z. Set reset whenever the history is useless, or ghosts of the old scene will linger."],["apple-platforms/metalfx.html#getting-the-quality-right","MetalFX Upscaling","Getting the quality right","Texture detail. A low-resolution render picks blurrier mip levels. Give your samplers a negative LOD bias of log2(renderWidth / outputWidth) , for example -1 when upscaling 2×, so textures keep their full-resolution sharpness. Ghosting behind moving objects means wrong or missing motion vectors. Check animated, skinned and instanced objects first. Transparent and particle effects have no motion vectors of their own. Mark them in the scaler's reactive mask so it trusts the new frame more there. Exposure. The scaler works in linear HDR and needs to know roughly how bright the image will be shown. Either enable isAutoExposureEnabled on the descriptor, or supply your own exposure texture. Dynamic resolution. Create the scaler for the largest input size and set inputContentWidth and inputContentHeight each frame to the region actually rendered."],["apple-platforms/metalfx.html#exercises","MetalFX Upscaling","Exercises","Add the spatial scaler to the render-target example: render at half size and compare with the .linear stretch. Visualize the motion texture while moving the camera. With the temporal scaler running, stop sending the jitter offset and describe what happens."],["apple-platforms/metal-4.html","Metal 4","","Metal 4, introduced in 2025, is the first large revision of Metal's core API. Nothing you have learned is obsolete: shaders, pipelines, textures, render passes and the frame structure are the same ideas. What changes is who is responsible for what. Metal 4 does less behind your back, so a large renderer spends less CPU time and memory, and so that engines written for Direct3D 12 or Vulkan map onto it directly. It runs on Apple silicon: Macs with M1 or later and iPhones and iPads with A14 or later, on the 2025 systems (macOS 26, iOS 26) and newer. Check at runtime, and keep the classic path as a fallback. Read this as a map This chapter explains the model and shows the shape of the code. The names follow Apple's documentation at the time of writing, and this part of Metal is young. Start real work from Apple's sample Drawing a triangle with Metal 4 ."],["apple-platforms/metal-4.html#side-by-side-with-the-metal-you-know","Metal 4","Side by side with the Metal you know","The new types carry an MTL4 prefix and live next to the old ones. The device, textures, buffers, libraries and render pipeline states are shared. Metal Metal 4 MTLCommandQueue MTL4CommandQueue MTLCommandBuffer , created by the queue, used once MTL4CommandBuffer , created by the device, reused memory managed by Metal MTL4CommandAllocator MTLRenderCommandEncoder MTL4RenderCommandEncoder compute, blit and acceleration structure encoders MTL4ComputeCommandEncoder setVertexBuffer , setFragmentTexture , ... MTL4ArgumentTable automatic retain and hazard tracking residency sets and barriers device.makeRenderPipelineState MTL4Compiler You can adopt it gradually: one part of a renderer can submit to an MTL4CommandQueue while the rest keeps its MTLCommandQueue , with events to order the work between them."],["apple-platforms/metal-4.html#command-buffers-and-allocators","Metal 4","Command buffers and allocators","A Metal 4 command buffer is a long-lived object. You begin it with an allocator , which supplies the memory the encoded commands are written into, end it, and commit it to a queue. Then you begin it again. Keep one allocator per frame in flight and reset it when you come back to it. The pass descriptor is an MTL4RenderPassDescriptor ; MTKView offers one through currentMTL4RenderPassDescriptor . Because committing takes an array, several threads can each encode into their own command buffer, with their own allocator, and the buffers are submitted together. A render pass can even be suspended at the end of one encoder and resumed in the next, which replaces the parallel render encoder."],["apple-platforms/metal-4.html#argument-tables","Metal 4","Argument tables","In classic Metal every encoder keeps a table of bindings for every stage, and you fill it with setVertexBuffer , setFragmentTexture and their relatives. In Metal 4 the table is an object of its own. You size it for what your shaders use, fill it, and attach it to the stages that need it. The same table can serve many encoders and many frames. A binding is an address or a resource ID, not an object, and an offset is plain arithmetic on the address. The shader does not change: [[buffer(1)]] still means slot 1. This pairs naturally with the argument buffers of the Advanced MSL chapter: put a whole scene's textures in one buffer, and bind that single buffer."],["apple-platforms/metal-4.html#residency-sets","Metal 4","Residency sets","Classic command buffers retain every resource they use and make sure it is available to the GPU. Metal 4 does neither. You keep your resources alive yourself, and you tell Metal which ones the GPU needs with a residency set . Build the set when you load a level, not every frame. A resource missing from every set is the classic Metal 4 mistake, and the result is undefined: a black object, or garbage."],["apple-platforms/metal-4.html#barriers","Metal 4","Barriers","Metal 4 does not track which pass writes a resource and which reads it. Inside one command buffer, stages of different passes may overlap. Where a later stage reads what an earlier one wrote, for example a fragment function sampling a texture a compute pass just filled, you add a barrier on the consuming encoder: it names the stages that must finish and the stages that must wait. Rendering to a texture in one pass and sampling it in the next is the everyday case. Metal's validation layer reports missing barriers, so keep it on while porting."],["apple-platforms/metal-4.html#compiling-pipelines","Metal 4","Compiling pipelines","Pipeline states are created by a compiler object that you own, from descriptors that name functions without loading them first. The result is an ordinary MTLRenderPipelineState . Three additions make shader compilation easier to control: asynchronous variants that return a task you can prioritize; flexible pipeline states , where one compiled pipeline is specialized cheaply for different blend and attachment settings; and ahead-of-time compilation, where pipelines harvested from a play session are compiled at build time into an archive, so players never wait for a shader."],["apple-platforms/metal-4.html#what-else-arrived-with-it","Metal 4","What else arrived with it","One compute encoder for kernels, blits and acceleration structure builds. Machine learning in the frame : an MTL4MachineLearningCommandEncoder runs a Core ML network on the GPU timeline, and the Shader ML API evaluates small networks inside a fragment function, for neural materials or learned upscaling. Texture view pools and placement sparse resources for streaming large worlds. MetalFX frame interpolation and denoising , described in the previous chapter. Tessellation is not part of the Metal 4 encoder; use mesh shaders ."],["apple-platforms/metal-4.html#should-you-use-it","Metal 4","Should you use it?","For learning, and for an app with a few hundred draw calls, classic Metal is simpler and exactly as fast on the GPU. Its automatic retains, tracking and residency cost little at that scale and prevent a whole class of bugs. Metal 4 pays off when the CPU cost of encoding matters: thousands of draws, many threads, large streaming scenes, or an engine that already thinks in descriptor tables and barriers. Every concept in this book carries over unchanged, which is the reason to learn them first."],["apple-platforms/metal-4.html#exercises","Metal 4","Exercises","List what setFragmentTexture did for you that an argument table does not. Port the Hello Triangle renderer to Metal 4, starting from Apple's sample project."],["apple-platforms/visionos.html","visionOS","","On Apple Vision Pro most apps draw with RealityKit. When you want your own renderer, for a game engine or a visualization, you draw fully immersive content with Metal through a framework called Compositor Services . Your shaders and pipelines are the ones from this book. What is new is everything around the frame: there are two eyes, the camera is a person's head, and the deadline is strict."],["apple-platforms/visionos.html#what-is-different","visionOS","What is different","Two views. Each frame is rendered once per eye, with a different view and projection matrix for each. You do not own the camera. The view matrix comes from the headset's tracked position, through ARKit. You do not own the frame loop. There is no MTKView and no display link. Compositor Services gives you frames, and tells you when to start working on each. The system reprojects. Just before display, the system warps your image to match the latest head position. To do that it needs your depth buffer, so depth must be correct for every pixel you draw. Foveation. The headset renders the periphery at a lower resolution. You get this almost for free, but it changes how viewports and post-processing work. Latency is comfort. A late frame is not a small stutter here. Aim for the display's rate, 90 frames per second or more, every frame."],["apple-platforms/visionos.html#the-app-and-the-layer","visionOS","The app and the layer","The entry point is SwiftUI. An ImmersiveSpace whose content is a CompositorLayer hands you a LayerRenderer , the object that replaces the view. Start a dedicated render thread from there; never render on the main thread. The entry point of a visionOS app is always SwiftUI, so a small Swift file like the one below stays in a C++ project. Everything after that can be C++: Compositor Services has a C API ( cp_layer_renderer_t , cp_frame_t , cp_drawable_t ), which is what engines use. The Swift closure passes the layer renderer to a C function that starts your render thread. There is no GLFW on visionOS, and metal-cpp needs a bridge: the C API returns Objective-C texture objects, which you cast to MTL::Texture* in an Objective-C++ file, as in the glfw_bridge.mm of the macOS starter. The layout decides how the two eyes' images are stored. .dedicated gives one texture per eye and needs two render passes. .layered gives one array texture with a slice per eye, and lets you draw both eyes in one pass. Prefer layered."],["apple-platforms/visionos.html#the-frame-loop","visionOS","The frame loop","The loop follows the state of the layer: wait while it is paused, render while it is running, and leave when it is invalidated. A frame has two phases that you announce to the system. In the update phase you run everything that does not depend on where the head is: animation, physics, input. Then you sleep until the last sensible moment, ask for the head pose, and enter the submission phase, where you encode and commit. Waiting matters: the later you read the pose, the less the system has to correct. With the C API the same frame reads as follows. Each call matches a Swift method one to one. worldTracking is an ARKit WorldTrackingProvider that you start once with ARKitSession().run([worldTracking]) . Setting deviceAnchor on the drawable tells the system which pose you rendered for, so it can correct the difference."],["apple-platforms/visionos.html#two-eyes-two-matrices","visionOS","Two eyes, two matrices","Each drawable has a list of views, normally two. A view gives the eye's transform relative to the device, and the drawable computes the eye's projection. Both are different for each eye, and the projection is not symmetric, so do not build it from a field of view. In C++ read the same values with cp_drawable_get_view , cp_view_get_transform and cp_drawable_compute_projection , and the device transform with ar_anchor_get_origin_from_anchor_transform . The view matrix is simd_inverse(simd_mul(deviceTransform, eyeTransform)) . visionOS uses reverse-Z : the projection maps the near plane to depth 1 and the far plane to 0. Clear depth to 0 and compare with .greater . Units are meters, and the origin is on the floor where the space was opened."],["apple-platforms/visionos.html#both-eyes-in-one-pass","visionOS","Both eyes in one pass","With the layered layout the render pass targets an array texture. Vertex amplification runs the vertex function once per eye for each draw call and routes each result to its slice, so the scene is encoded once. The render pass is built the same way with metal-cpp, from cp_drawable_get_color_texture(drawable, 0) , cp_drawable_get_depth_texture(drawable, 0) , cp_drawable_get_view_count(drawable) and cp_drawable_get_rasterization_rate_map(drawable, 0) . Then call encoder->setViewports , encoder->setVertexAmplificationCount(count, mappings) and bind the array of per-eye uniforms. The pipeline descriptor needs maxVertexAmplificationCount = 2 , and the vertex function picks its matrices with the amplification index. Note what is stored: unlike on the Mac, the depth attachment uses .store , because the compositor reads it. This is the one place where the advice of the tile-based GPU chapter is overruled."],["apple-platforms/visionos.html#practical-notes","visionOS","Practical notes","Foveation and post-processing. With a rasterization rate map the texture is not uniform, so a full-screen pass that samples it must convert coordinates through the map. Start without post-processing. Passthrough. For mixed immersion, where your content appears in the person's room, clear to transparent black and write premultiplied alpha; the system composites the camera image behind you. Input. There are no keys or mouse. Pinches and gazes arrive as spatial events on the layer renderer, and hands through ARKit hand tracking. The Simulator does not support every layout or foveation. Check the capabilities instead of assuming, as the configuration above does. Budget. Two views at high resolution and 90 Hz leave a few milliseconds of GPU time. Forward rendering with MSAA, few passes and half-precision shaders is the usual choice."],["apple-platforms/visionos.html#exercises","visionOS","Exercises","Draw the ten-cube scene of Getting Started two meters in front of the starting position. Give each eye a different clear color and check that the layers are routed correctly. Switch to the dedicated layout and render with two passes. Compare the CPU time of encoding."],["resources/examples.html","Example Code","","There are two sets of projects: Swift examples for the main chapters, and a C++ starter for readers using metal-cpp. The repository includes a Swift package with runnable examples for the main chapters. Each one is a small macOS app in a single main.swift plus a Shaders.metal file. Download all examples (.zip)"],["resources/examples.html#running-an-example","Example Code","Running an example","You need macOS 13 or later and Xcode 15 or later. In the unzipped folder: You can also open Package.swift in Xcode, choose an example from the scheme menu and press Run. That gives you API validation, the GPU frame debugger and the performance HUD described in Debugging ."],["resources/examples.html#the-examples","Example Code","The examples","Target Chapters HelloView Hello View HelloTriangle Hello Triangle Shaders Shaders Textures Textures Transformations Transformations CoordinateSystems Coordinate Systems Camera Camera BasicLighting Colors , Basic Lighting , Materials LightingMaps Lighting Maps MultipleLights Light Casters , Multiple Lights ModelLoading Model I/O , Mesh , Model RenderTargets Render Targets Instancing Instancing ShadowMapping Shadow Mapping HDRBloom HDR , Bloom PBR PBR Lighting ComputeParticles Compute Shaders Chapters that are not in this list have their code inline only."],["resources/examples.html#how-they-differ-from-the-chapters","Example Code","How they differ from the chapters","No Xcode project. The chapters build a SwiftUI app. The examples use a small library, LearnMetalKit , that opens a plain AppKit window, so each example is one file. Shaders are compiled at launch from the Shaders.metal text with makeLibrary(source:) . That lets swift run work without Xcode's Metal build step. Shader errors are printed to the console. Textures are generated in code , so there is nothing else to download. Examples 01 to 03 write the frame loop by hand. Later ones inherit it from a BaseRenderer class to stay short."],["resources/examples.html#c-starter","Example Code","C++ starter","Download the C++ starter (.zip) The starter is the Hello Triangle chapter in C++: a GLFW window, a CAMetalLayer , and a renderer written with Apple's metal-cpp. It needs brew install glfw cmake and a copy of metal-cpp unzipped into the folder. Build it with CMake as described in Project Setup . It is the base that the C++ code in the chapters builds on."],["resources/examples.html#the-engine","Example Code","The engine","The largest example has a repository of its own. The LearnMetal Engine is a small game engine with a playable game, written in Swift and in C++, that puts the techniques of this site to work together. A Game Engine introduces it, and the chapters after it walk through the renderer, the game and the editor."],["resources/examples.html#about-the-live-demos-and-the-figures","Example Code","About the live demos and the figures","The live demos run in your browser with WebGL. The shader you edit is Metal Shading Language, translated to GLSL on the fly by a small script ( assets/live.js ). The translator covers the subset of the language the chapters use; it is a teaching aid and not a full Metal implementation, so a few things differ from a real Mac, such as stricter rules about mixing int and float . The remaining static images are illustrations. They are drawn by a small software renderer in the repository's tools/ folder, which implements the same math as each chapter's shaders with numpy. They are not screenshots of the Metal examples, so expect small differences in textures and framing when you run the code."]];
